{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"You are the fixer in Flint's ingest review gate. Apply the fixes from a round-1 review to staged library entries, check each against the source yourself, and write a fix log.\n\nRead first:\n- Review: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/review_supertraining_ch6b_opus.md` (GO AFTER FIXES: 11 FIX entries, 7 numbered fix groups, plus reciprocal-link suggestions and 5 missing ideas).\n- Standards: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/ATOMIZE_BRIEF.md` (Windows paths; Mac vault is `/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library`, baseline `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/docs/method-baseline.md`).\n- Log format to copy: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch5b_applied.md`.\n\nFiles: the 16 entries in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_scope_supertraining_ch6b.txt`, in `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/`. Other fixers are editing 6a, 6c and 6d files in the same folder at the same time: edit ONLY 6b files.\n\nSource: `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`, PDF idx (0-based) = printed + 11, covered PDF 347-362 (plus 305, 359, 375, 381). Text: `~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"import pymupdf; d=pymupdf.open('<pdf>'); print(d[N].get_text())\"`. Render to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/_renders/f6b_pN.png` and open with Read for anything you touch. For Fig 6.22 render at dpi=400 and read graph A yourself before changing anything; the review says it is a decelerating curve consistent with the text's parabola, while the writer said it was nearly straight. Decide from the image; values off a graph say \"about\".\n\nRules:\n- Verify each review claim on the page before applying. If the review is wrong, do not apply it; record why under \"Disagreements with the review\".\n- Block durations: sort the numbers by what each measures, as the review lays out: concentrated block length (3-4 weeks PDF 360; 4-8 weeks PDF 305; \"up to 2 months\" PDF 381), single-emphasis stage length (4-12 weeks PDF 305 and 381), LDTE duration (4-12 weeks PDF 375), adaptation-reserve span (18-22 weeks). Keep `contested` only on the first group if those three truly conflict.\n- Distributed-vs-concentrated: name the critiqued model as Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model, compare the right quantities, and do not rule it out for medium-level athletes unless the page does.\n- Remove any process note mentioning other batches (\"batch 6a\" etc.) from entry bodies.\n- Missing ideas: the management-theory items (decision-making, management versus leadership, Blake-Mouton grid, needs theories) were skipped on purpose because the authors do not apply them to training on the page. Add one only if you find the authors DO apply it to training on the page; otherwise record \"not added: not applied to training\" in the log. The endurance monitoring point on PDF 359: fold it into an existing 6b entry if it fits.\n- Related links: add the review's suggested links only within 6b files and only to targets that exist today.\n- No renames unless required; if you rename, use `mv` with fully literal absolute paths and report any 6a/6c/6d file that links the old slug (6c links several 6b files; do not edit them).\n- Do not touch `_book.md` or the vault.\n- Brief rules hold: label every voice, locators on every number and quote, no em dashes in titles or body (section headers keep `**What it is** —`), no review history in entries, never wrap a [[wikilink]] across lines, baseline quotes carry line number and tag.\n- Write the log to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6b_applied.md` (Fixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03).\n- Prove the fixes landed: grep each fixed file for the new wording, and grep 6b files for \"batch\" (expect none). Run `cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint && ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky` and paste the lines for 6b files and the total. Paste both in your final reply and at the end of the log.\n- No git. No vault edits.\n\nFinal reply: per-fix outcome (applied / disagreed), the Fig 6.22 reading, any renames, grep proof, lint output.\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \"tool not found\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\n    ToolSearch(query: \"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\")\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n</context_window_protection>"},"uuid":"53c073d5-30f8-4f1b-996d-8fe99d62f246","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:37:40.935Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
