{"parentUuid":"a9e4ed99-570e-4ff1-9ead-fe94e1969283","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnjgEMSD9MvWTaQTANN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FrBpAnp2noxSnCWvgnyD9b","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; sed -n 1,200p fixes_supertraining_ch5b_applied.md","description":"Read log format example"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13289,"cache_read_input_tokens":59412,"output_tokens":121,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":13289},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":121,"cache_read_input_tokens":59412,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":13289,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FrBpAnp2noxSnCWvgnyD9b":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; sed -n 1,200p fixes_supertraining_ch5b_applied.md","description":"Read log format example"}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CffnjfsmVEY4dLtzYAs5P","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"2780526c-536a-46af-8949-5a2efbc3a3d8","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:37:47.171Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"2780526c-536a-46af-8949-5a2efbc3a3d8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FrBpAnp2noxSnCWvgnyD9b","type":"tool_result","content":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 5.2.4-5.2.5 (Ch5b), 30 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch5b_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 13 FIX rows, 12 numbered fixes in the verdict list, 5 missing ideas, a Wilson 1990/1991 citation question).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 233 and 278-299 with PyMuPDF (`staging/_tmp/f5b_text.txt`). I rendered PDF 233, 279, 280, 281, 283, 284, 285, 286, 287, 288, 289, 292, 293, 296, 297 and 299 to `staging/_renders/f5b_p<N>.png`. I opened PDF 233 (\"considerably lower\"), 280 (safety passage), 281 (margin cut, EM-delay caption), 286 (item 1 \"1-15 minutes\", Fig 5.10), 296 (strength-endurance specificity, \"carry-over\" hyphenated across a line break), 284 (Fig 5.8, low vs high inertia), 285 (Fig 5.9, prescription list), 287 (Fig 5.11), 292-293 (the \"No resistance / methods should not\" page break) and 299 (novice experiments). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 30 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt`. I edited the 13 flagged entries plus `claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md` for the Wilson source note. No new entries, so the scope file is unchanged (30 lines). No renames. I did not edit `_book.md`, any 5a or 5c file, or the vault.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md | **Fix 1.** `confidence: contested` is now `supported`. The \"Internal conflict, recorded\" block is replaced by one sentence: the \"futile to rely predominantly\" passage is contradicted half a page later, but \"that conflict concerns explosive strength, not this entry's Nicol et al (1997) claim, and is recorded in [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]\". When NOT now says the rating is `supported` because the claim rests on one undescribed citation and nothing in the book contradicts it. The baseline line 122 citation now carries its tag, \"Explicit\". |\n| heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md | **Fix 2.** `contested` is now `supported`. The \"Internal tension\" block is replaced with a labelled Inference. It says these are different aims: firm surfaces for maximal rebounds (p. 273), sand for short-term skill gains (p. 278) and soft mats for the no-rebound landing experiment (p. 277). It quotes the text's \"as alluded to earlier ...\" (PDF 289) to show the sand finding sits beside the soft-surface warning on purpose. It reads \"suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" (PDF 288) as one instruction. The swing and pogo-stick \"no data\" flag in When NOT is kept. |\n| heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md | **Fix 3.** The conflict is now the skater example only (80-85%, Mikhailov & Panov 1975). The rowers' \"50-80% of 1RM\" is recorded as \"at or under the ceiling, so it is not part of the conflict\". When NOT now reads \"without the 80-85% skater example beside it\". The glycolytic inference is rewritten: line 116 (Explicit) \"worst case scenario\", then \"staff do program it in the off-season\" (line 117, Explicit, interview 2026-09-28, Q19). \"Inference: to-fatigue strength-endurance sets fit the off-season, not in-season weeks.\" **Missing idea 2** is added as a **Specificity** bullet quoting PDF 296 / p.285, with a pointer to the Ch3.2.2 taxonomy entry, which already holds the same point from Ch3. |\n| heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md | **Fix 4.** (1) The line 76 citation now reads \"Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)\", followed by the baseline's tag verbatim: \"[contested; the ratio tracks IMTP strength; one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]\". (2) The Example now quotes the p.278 wording, \"the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability of the muscles\", and adds \"The passage names no drill for the second case\". \"jumping from depth\" is removed. (3) When NOT now opens with the novice caveat, scoped to the **within-workout** experiment on PDF 299 (\"There was no statistically significant difference ... first two groups\"; the depth-jump group gained most). It also notes the separate across-stage experiment on the same page (weights first, then depth jumps, was more effective) and the authors' hedge that \"the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation\". Related now links [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]. |\n| claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md | **Fix 5.** The title now says \"93 Tons\" (the source's word), not \"Tonnes\". When NOT adds the novice caveat, scoped to the within-workout experiment (PDF 299 / p.288), with the authors' \"careful experimentation\" hedge. When to apply's sequencing Inference now ends \"(for novices, see When NOT to apply)\". Related now links the 5c novice entry. |\n| heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md | **Fix 6.** (1) The baseline sentence now reads: \"Eric's baseline: mRSI from daily CMJ testing 'was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker' (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 74, Explicit). Inference: a drop-jump RSI ... at stepped heights would be a separate test of the authors' ... rule; the baseline has no drop-jump test.\" (2) \"considerably lower\" is now the full quote \"using considerably lower jump heights with anyone who is unused to this type of training\" with the locator (Ch4.2.3, PDF p. 233 / printed p. 222, verified against the page image). |\n| heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md | **Fix 7.** (1) Depth-jump item 1 is moved out of Model 2 into its own paragraph, \"Depth-jump item 1, kept out of Model 2\". It quotes the \"complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11)\" sentence and the Fig 5.11 caption, and says the figure shows ground jumps with no box (verified against the image of PDF 287). It adds \"Inference: 'each exercise' most plausibly refers to that complex of ground jumps\". \"1-15 minutes\" is removed from the rest list in The conflict; it stays recorded as printed in the item 1 paragraph and in When NOT. (2) Voices are now labelled per item. Model 1's PDF 283, 293 and 294 statements are \"Siff's text\" (his term \"powermetrics\", PDF 279), and item 6 is from the authors' prescription list. In Model 2, item 4 is from the authors' prescription list. Items 2 and 3 are from the \"prolific experience\" recommendations, whose figures are credited to Verkhoshansky (1977); the list itself carries no citation. (3) Baseline: line 105 now carries its tag, \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\", and the baseline's \"Not claimed in the interview\". Line 186 is added, \"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)), with an Inference tying maximal depth-jump sessions to that cut-off. `contested` stays. |\n| framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md | **Fix 8.** The caption-versus-text paragraph now reads \"The caption words the EM delay differently ...\", quotes both, and ends \"Inference: both describe the electrical-to-mechanical interval.\" \"the book does not reconcile them\" is removed. In When NOT, \"Do not quote the EM delay definition without noting the caption and text differ\" is removed. |\n| framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md | **Fix 9 (classification).** Every \"the authors\" is now Siff. What it is opens \"The text (Siff's classification; it uses his term 'powermetrics', PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) replaces ...\". Why it works, When to apply, When NOT and Example say \"Siff's\"; the count of \"authors\" in the file is 0. **Missing idea 1** is added: the full F = ma paragraph from PDF 284 / p.273 (verified against the image), ending \"Explosive, low inertia training targets involuntary, neuromuscular and central nervous processes more strongly than high inertia training, which has a greater effect on static strength development and muscle hypertrophy\". The entry notes that no study is given and that Ch1's [[principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods]] holds the general equal-force point but not this split. That link is added to Related. |\n| heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md | **Fix 9 (momentum).** \"The authors propose\" is now \"The text (Siff, using his term 'powermetrics', PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) proposes\". \"The authors give no numeric scale bands\" is now \"Siff gives no ...\", and \"The authors' reasoning\" in Why it works is now \"Siff's reasoning\". Items 1 and 4 stay \"the authors'\" (\"The authors' prescription list adds\"). **Missing idea 3** is added: the Fig 5.9 caption as printed, the text's \"Practical application ... to different muscle groups\" and \"select the necessary complex of exercises personally\" sentences, and a description read from the image and marked approximate (drop push-ups between blocks; pulley and weight-stack pushes, pulls, kicks and rows; incline-board work; a seated kick against suspended apparatus). It ends with a labelled Inference that the falling weight stack supplies item 1's \"weight and the height of its free-fall\". |\n| claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md | **Fix 10.** The title is now \"The Lewis Formula P = 21.693 x B x √H Tracks Jump Progress (Inference: It Is Weight Times Mean Free-Fall Speed, Not Take-Off Power)\". The line 77 pointer now carries the baseline's full tag: \"Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)\". |\n| claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md | **Fix 11.** The quote is now \"adequ[ate] restoration regimes ... running a[nd] jumping\" (PDF pp. 280-281 / printed pp. 269-270). It notes that the right margin is cut in the scan on PDF 281 (verified against the image), so the bracketed letters are not printed. **Missing idea 5** is added as a bullet: \"it might be preferable to avoid use of the popular te[rm] plyometric *drills*, because drills tend to be associated with militarism and authoritarianism rather than wi[th] individual flow, sensitivity, flair and nuances of personal interpretation\" (PDF 281 / p.270, cut letters bracketed), labelled \"Voice: Siff\". |\n| heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics.md | **Fix 12.** The sentence is now quoted as printed: \"No resistance methods should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords during depth jumps ...\", followed by \"(sic: the sentence runs across the page break as 'No resistance / methods should not use', PDF pp. 292-293 / printed pp. 281-282. Inference: the intended sense is 'should not')\". The locator line now says both pages were verified against the page images. |\n| claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md | **Wilson.** A \"Source note\" is added after the internal-conflict paragraph with the reference: Wilson, G.J., Elliott, B.C. & Wood, G.A. (1991), *Med Sci Sports Exerc* 23(3):364-370, with the abstract's design and its \"half-life of 0.85 s\". It says the abstract does not give the 4 s figure. It adds an Inference that 0.85 s matches the Fig 5.6 curve, so this is probably the bench-press study and the caption's 1991 is right. It says the 0.37 s Wilson (1991) result was not checked. `contested` is kept: the book itself calls the decay results an \"apparent contradiction\". |\n\n## Missing ideas: placement\n\n| # | idea | placement |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Low vs high inertia training effect (PDF 284) | Added to the classification entry (flagged; same page as Fig 5.8). Ch1 `principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods` has F = ma and \"different training effect\" but not the low-inertia/neural vs high-inertia/hypertrophy split, so the 5b passage is new. |\n| 2 | Strength-endurance specificity is weaker than speed's, carry-over greater (PDF 296) | Added to the ceiling entry (flagged; the sentence sits just before the ceiling quote). The Ch3.2.2 taxonomy entry already quotes the Ch3 version, and the new bullet links it. |\n| 3 | Fig 5.9 apparatus catalogue (PDF 285) | Added to the momentum-intensity entry (flagged; Fig 5.9 introduces the same prescription list that entry quotes). |\n| 4 | Upper-body transfer (PDF 288) | **Not added: already captured.** See Disagreements. |\n| 5 | Avoid the term \"drills\" (PDF 281) | Added to the injury-risk entry (flagged; the same paragraph, Siff's voice). |\n\nNo new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt` is unchanged.\n\n## Wilson citation\n\nThe Zotero MCP (`mcp__zotero__search`) returned \"MCP tenant gate is not configured\", so the check went through the web. A web search found the paper. I then fetched the journal's own abstract page (journals.lww.com, redirecting to ovid.com, accession 00005768-199103000-00016). Confirmed: Wilson GJ, Elliott BC, Wood GA. The effect on performance of imposing a delay during a stretch-shorten cycle movement. Med Sci Sports Exerc 1991;23(3):364-370. Twelve experienced male weight lifters, bench press at 95% of maximum, decay fitted by a negative exponential with a half-life of 0.85 s. That matches the Fig 5.6 curve, so the caption's 1991 is probably right and the text's 1990 probably wrong. This is recorded in the entry as a source note, with the match labelled Inference. The abstract does not state the 4 s endpoint, and the entry says so.\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Missing idea 4 (upper-body transfer) is not missing.** `heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps.md` already quotes the PDF 288 / p.277 sentence (medicine ball catching and throwing, plyometric press-ups and bench presses). The source gives that point in one sentence, and the entry gives it one line. No edit was made. Only \"loaded swings or other specialised apparatus\" is left out, and the Fig 5.9 addition now covers apparatus.\n2. **Fix 4(3) and fix 5: the review's wording \"order made no significant difference in novices\" is too broad.** PDF 299 holds three relevant findings. (a) Across the preparatory period, order \"has no real significance for novices\". (b) In an across-stage experiment, weights first, then depth jumps, \"was more effective\". (c) In the within-workout experiment, which is the one that matches \"resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\", there was \"no statistically significant difference\". The authors then say they expect the effect in well-trained athletes, but that this needs \"careful experimentation\". The caveat I wrote names the within-workout experiment, mentions (b), and quotes the hedge, so it does not read as \"sequencing never matters\".\n3. **Fix 7(1): the reading is stated as Inference, not fact.** The source sentence is \"Good results are obtained with depth-jumps using a complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11). Each exercise is executed in sets of 10 ...\". Fig 5.11 shows ground jumps (confirmed on the image), but \"each exercise\" could grammatically take in the depth jumps. I moved item 1 out of Model 2 as asked, labelled the reading \"Inference\", and kept it out of the conflict count.\n4. **Fix 7(2): voice labels are per item, not per model.** Model 1 includes prescription item 6 and Model 2 includes prescription item 4. Both come from the PDF 285 list, which the review itself treats as \"the authors'\" (momentum row). So I labelled the Siff passages Siff, the prescription items the authors', and the PDF 286 list \"credited to 'prolific experience' ... whose figures are credited to Verkhoshansky (1977); the list itself carries no citation\". I did not call the list Verkhoshansky's.\n5. **Fix 10: the full baseline tag is used.** The review gives \"(Inferred, protocol)\". Line 77 prints \"**Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)**\", and the brief asks for the tag verbatim.\n6. **Beyond the review: baseline tags were added to every baseline citation in the edited files** (lines 74, 76, 105, 116, 117, 122, 186). Line 105 needed this most: it is Inferred and \"Not claimed in the interview\", and the entry called it \"the stated rule\" with no tag.\n7. **Optional items not applied.** The review's optional rows for GO entries (Dursenev wording, \"text layer\" to \"verified\" in three GO entries, the cyclic-endurance lead, the frequency entry's line 186 anchor, the non-impact locator) are outside the \"flagged files only\" rule and were not applied. Exception: the line 186 anchor was added to the volume-and-rest entry, which is flagged; the review offered it there as optional.\n\nEvery other review claim held up on the page: the PDF 281 margin cut (\"adequ\", \"running a\"), the \"No resistance / methods should not\" page break, the Fig 5.11 ground jumps, the absence of \"jumping from depth\" in the p.278 passage, the PDF 233 \"considerably lower\" locator, the baseline lines 74/76/77/116/117, Siff's \"powermetrics\" on PDF 279 and in the Fig 5.8 caption, and the rowers' 50-80% versus the skaters' 80-85% on PDF 297.\n\n## For the coordinator\n\n- Reciprocal links (5c, not edited by me): `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments.md` should link back to [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]] and [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]].\n- Confidence tallies change: `contested` 8 to 6, `supported` up by 2 (high-volume, surface-footwear).\n\n## Checks\n\nGrep proof, run from the book folder (one or more lines per fixed file):\n\n```\nclaim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md:9:confidence: supported\nclaim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md:20:that conflict concerns explosive strength, not this entry's Nicol et al (1997) claim\nheuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md:9:confidence: supported\nheuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md:22:Inference: these are different aims, not a contradiction\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md:24:sits at or under the ceiling, so it is not part of the conflict\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md:28:to-fatigue strength-endurance sets fit the off-season, not in-season weeks\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md:19:expressed to a lesser degree than the specificity of speed\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md:27:one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md:31:The passage names no drill for the second case\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md:29:The resistance-first rule for combined sessions is not supported for novices\nclaim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md:14:93 Tons\nclaim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md:24:The resistance-first rule is untested for novices\nheuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md:26:the baseline has no drop-jump test\nheuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md:28:(Ch4.2.3, PDF p. 233 / printed p. 222\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:29:Depth-jump item 1, kept out of Model 2\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:18:are Siff's text\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:35:line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\nframework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md:25:Inference: both describe the electrical-to-mechanical interval\nframework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md:16:The text (Siff's classification; it uses his term \"powermetrics\"\nframework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md:24:Explosive, low inertia training targets involuntary\nheuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md:16:The text (Siff, using his term \"powermetrics\"\nheuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md:20:The prescription list is introduced by Fig 5.9\nclaim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md:14:(Inference: It Is Weight Times Mean Free-Fall Speed, Not Take-Off Power)\nclaim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md:20:Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)\nclaim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md:21:adequ[ate] restoration regimes\nclaim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md:21:running a[nd] jumping\nclaim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md:22:militarism and authoritarianism\nheuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics.md:18:\"No resistance methods should not use heavy weights\nclaim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md:27:Source note: Wilson, G.J., Elliott, B.C. & Wood, G.A. (1991)\n```\n\nOld phrases, count of scoped files still holding each (all 0):\n\n```\nInternal conflict, recorded:** the \"futile: 0\nInternal tension, recorded: 0\nMale rowers obtain good results: 0\nthe department deliberately does not program: 0\nversus jumping from depth: 0\n93 Tonnes: 0\nreads reactive ability through mRSI: 0\ncredited to \"prolific experience\").**: 0\nnoting the caption and text differ: 0\nThe authors replace: 0\nThe authors propose: 0\nComputes a Falling-Body Power: 0\n\"adequate restoration: 0\nrunning or jumping: 0\n- Resistance methods \"should not: 0\n\"reconcile\" in framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md: 0\n```\n\nMechanical greps over all 30 scoped files (run under `/bin/bash` with `F=$(cat ../../../_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt | tr -d \"\\r\")`; zsh does not word-split `$F`):\n\n```\n== em dashes not in section headers:\n(end)\n== em dashes in titles:\n(end)\n== review-history words:\n(end)\n== wrapped wikilinks:\n(end)\n== header count per file:\n  30 6\n== CR endings:\n       0\n```\n\nNew link targets exist in the book folder: `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments`, `principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods`, `framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial`.\n\nLint, run from the Flint root:\n\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\n\nThe one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside my edits and outside scope.\n\n## Coordinator fixes after round 2\n\nRound 2 (`review_supertraining_ch5b_opus_round2.md`): GO AFTER FIXES, 28 GO / 2 FIX. Both applied:\n1. `claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md`: \"The resistance-first rule is untested for novices\" changed to \"not supported for novices\" (the PDF 299 experiment tested it on novices and found no significant difference).\n2. `heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md`: the line-105 baseline quote now carries its tag, \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\", plus \"Not claimed in the interview\" (checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` line 105).","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"2ec24bdf-acc1-4543-90f9-55c04a941d0b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:37:47.421Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Fixes applied: Supertraining 5.2.4-5.2.5 (Ch5b), 30 entries\n\nFixer: Claude Opus. Date: 2026-10-03. Review: `staging/review_supertraining_ch5b_opus.md` (round 1, GO AFTER FIXES: 13 FIX rows, 12 numbered fixes in the verdict list, 5 missing ideas, a Wilson 1990/1991 citation question).\nSource checked: `staging/books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf`. I extracted the text of PDF idx 233 and 278-299 with PyMuPDF (`staging/_tmp/f5b_text.txt`). I rendered PDF 233, 279, 280, 281, 283, 284, 285, 286, 287, 288, 289, 292, 293, 296, 297 and 299 to `staging/_renders/f5b_p<N>.png`. I opened PDF 233 (\"considerably lower\"), 280 (safety passage), 281 (margin cut, EM-delay caption), 286 (item 1 \"1-15 minutes\", Fig 5.10), 296 (strength-endurance specificity, \"carry-over\" hyphenated across a line break), 284 (Fig 5.8, low vs high inertia), 285 (Fig 5.9, prescription list), 287 (Fig 5.11), 292-293 (the \"No resistance / methods should not\" page break) and 299 (novice experiments). Page key: PDF idx = printed + 11.\nScope: the 30 files in `staging/_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt`. I edited the 13 flagged entries plus `claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md` for the Wilson source note. No new entries, so the scope file is unchanged (30 lines). No renames. I did not edit `_book.md`, any 5a or 5c file, or the vault.\n\n| file | what changed |\n|---|---|\n| claim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md | **Fix 1.** `confidence: contested` is now `supported`. The \"Internal conflict, recorded\" block is replaced by one sentence: the \"futile to rely predominantly\" passage is contradicted half a page later, but \"that conflict concerns explosive strength, not this entry's Nicol et al (1997) claim, and is recorded in [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]]\". When NOT now says the rating is `supported` because the claim rests on one undescribed citation and nothing in the book contradicts it. The baseline line 122 citation now carries its tag, \"Explicit\". |\n| heuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md | **Fix 2.** `contested` is now `supported`. The \"Internal tension\" block is replaced with a labelled Inference. It says these are different aims: firm surfaces for maximal rebounds (p. 273), sand for short-term skill gains (p. 278) and soft mats for the no-rebound landing experiment (p. 277). It quotes the text's \"as alluded to earlier ...\" (PDF 289) to show the sand finding sits beside the soft-surface warning on purpose. It reads \"suitably firm shock-absorbing surfaces\" (PDF 288) as one instruction. The swing and pogo-stick \"no data\" flag in When NOT is kept. |\n| heuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md | **Fix 3.** The conflict is now the skater example only (80-85%, Mikhailov & Panov 1975). The rowers' \"50-80% of 1RM\" is recorded as \"at or under the ceiling, so it is not part of the conflict\". When NOT now reads \"without the 80-85% skater example beside it\". The glycolytic inference is rewritten: line 116 (Explicit) \"worst case scenario\", then \"staff do program it in the off-season\" (line 117, Explicit, interview 2026-09-28, Q19). \"Inference: to-fatigue strength-endurance sets fit the off-season, not in-season weeks.\" **Missing idea 2** is added as a **Specificity** bullet quoting PDF 296 / p.285, with a pointer to the Ch3.2.2 taxonomy entry, which already holds the same point from Ch3. |\n| heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md | **Fix 4.** (1) The line 76 citation now reads \"Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q21)\", followed by the baseline's tag verbatim: \"[contested; the ratio tracks IMTP strength; one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]\". (2) The Example now quotes the p.278 wording, \"the resistance is small and the working effect depends upon the reactive ability of the muscles\", and adds \"The passage names no drill for the second case\". \"jumping from depth\" is removed. (3) When NOT now opens with the novice caveat, scoped to the **within-workout** experiment on PDF 299 (\"There was no statistically significant difference ... first two groups\"; the depth-jump group gained most). It also notes the separate across-stage experiment on the same page (weights first, then depth jumps, was more effective) and the authors' hedge that \"the validity of this hypothesis can be established conclusively only by careful experimentation\". Related now links [[case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments]]. |\n| claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md | **Fix 5.** The title now says \"93 Tons\" (the source's word), not \"Tonnes\". When NOT adds the novice caveat, scoped to the within-workout experiment (PDF 299 / p.288), with the authors' \"careful experimentation\" hedge. When to apply's sequencing Inference now ends \"(for novices, see When NOT to apply)\". Related now links the 5c novice entry. |\n| heuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md | **Fix 6.** (1) The baseline sentence now reads: \"Eric's baseline: mRSI from daily CMJ testing 'was deliberately chosen as the daily athlete-readiness marker' (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 74, Explicit). Inference: a drop-jump RSI ... at stepped heights would be a separate test of the authors' ... rule; the baseline has no drop-jump test.\" (2) \"considerably lower\" is now the full quote \"using considerably lower jump heights with anyone who is unused to this type of training\" with the locator (Ch4.2.3, PDF p. 233 / printed p. 222, verified against the page image). |\n| heuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md | **Fix 7.** (1) Depth-jump item 1 is moved out of Model 2 into its own paragraph, \"Depth-jump item 1, kept out of Model 2\". It quotes the \"complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11)\" sentence and the Fig 5.11 caption, and says the figure shows ground jumps with no box (verified against the image of PDF 287). It adds \"Inference: 'each exercise' most plausibly refers to that complex of ground jumps\". \"1-15 minutes\" is removed from the rest list in The conflict; it stays recorded as printed in the item 1 paragraph and in When NOT. (2) Voices are now labelled per item. Model 1's PDF 283, 293 and 294 statements are \"Siff's text\" (his term \"powermetrics\", PDF 279), and item 6 is from the authors' prescription list. In Model 2, item 4 is from the authors' prescription list. Items 2 and 3 are from the \"prolific experience\" recommendations, whose figures are credited to Verkhoshansky (1977); the list itself carries no citation. (3) Baseline: line 105 now carries its tag, \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\", and the baseline's \"Not claimed in the interview\". Line 186 is added, \"lifting and hard practice end 72 hours before a Saturday game\" (Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q15)), with an Inference tying maximal depth-jump sessions to that cut-off. `contested` stays. |\n| framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md | **Fix 8.** The caption-versus-text paragraph now reads \"The caption words the EM delay differently ...\", quotes both, and ends \"Inference: both describe the electrical-to-mechanical interval.\" \"the book does not reconcile them\" is removed. In When NOT, \"Do not quote the EM delay definition without noting the caption and text differ\" is removed. |\n| framework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md | **Fix 9 (classification).** Every \"the authors\" is now Siff. What it is opens \"The text (Siff's classification; it uses his term 'powermetrics', PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) replaces ...\". Why it works, When to apply, When NOT and Example say \"Siff's\"; the count of \"authors\" in the file is 0. **Missing idea 1** is added: the full F = ma paragraph from PDF 284 / p.273 (verified against the image), ending \"Explosive, low inertia training targets involuntary, neuromuscular and central nervous processes more strongly than high inertia training, which has a greater effect on static strength development and muscle hypertrophy\". The entry notes that no study is given and that Ch1's [[principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods]] holds the general equal-force point but not this split. That link is added to Related. |\n| heuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md | **Fix 9 (momentum).** \"The authors propose\" is now \"The text (Siff, using his term 'powermetrics', PDF p. 279 / printed p. 268) proposes\". \"The authors give no numeric scale bands\" is now \"Siff gives no ...\", and \"The authors' reasoning\" in Why it works is now \"Siff's reasoning\". Items 1 and 4 stay \"the authors'\" (\"The authors' prescription list adds\"). **Missing idea 3** is added: the Fig 5.9 caption as printed, the text's \"Practical application ... to different muscle groups\" and \"select the necessary complex of exercises personally\" sentences, and a description read from the image and marked approximate (drop push-ups between blocks; pulley and weight-stack pushes, pulls, kicks and rows; incline-board work; a seated kick against suspended apparatus). It ends with a labelled Inference that the falling weight stack supplies item 1's \"weight and the height of its free-fall\". |\n| claim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md | **Fix 10.** The title is now \"The Lewis Formula P = 21.693 x B x √H Tracks Jump Progress (Inference: It Is Weight Times Mean Free-Fall Speed, Not Take-Off Power)\". The line 77 pointer now carries the baseline's full tag: \"Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)\". |\n| claim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md | **Fix 11.** The quote is now \"adequ[ate] restoration regimes ... running a[nd] jumping\" (PDF pp. 280-281 / printed pp. 269-270). It notes that the right margin is cut in the scan on PDF 281 (verified against the image), so the bracketed letters are not printed. **Missing idea 5** is added as a bullet: \"it might be preferable to avoid use of the popular te[rm] plyometric *drills*, because drills tend to be associated with militarism and authoritarianism rather than wi[th] individual flow, sensitivity, flair and nuances of personal interpretation\" (PDF 281 / p.270, cut letters bracketed), labelled \"Voice: Siff\". |\n| heuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics.md | **Fix 12.** The sentence is now quoted as printed: \"No resistance methods should not use heavy weights or stiff elastic cords during depth jumps ...\", followed by \"(sic: the sentence runs across the page break as 'No resistance / methods should not use', PDF pp. 292-293 / printed pp. 281-282. Inference: the intended sense is 'should not')\". The locator line now says both pages were verified against the page images. |\n| claim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md | **Wilson.** A \"Source note\" is added after the internal-conflict paragraph with the reference: Wilson, G.J., Elliott, B.C. & Wood, G.A. (1991), *Med Sci Sports Exerc* 23(3):364-370, with the abstract's design and its \"half-life of 0.85 s\". It says the abstract does not give the 4 s figure. It adds an Inference that 0.85 s matches the Fig 5.6 curve, so this is probably the bench-press study and the caption's 1991 is right. It says the 0.37 s Wilson (1991) result was not checked. `contested` is kept: the book itself calls the decay results an \"apparent contradiction\". |\n\n## Missing ideas: placement\n\n| # | idea | placement |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Low vs high inertia training effect (PDF 284) | Added to the classification entry (flagged; same page as Fig 5.8). Ch1 `principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods` has F = ma and \"different training effect\" but not the low-inertia/neural vs high-inertia/hypertrophy split, so the 5b passage is new. |\n| 2 | Strength-endurance specificity is weaker than speed's, carry-over greater (PDF 296) | Added to the ceiling entry (flagged; the sentence sits just before the ceiling quote). The Ch3.2.2 taxonomy entry already quotes the Ch3 version, and the new bullet links it. |\n| 3 | Fig 5.9 apparatus catalogue (PDF 285) | Added to the momentum-intensity entry (flagged; Fig 5.9 introduces the same prescription list that entry quotes). |\n| 4 | Upper-body transfer (PDF 288) | **Not added: already captured.** See Disagreements. |\n| 5 | Avoid the term \"drills\" (PDF 281) | Added to the injury-risk entry (flagged; the same paragraph, Siff's voice). |\n\nNo new entry was written, so `_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt` is unchanged.\n\n## Wilson citation\n\nThe Zotero MCP (`mcp__zotero__search`) returned \"MCP tenant gate is not configured\", so the check went through the web. A web search found the paper. I then fetched the journal's own abstract page (journals.lww.com, redirecting to ovid.com, accession 00005768-199103000-00016). Confirmed: Wilson GJ, Elliott BC, Wood GA. The effect on performance of imposing a delay during a stretch-shorten cycle movement. Med Sci Sports Exerc 1991;23(3):364-370. Twelve experienced male weight lifters, bench press at 95% of maximum, decay fitted by a negative exponential with a half-life of 0.85 s. That matches the Fig 5.6 curve, so the caption's 1991 is probably right and the text's 1990 probably wrong. This is recorded in the entry as a source note, with the match labelled Inference. The abstract does not state the 4 s endpoint, and the entry says so.\n\n## Disagreements with the review\n\n1. **Missing idea 4 (upper-body transfer) is not missing.** `heuristic--preparatory-progression-before-depth-jumps.md` already quotes the PDF 288 / p.277 sentence (medicine ball catching and throwing, plyometric press-ups and bench presses). The source gives that point in one sentence, and the entry gives it one line. No edit was made. Only \"loaded swings or other specialised apparatus\" is left out, and the Fig 5.9 addition now covers apparatus.\n2. **Fix 4(3) and fix 5: the review's wording \"order made no significant difference in novices\" is too broad.** PDF 299 holds three relevant findings. (a) Across the preparatory period, order \"has no real significance for novices\". (b) In an across-stage experiment, weights first, then depth jumps, \"was more effective\". (c) In the within-workout experiment, which is the one that matches \"resistance movements precede the plyometric activities in individual sessions\", there was \"no statistically significant difference\". The authors then say they expect the effect in well-trained athletes, but that this needs \"careful experimentation\". The caveat I wrote names the within-workout experiment, mentions (b), and quotes the hedge, so it does not read as \"sequencing never matters\".\n3. **Fix 7(1): the reading is stated as Inference, not fact.** The source sentence is \"Good results are obtained with depth-jumps using a complex of vertical jumps (Fig 5.11). Each exercise is executed in sets of 10 ...\". Fig 5.11 shows ground jumps (confirmed on the image), but \"each exercise\" could grammatically take in the depth jumps. I moved item 1 out of Model 2 as asked, labelled the reading \"Inference\", and kept it out of the conflict count.\n4. **Fix 7(2): voice labels are per item, not per model.** Model 1 includes prescription item 6 and Model 2 includes prescription item 4. Both come from the PDF 285 list, which the review itself treats as \"the authors'\" (momentum row). So I labelled the Siff passages Siff, the prescription items the authors', and the PDF 286 list \"credited to 'prolific experience' ... whose figures are credited to Verkhoshansky (1977); the list itself carries no citation\". I did not call the list Verkhoshansky's.\n5. **Fix 10: the full baseline tag is used.** The review gives \"(Inferred, protocol)\". Line 77 prints \"**Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)**\", and the brief asks for the tag verbatim.\n6. **Beyond the review: baseline tags were added to every baseline citation in the edited files** (lines 74, 76, 105, 116, 117, 122, 186). Line 105 needed this most: it is Inferred and \"Not claimed in the interview\", and the entry called it \"the stated rule\" with no tag.\n7. **Optional items not applied.** The review's optional rows for GO entries (Dursenev wording, \"text layer\" to \"verified\" in three GO entries, the cyclic-endurance lead, the frequency entry's line 186 anchor, the non-impact locator) are outside the \"flagged files only\" rule and were not applied. Exception: the line 186 anchor was added to the volume-and-rest entry, which is flagged; the review offered it there as optional.\n\nEvery other review claim held up on the page: the PDF 281 margin cut (\"adequ\", \"running a\"), the \"No resistance / methods should not\" page break, the Fig 5.11 ground jumps, the absence of \"jumping from depth\" in the p.278 passage, the PDF 233 \"considerably lower\" locator, the baseline lines 74/76/77/116/117, Siff's \"powermetrics\" on PDF 279 and in the Fig 5.8 caption, and the rowers' 50-80% versus the skaters' 80-85% on PDF 297.\n\n## For the coordinator\n\n- Reciprocal links (5c, not edited by me): `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments.md` should link back to [[claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers]] and [[heuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size]].\n- Confidence tallies change: `contested` 8 to 6, `supported` up by 2 (high-volume, surface-footwear).\n\n## Checks\n\nGrep proof, run from the book folder (one or more lines per fixed file):\n\n```\nclaim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md:9:confidence: supported\nclaim--high-volume-plyometrics-do-not-build-rebound-fatigue-resistance.md:20:that conflict concerns explosive strength, not this entry's Nicol et al (1997) claim\nheuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md:9:confidence: supported\nheuristic--plyometric-surface-footwear-and-simple-apparatus.md:22:Inference: these are different aims, not a contradiction\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md:24:sits at or under the ceiling, so it is not part of the conflict\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md:28:to-fatigue strength-endurance sets fit the off-season, not in-season weeks\nheuristic--strength-endurance-load-tempo-and-the-75-80-percent-ceiling.md:19:expressed to a lesser degree than the specificity of speed\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md:27:one controlled trial (Pleša 2024, basketball, split at 0.90) supported only the strength arm; no football data]\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md:31:The passage names no drill for the second case\nheuristic--brief-maximal-tension-vs-plyometrics-by-resistance-size.md:29:The resistance-first rule for combined sessions is not supported for novices\nclaim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md:14:93 Tons\nclaim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md:24:The resistance-first rule is untested for novices\nheuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md:26:the baseline has no drop-jump test\nheuristic--depth-jump-height-set-by-transition-speed-and-heel-contact.md:28:(Ch4.2.3, PDF p. 233 / printed p. 222\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:29:Depth-jump item 1, kept out of Model 2\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:18:are Siff's text\nheuristic--maximal-plyometric-and-depth-jump-volume-and-rest.md:35:line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\nframework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md:25:Inference: both describe the electrical-to-mechanical interval\nframework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md:16:The text (Siff's classification; it uses his term \"powermetrics\"\nframework--plyometric-training-classification-impact-intensity-and-function.md:24:Explosive, low inertia training targets involuntary\nheuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md:16:The text (Siff, using his term \"powermetrics\"\nheuristic--rate-plyometric-intensity-by-contact-momentum-from-drop-height.md:20:The prescription list is introduced by Fig 5.9\nclaim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md:14:(Inference: It Is Weight Times Mean Free-Fall Speed, Not Take-Off Power)\nclaim--lewis-formula-estimates-jump-power-from-bodymass-and-height.md:20:Inferred (protocol; author uncertain)\nclaim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md:21:adequ[ate] restoration regimes\nclaim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md:21:running a[nd] jumping\nclaim--plyometric-injury-risk-comes-from-prescription-not-the-method.md:22:militarism and authoritarianism\nheuristic--resisted-and-water-plyometrics.md:18:\"No resistance methods should not use heavy weights\nclaim--prestretch-benefit-decay-time-differs-by-task.md:27:Source note: Wilson, G.J., Elliott, B.C. & Wood, G.A. (1991)\n```\n\nOld phrases, count of scoped files still holding each (all 0):\n\n```\nInternal conflict, recorded:** the \"futile: 0\nInternal tension, recorded: 0\nMale rowers obtain good results: 0\nthe department deliberately does not program: 0\nversus jumping from depth: 0\n93 Tonnes: 0\nreads reactive ability through mRSI: 0\ncredited to \"prolific experience\").**: 0\nnoting the caption and text differ: 0\nThe authors replace: 0\nThe authors propose: 0\nComputes a Falling-Body Power: 0\n\"adequate restoration: 0\nrunning or jumping: 0\n- Resistance methods \"should not: 0\n\"reconcile\" in framework--five-phases-of-a-plyometric-action.md: 0\n```\n\nMechanical greps over all 30 scoped files (run under `/bin/bash` with `F=$(cat ../../../_scope_supertraining_ch5b.txt | tr -d \"\\r\")`; zsh does not word-split `$F`):\n\n```\n== em dashes not in section headers:\n(end)\n== em dashes in titles:\n(end)\n== review-history words:\n(end)\n== wrapped wikilinks:\n(end)\n== header count per file:\n  30 6\n== CR endings:\n       0\n```\n\nNew link targets exist in the book folder: `case-study--novice-strength-and-speed-strength-sequencing-experiments`, `principle--equal-force-different-loading-methods`, `framework--strength-endurance-taxonomy-and-absolute-vs-partial`.\n\nLint, run from the Flint root:\n\n```\n$ ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 staging/_tmp/lint_mac.py staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 114, 115]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\n```\n\nThe one issue is the known `_book.md` false positive (title and table dashes), outside my edits and outside scope.\n\n## Coordinator fixes after round 2\n\nRound 2 (`review_supertraining_ch5b_opus_round2.md`): GO AFTER FIXES, 28 GO / 2 FIX. Both applied:\n1. `claim--depth-jump-training-beat-higher-volume-traditional-training-in-jumpers.md`: \"The resistance-first rule is untested for novices\" changed to \"not supported for novices\" (the PDF 299 experiment tested it on novices and found no significant difference).\n2. `heuristic--depth-jump-weekly-frequency-and-seasonal-placement.md`: the line-105 baseline quote now carries its tag, \"Inferred (transcript + shared slides)\", plus \"Not claimed in the interview\" (checked against `docs/method-baseline.md` line 105).","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2780526c-536a-46af-8949-5a2efbc3a3d8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraini