{"parentUuid":"c6c41f6b-e384-494c-bc9b-82e8f34f98ce","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffnk8hZ7bBuYWTxBBv4f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DNxzmEgb3qtAYyFUtbxWMe","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; ~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"\nimport pymupdf; d=pymupdf.open('books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf')\nfor n,dpi in [(358,150),(348,150),(351,150),(357,150),(359,150),(360,150),(361,150),(362,150),(381,150),(305,150)]:\n    d[n].get_pixmap(dpi=dpi).save(f'_renders/f6b_p{n}.png')\np=d[358]\nfor b in p.get_text('blocks'):\n    if 'Fig' in b[4] or '6.22' in b[4]: print(b[:4], b[4][:80].replace(chr(10),' '))\nprint(p.rect)\n\"","description":"Render pages and locate Fig 6.22"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1215,"cache_read_input_tokens":82064,"output_tokens":379,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":30},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1215},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":379,"cache_read_input_tokens":82064,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1215,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1215,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01DNxzmEgb3qtAYyFUtbxWMe":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging; ~/.local/bin/uv run --with pymupdf python3 -c \"\nimport pymupdf; d=pymupdf.open('books/supertraining-6th-expanded.pdf')\nfor n,dpi in [(358,150),(348,150),(351,150),(357,150),(359,150),(360,150),(361,150),(362,150),(381,150),(305,150)]:\n    d[n].get_pixmap(dpi=dpi).save(f'_renders/f6b_p{n}.png')\np=d[358]\nfor b in p.get_text('blocks'):\n    if 'Fig' in b[4] or '6.22' in b[4]: print(b[:4], b[4][:80].replace(chr(10),' '))\nprint(p.rect)\n\"","description":"Render pages and locate Fig 6.22"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffnk8PTwFQ6DnJmXA4Sj","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"8148db1e-a589-4cbb-8ea8-58ee969ef002","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:37:55.390Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8148db1e-a589-4cbb-8ea8-58ee969ef002","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DNxzmEgb3qtAYyFUtbxWMe","type":"tool_result","content":"(20.199996948242188, 624.472900390625, 588.5760498046875, 679.1728515625) The performance results of the adaptation process, expressed by the dynamics of \n(22.29999542236328, 849.59765625, 468.942626953125, 859.09765625) Figure 6.22 The dynamics o f adaptation in sports training over several years (S\nRect(0.0, 0.0, 655.5499877929688, 946.4500122070312)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e35cc93d-9764-49b1-a561-f145e3f928f1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:37:59.451Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"(20.199996948242188, 624.472900390625, 588.5760498046875, 679.1728515625) The performance results of the adaptation process, expressed by the dynamics of \n(22.29999542236328, 849.59765625, 468.942626953125, 859.09765625) Figure 6.22 The dynamics o f adaptation in sports training over several years (S\nRect(0.0, 0.0, 655.5499877929688, 946.4500122070312)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":7,"newStart":19,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **Verkhoshansky (1997), as reported by Siff.** Periodisation \"is not a suitable model for training for elite sportsmen\" and should be \"rejected or modified\" for modern crowded calendars; \"at best, separate forms of periodisation should be used by novice and young athletes.\" The mechanical division into periods and mesocycles rests on 1950s Soviet experience \"mainly on the example of three sports, namely swimming, weightlifting, track and field athletics.\" Training should rest \"much more on physiology.\" Plans should meet demands \"distributed throughout a competitive season\" rather than \"notorious peaks of sports form.\" Kenyan runners \"have never implemented periodisation\"; British runners declined after adopting \"Matveyev's block scheme\" widely after 1980 (Verkhoshansky's own claim). He cites Horwill (1992, 1995) condemning \"the slavish worship of the theory of periodisation as used by some runners in different countries\", and Tschiene (1991, 1992) concluding that \"periodisation has not changed from the moment of Matveyev's first formalisation of the topic in 1965\" and that \"many coach's doctrines have not advanced\" (PDF p. 344 / printed p. 333). He rejects \"laws\" of training and dismisses \"'undulating models and 'wave models'\" as \"word play\" (PDF p. 345 / printed p. 334). He also observes that \"the general paradigm of periodisation was based heavily upon the philosophies and methodologies of communism\": its cyclical nature \"was strongly influenced by the 'five year plans' and other cycles of productivity in the soviet system,\" and \"the precise calculation of training quantities reflected attempts to minimise the unscientific factors of subjectivity and emotiveness\" (PDF p. 345 / printed p. 334)."," ","-**Zheljazkov (1998), as reported by Siff.** Periodisation \"still managed to be utilised very effectively for many years in training numerous elite soviet and Eastern European athletes\"; it is \"erroneous\" to treat Matveyev's concept as \"the complete theory of sports training\"; Verkhoshansky's charges of \"primitive methodology\" and \"scholastic demagogy\" are \"a unprofessional personal attack\"; national rises and falls in running are natural, not caused by periodisation. Classical periodisation \"has not lost its fundamental importance\" but has \"serious qualitative limitations\" to be modified by research on \"exhaustion, adaptation and restoration,\" since regeneration is \"nonlinear and heterochronical\"; applied sport science \"does not at all replace the entire concept of periodisation, but enriches it\" (PDF p. 345-346 / printed p. 334-335).","+**Zheljazkov (1998), as reported by Siff.** Periodisation \"still managed to be utilised very effectively for many years in training numerous elite soviet and Eastern European athletes\"; it is \"erroneous\" to treat Matveyev's concept as \"the complete theory of sports training\"; Verkhoshansky's charges of \"primitive methodology\" and \"scholastic demagogy\" are \"a unprofessional personal attack\"; national rises and falls in running are natural, not caused by periodisation. On the communism point he partly agrees: \"models of society such as communism indeed have a profound impact on the nature of sports training, but ... this happens in all countries and should not necessarily be taken to mean that this interaction between sport and society is always negative\" (PDF p. 345 / printed p. 334). Classical periodisation \"has not lost its fundamental importance\" but has \"serious qualitative limitations\" to be modified by research on \"exhaustion, adaptation and restoration,\" since regeneration is \"nonlinear and heterochronical\"; applied sport science \"does not at all replace the entire concept of periodisation, but enriches it\" (PDF p. 345-346 / printed p. 334-335)."," "," **Why it works** — Each side argues from different evidence: Verkhoshansky from the 1950s origins of the model, its three-sport base and national running records; Zheljazkov from decades of Soviet and Eastern European elite success. Neither gives controlled data on these pages, and both national-results arguments are correlational (Inference)."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--periodisation-controversy-verkhoshansky-vs-zheljazkov.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8148db1e-a589-4cbb-8ea8-58ee969ef002","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
