{"parentUuid":"abf728f8-ceab-4a1a-949b-58600a349833","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnniNfpKZ1KHmthRWG9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FysxBzRLgJER7X3jqoaMVs","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1928,"cache_read_input_tokens":91507,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1928,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FysxBzRLgJER7X3jqoaMVs":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffnngczZn88hBat1KTPs","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"fa6ff6f8-ca71-4974-8ea4-289093582d30","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:38:29.380Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"fa6ff6f8-ca71-4974-8ea4-289093582d30","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FysxBzRLgJER7X3jqoaMVs","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttype: library-entry\n3\tkind: framework\n4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n6\tchapter: \"6.4\"\n7\ttopic: training as a management system\n8\tcategory: [programming, monitoring, training-load, theory]\n9\tconfidence: untested\n10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Training as a Managed System: Training Load, Fitness State and External Interactions, Controlled at Two Levels\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — The authors treat programming \"as a problem in management\" and name three components of the managed system (PDF p. 347 / printed p. 336, checked against the page image):\n17\t1. \"The complex of the athlete's external influences encountered in competition.\" \"The greater the athlete's motor potential, the more effectively the complex of external interactions can be organised.\" Sports results \"are the observable product of the organisation of the athlete's external interactions.\"\n18\t2. \"the athlete's fitness state,\" defined as \"the athlete's structural-functional qualities which determine the athlete's potential to produce sporting prowess (Zatsiorsky, 1979).\"\n19\t3. \"specialised motor activities, i.e. the training load.\"\n20\t\n21\tThe causal chain runs one way: \"The dosage of the training load is prescribed to promote changes in the athlete's fitness state which, in turn, entails changes in the complex of external interactions of the athlete and a corresponding improvement in sporting results.\" When planned results are reached, \"the sequence of the preceding conditions is repeated at higher levels\" (PDF p. 347 / printed p. 336).\n22\t\n23\tFig 6.12, \"A logical scheme for organising sports training (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337, checked against the page image), draws the loop: Fitness needs for given sport, Training objectives, Programming of training, Characteristics of training model, then Training load, Training potential of loading, Athlete's fitness state (shaded box A), Training effect of loading, Athlete's external interactions (shaded box B), Change in performance, and Assessment of effect (fed by \"Sports Competition\" and \"Laboratory Tests\"), which returns to Fitness needs.\n24\t\n25\tKey terms (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337):\n26\t- **Training potential of the loading:** \"its potential for promoting adaptation of the body and corresponding changes in its fitness state,\" and \"a relative concept which should be examined and assessed in accordance with the athlete's current fitness state.\"\n27\t- **Assessment of the training effect:** \"measuring the performance change that the loading produces in each case under competitive and laboratory conditions.\"\n28\t- **Two levels of control:** the fitness state (\"control of the effect of the loading on fitness\") and external interactions (\"control of the change in their characteristics as a result of changes in fitness\").\n29\t- **Two subsystems:** A = {Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State}; B = {Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State, Athlete's External Interactions}. \"the lower the measures for the first one (A), the weaker the link in the entire management system.\"\n30\t\n31\t**Why it works** — The authors' reason is structural: fitness state is the only route from load to result, so both the load-to-fitness link and the fitness-to-performance link must be measured. The basis for the training effect is \"the accumulation of adaptive traces within the body produced by the entire complex of training influences\" (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337). They give no data for the scheme; it is a conceptual model, hence `untested`.\n32\t\n33\t**When to apply** — Inference: when auditing a monitoring set-up, for each test, ask which box of Fig 6.12 it measures. A jump test reads the fitness state; game or practice output reads external interactions; neither alone closes the loop. Eric's baseline: \"Preparedness (the athlete's underlying physical capability, built over a long period) is explicitly distinguished from readiness (day-to-day fluctuation)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 64, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' \"fitness state\" corresponds to that preparedness, and the two-level control is the same split between what the body can do and what it shows in competition. Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place\" (line 32, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)). Inference: Fig 6.12 gives each test a place to answer to: a test that sits in no box of the loop is the kind the rule removes.\n34\t\n35\t**When NOT to apply** — The scheme names what to measure but gives no measures, thresholds or numbers. \"One can be certain that the lower the measures for the first one (A), the weaker the link\" is stated without a study. The authors also widen \"management\" in the same passage to \"motivation, the athlete's personality and social factors\" (PDF p. 347 / printed p. 336), which the figure does not show. Inference: the model is linear and single-athlete; it does not cover fatigue, injury or sport-skill learning as separate inputs.\n36\t\n37\t**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: a sprinter's training load (sprints, jumps, lifts) is assessed at level A by laboratory tests (jump height, isometric force) and at level B by race times; if the tests rise but race times do not, the weak link is between fitness state and external interactions, not the load.\n38\t\n39\t**Related** — [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]], [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]], [[heuristic--build-training-theory-from-interacting-complexes-and-coach-scientist-cooperation]] (6.5, the research programme behind Fig 6.13), [[old-bull-performance-course--old-bull/framework--monitoring-versus-testing]] (Weber separates short-horizon monitoring from phase-length testing; Inference: a practical split for the two control levels)\n40\t"}]},"uuid":"b7564e42-8c48-4412-8904-afe74b71f892","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:38:29.585Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.4\"\ntopic: training as a management system\ncategory: [programming, monitoring, training-load, theory]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Training as a Managed System: Training Load, Fitness State and External Interactions, Controlled at Two Levels\n\n**What it is** — The authors treat programming \"as a problem in management\" and name three components of the managed system (PDF p. 347 / printed p. 336, checked against the page image):\n1. \"The complex of the athlete's external influences encountered in competition.\" \"The greater the athlete's motor potential, the more effectively the complex of external interactions can be organised.\" Sports results \"are the observable product of the organisation of the athlete's external interactions.\"\n2. \"the athlete's fitness state,\" defined as \"the athlete's structural-functional qualities which determine the athlete's potential to produce sporting prowess (Zatsiorsky, 1979).\"\n3. \"specialised motor activities, i.e. the training load.\"\n\nThe causal chain runs one way: \"The dosage of the training load is prescribed to promote changes in the athlete's fitness state which, in turn, entails changes in the complex of external interactions of the athlete and a corresponding improvement in sporting results.\" When planned results are reached, \"the sequence of the preceding conditions is repeated at higher levels\" (PDF p. 347 / printed p. 336).\n\nFig 6.12, \"A logical scheme for organising sports training (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337, checked against the page image), draws the loop: Fitness needs for given sport, Training objectives, Programming of training, Characteristics of training model, then Training load, Training potential of loading, Athlete's fitness state (shaded box A), Training effect of loading, Athlete's external interactions (shaded box B), Change in performance, and Assessment of effect (fed by \"Sports Competition\" and \"Laboratory Tests\"), which returns to Fitness needs.\n\nKey terms (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337):\n- **Training potential of the loading:** \"its potential for promoting adaptation of the body and corresponding changes in its fitness state,\" and \"a relative concept which should be examined and assessed in accordance with the athlete's current fitness state.\"\n- **Assessment of the training effect:** \"measuring the performance change that the loading produces in each case under competitive and laboratory conditions.\"\n- **Two levels of control:** the fitness state (\"control of the effect of the loading on fitness\") and external interactions (\"control of the change in their characteristics as a result of changes in fitness\").\n- **Two subsystems:** A = {Training Load, Training Potential of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State}; B = {Training Effect of Loading, Athlete's Fitness State, Athlete's External Interactions}. \"the lower the measures for the first one (A), the weaker the link in the entire management system.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is structural: fitness state is the only route from load to result, so both the load-to-fitness link and the fitness-to-performance link must be measured. The basis for the training effect is \"the accumulation of adaptive traces within the body produced by the entire complex of training influences\" (PDF p. 348 / printed p. 337). They give no data for the scheme; it is a conceptual model, hence `untested`.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when auditing a monitoring set-up, for each test, ask which box of Fig 6.12 it measures. A jump test reads the fitness state; game or practice output reads external interactions; neither alone closes the loop. Eric's baseline: \"Preparedness (the athlete's underlying physical capability, built over a long period) is explicitly distinguished from readiness (day-to-day fluctuation)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 64, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the authors' \"fitness state\" corresponds to that preparedness, and the two-level control is the same split between what the body can do and what it shows in competition. Eric's baseline: \"If it doesn't answer a question anyone has, or a direct need, then it doesn't have a place\" (line 32, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q3)). Inference: Fig 6.12 gives each test a place to answer to: a test that sits in no box of the loop is the kind the rule removes.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The scheme names what to measure but gives no measures, thresholds or numbers. \"One can be certain that the lower the measures for the first one (A), the weaker the link\" is stated without a study. The authors also widen \"management\" in the same passage to \"motivation, the athlete's personality and social factors\" (PDF p. 347 / printed p. 336), which the figure does not show. Inference: the model is linear and single-athlete; it does not cover fatigue, injury or sport-skill learning as separate inputs.\n\n**Example** — The authors give no worked case. Inference: a sprinter's training load (sprints, jumps, lifts) is assessed at level A by laboratory tests (jump height, isometric force) and at level B by race times; if the tests rise but race times do not, the weak link is between fitness state and external interactions, not the load.\n\n**Related** — [[framework--acute-delayed-particular-cumulative-training-effect-taxonomy]], [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]], [[heuristic--build-training-theory-from-interacting-complexes-and-coach-scientist-cooperation]] (6.5, the research programme behind Fig 6.13), [[old-bull-performance-course--old-bull/framework--monitoring-versus-testing]] (Weber separates short-horizon monitoring from phase-length testing; Inference: a practical split for the two control levels)\n","numLines":40,"startLine":1,"totalLines":40}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fa6ff6f8-ca71-4974-8ea4-289093582d30","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
