{"parentUuid":"575f798f-4e0f-4d0e-8949-8b034b421c1c","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnohzdAMqNL4JDi6yTb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Confirmed: B is a hump on the broad curve's right slope, and C is the narrow spike. Applying."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7201,"cache_read_input_tokens":99511,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7201,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffnofMsfAJUqK24p14wa","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"1feb1ac6-bb4c-4795-95ab-3ea9004b96ec","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:38:43.642Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1feb1ac6-bb4c-4795-95ab-3ea9004b96ec","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnohzdAMqNL4JDi6yTb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013hxVLmFdqQ7hdfKzt4Wj5a","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity.md","old_string":"one broad inverted-U over \"low / optimal / high\" arousal, a narrow spike A at low arousal, and spikes B and C near high arousal.","new_string":"one broad inverted-U over \"low / optimal / high\" arousal; a narrow spike A at low arousal; a second hump B on the right-hand slope of the broad curve (\"second wind\"); and a narrow spike C at high arousal."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7201,"cache_read_input_tokens":99511,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7201,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_013hxVLmFdqQ7hdfKzt4Wj5a":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity.md","old_string":"one broad inverted-U over \"low / optimal / high\" arousal, a narrow spike A at low arousal, and spikes B and C near high arousal.","new_string":"one broad inverted-U over \"low / optimal / high\" arousal; a narrow spike A at low arousal; a second hump B on the right-hand slope of the broad curve (\"second wind\"); and a narrow spike C at high arousal."}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffnofMsfAJUqK24p14wa","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5b8f6602-e020-4004-9472-bd94c3658990","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:38:45.513Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"5b8f6602-e020-4004-9472-bd94c3658990","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013hxVLmFdqQ7hdfKzt4Wj5a","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"00cab9ab-9c87-432c-a1bc-aa7948e2d705","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:38:45.671Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity.md","oldString":"one broad inverted-U over \"low / optimal / high\" arousal, a narrow spike A at low arousal, and spikes B and C near high arousal.","newString":"one broad inverted-U over \"low / optimal / high\" arousal; a narrow spike A at low arousal; a second hump B on the right-hand slope of the broad curve (\"second wind\"); and a narrow spike C at high arousal.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.6.1\"\ntopic: arousal, stress and restoration\ncategory: [psychology, restoration, overtraining, individualisation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Arousal Optimum Is Individual: Fit Restoration to Personality and Task Complexity, and Do Not Read the Yerkes-Dodson Curve Literally\n\n**What it is** — The model is Yerkes and Dodson's inverted-U of performance against arousal; the authors borrow it and apply it to training stress and restoration. The 6.6.1 section cites \"(Siff, 1990)\" as the source of its selection of models; this entry still writes \"the authors\" because no passage names Verkhoshansky in the third person. The authors' points (PDF p. 351 / printed p. 340, checked against the page image, unless noted):\n1. **The optimum is individual.** \"There appears to be an optimum level of arousal for any person performing a specific task at a specific time.\" The curve's shape \"depends on individual characteristics such as level of introversion/extroversion, level of exhaustion, standard of health, and task complexity,\" and \"all motivational techniques are not universally applicable to everyone.\"\n2. **Personality and restoration.** \"the introvert appears to have a lower optimal arousal zone, unlike the extrovert who requires greater stimulation and can cope better with high levels of stress.\" This \"has important implications for the prescription of restorative procedures, with the introvert probably requiring early restoration and the extrovert requiring careful monitoring to prevent overtraining caused by a desire for greater stimulation.\"\n3. **Task complexity.** \"Complex tasks require low levels of arousal to reach optimal performance over very limited periods (e.g. in golf, gymnastics and pole vaulting), whereas simple sports tasks may be executed over a longer period at higher levels of arousal.\" Applied to training: \"Excessive exposure of an athlete to complex training tasks can lead rapidly to 'overstress', while simple tasks may be insufficiently challenging to produce continued supercompensation. A balance between complexity, simplicity, intensity and volume of load is necessary to ensure growth and to minimise overtraining\" (PDF pp. 351-352 / printed pp. 340-341).\n4. **Repeated stress changes the response.** \"regular exposure to a stressor may produce either habituation or oversensitisation, so that the same level of stress may not produce the same response at a later stage. In either event, timely use of restoration measures is of central importance.\"\n5. **Limits of the curve.** It \"implies that there is just one discrete peak\"; the \"'second wind' phenomenon (curve B) and the effect of task complexity on curve shape are two of several reasons why the original curve must be applied to restoration and stress management with circumspection.\" Also, \"it is well known that both sensory underload and overload may lead to phases of enhanced performance (curves A, C),\" and meditation and similar states \"may enhance performance in certain situations (curve A).\" Hence \"both optimisation and transcendental stress management strategies are necessary.\"\n\nFig 6.15 (checked against the page image) is a schematic with no units: one broad inverted-U over \"low / optimal / high\" arousal, a narrow spike A at low arousal, and spikes B and C near high arousal.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism and no data for the introvert/extrovert or task-complexity claims; point 2 is hedged (\"appears\", \"probably\"), and the \"it is well known\" sentence in point 5 carries no citation.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when an athlete's response to the same load differs from a teammate's: consider temperament and task complexity before changing the load. Technical sessions (high complexity) may need calm conditions and short exposures; simple conditioning can tolerate higher arousal for longer. Eric's baseline: \"Recovery is managed through training-load design alone; nothing objective is tracked, sleep included\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 153, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q23)). Inference: the authors' point 2 suggests that load design alone may under-serve the athletes who need restoration early, and point 4 says the same load can stop meaning the same thing over a season.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Inference: do not sort athletes into introvert and extrovert recovery plans on this basis, since the authors give no study and their own wording is tentative. Inference: a measured fatigue marker is the better trigger for restoration. The figure's curves A, B and C are illustrations, not data. The task-complexity claim is about arousal during performance; the jump to \"complex training tasks ... 'overstress'\" is the authors' extension, also without data.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: golf, gymnastics and pole vaulting as complex tasks needing low arousal over very limited periods (PDF p. 351 / printed p. 340). Inference: a pole vaulter's technical session is kept short and calm, while the same athlete's sprint conditioning tolerates a louder, longer session.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent]], [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]], [[claim--voluntary-involuntary-strength-reserve-magnitudes]], [[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--verkhoshansky-activation-levels-and-threshold-of-adaptability]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":20,"oldLines":7,"newStart":20,"newLines":7,"lines":[" 4. **Repeated stress changes the response.** \"regular exposure to a stressor may produce either habituation or oversensitisation, so that the same level of stress may not produce the same response at a later stage. In either event, timely use of restoration measures is of central importance.\""," 5. **Limits of the curve.** It \"implies that there is just one discrete peak\"; the \"'second wind' phenomenon (curve B) and the effect of task complexity on curve shape are two of several reasons why the original curve must be applied to restoration and stress management with circumspection.\" Also, \"it is well known that both sensory underload and overload may lead to phases of enhanced performance (curves A, C),\" and meditation and similar states \"may enhance performance in certain situations (curve A).\" Hence \"both optimisation and transcendental stress management strategies are necessary.\""," ","-Fig 6.15 (checked against the page image) is a schematic with no units: one broad inverted-U over \"low / optimal / high\" arousal, a narrow spike A at low arousal, and spikes B and C near high arousal.","+Fig 6.15 (checked against the page image) is a schematic with no units: one broad inverted-U over \"low / optimal / high\" arousal; a narrow spike A at low arousal; a second hump B on the right-hand slope of the broad curve (\"second wind\"); and a narrow spike C at high arousal."," "," **Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism and no data for the introvert/extrovert or task-complexity claims; point 2 is hedged (\"appears\", \"probably\"), and the \"it is well known\" sentence in point 5 carries no citation."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5b8f6602-e020-4004-9472-bd94c3658990","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
