{"parentUuid":"e5c33db7-fac0-4ef9-8735-f384a0d1e0d7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018EUyzzcUfXLQALbDftPrDw","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"ffa99f3c-aa37-45e5-bef4-e7e115d7f045","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:38:47.316Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--arousal-optimum-is-individual-so-fit-restoration-to-personality-and-task-complexity.md","oldString":"prevent overtraining caused by a desire for greater stimulation.\"\n3.","newString":"prevent overtraining caused by a desire for greater stimulation.\" The book gives no source for the personality difference; it is presented as a general claim (\"appears\"), not as the authors' finding.\n3.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.6.1\"\ntopic: arousal, stress and restoration\ncategory: [psychology, restoration, overtraining, individualisation]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# The Arousal Optimum Is Individual: Fit Restoration to Personality and Task Complexity, and Do Not Read the Yerkes-Dodson Curve Literally\n\n**What it is** — The model is Yerkes and Dodson's inverted-U of performance against arousal; the authors borrow it and apply it to training stress and restoration. The 6.6.1 section cites \"(Siff, 1990)\" as the source of its selection of models; this entry still writes \"the authors\" because no passage names Verkhoshansky in the third person. The authors' points (PDF p. 351 / printed p. 340, checked against the page image, unless noted):\n1. **The optimum is individual.** \"There appears to be an optimum level of arousal for any person performing a specific task at a specific time.\" The curve's shape \"depends on individual characteristics such as level of introversion/extroversion, level of exhaustion, standard of health, and task complexity,\" and \"all motivational techniques are not universally applicable to everyone.\"\n2. **Personality and restoration.** \"the introvert appears to have a lower optimal arousal zone, unlike the extrovert who requires greater stimulation and can cope better with high levels of stress.\" This \"has important implications for the prescription of restorative procedures, with the introvert probably requiring early restoration and the extrovert requiring careful monitoring to prevent overtraining caused by a desire for greater stimulation.\"\n3. **Task complexity.** \"Complex tasks require low levels of arousal to reach optimal performance over very limited periods (e.g. in golf, gymnastics and pole vaulting), whereas simple sports tasks may be executed over a longer period at higher levels of arousal.\" Applied to training: \"Excessive exposure of an athlete to complex training tasks can lead rapidly to 'overstress', while simple tasks may be insufficiently challenging to produce continued supercompensation. A balance between complexity, simplicity, intensity and volume of load is necessary to ensure growth and to minimise overtraining\" (PDF pp. 351-352 / printed pp. 340-341).\n4. **Repeated stress changes the response.** \"regular exposure to a stressor may produce either habituation or oversensitisation, so that the same level of stress may not produce the same response at a later stage. In either event, timely use of restoration measures is of central importance.\"\n5. **Limits of the curve.** It \"implies that there is just one discrete peak\"; the \"'second wind' phenomenon (curve B) and the effect of task complexity on curve shape are two of several reasons why the original curve must be applied to restoration and stress management with circumspection.\" Also, \"it is well known that both sensory underload and overload may lead to phases of enhanced performance (curves A, C),\" and meditation and similar states \"may enhance performance in certain situations (curve A).\" Hence \"both optimisation and transcendental stress management strategies are necessary.\"\n\nFig 6.15 (checked against the page image) is a schematic with no units: one broad inverted-U over \"low / optimal / high\" arousal; a narrow spike A at low arousal; a second hump B on the right-hand slope of the broad curve (\"second wind\"); and a narrow spike C at high arousal.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism and no data for the introvert/extrovert or task-complexity claims; point 2 is hedged (\"appears\", \"probably\"), and the \"it is well known\" sentence in point 5 carries no citation.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when an athlete's response to the same load differs from a teammate's: consider temperament and task complexity before changing the load. Technical sessions (high complexity) may need calm conditions and short exposures; simple conditioning can tolerate higher arousal for longer. Eric's baseline: \"Recovery is managed through training-load design alone; nothing objective is tracked, sleep included\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 153, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q23)). Inference: the authors' point 2 suggests that load design alone may under-serve the athletes who need restoration early, and point 4 says the same load can stop meaning the same thing over a season.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Inference: do not sort athletes into introvert and extrovert recovery plans on this basis, since the authors give no study and their own wording is tentative. Inference: a measured fatigue marker is the better trigger for restoration. The figure's curves A, B and C are illustrations, not data. The task-complexity claim is about arousal during performance; the jump to \"complex training tasks ... 'overstress'\" is the authors' extension, also without data.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: golf, gymnastics and pole vaulting as complex tasks needing low arousal over very limited periods (PDF p. 351 / printed p. 340). Inference: a pole vaulter's technical session is kept short and calm, while the same athlete's sprint conditioning tolerates a louder, longer session.\n\n**Related** — [[claim--plyometric-skill-is-cognitive-and-state-dependent]], [[framework--types-of-maximal-strength]], [[claim--voluntary-involuntary-strength-reserve-magnitudes]], [[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--verkhoshansky-activation-levels-and-threshold-of-adaptability]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":15,"oldLines":7,"newStart":15,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **What it is** — The model is Yerkes and Dodson's inverted-U of performance against arousal; the authors borrow it and apply it to training stress and restoration. The 6.6.1 section cites \"(Siff, 1990)\" as the source of its selection of models; this entry still writes \"the authors\" because no passage names Verkhoshansky in the third person. The authors' points (PDF p. 351 / printed p. 340, checked against the page image, unless noted):"," 1. **The optimum is individual.** \"There appears to be an optimum level of arousal for any person performing a specific task at a specific time.\" The curve's shape \"depends on individual characteristics such as level of introversion/extroversion, level of exhaustion, standard of health, and task complexity,\" and \"all motivational techniques are not universally applicable to everyone.\"","-2. **Personality and restoration.** \"the introvert appears to have a lower optimal arousal zone, unlike the extrovert who requires greater stimulation and can cope better with high levels of stress.\" This \"has important implications for the prescription of restorative procedures, with the introvert probably requiring early restoration and the extrovert requiring careful monitoring to prevent overtraining caused by a desire for greater stimulation.\"","+2. **Personality and restoration.** \"the introvert appears to have a lower optimal arousal zone, unlike the extrovert who requires greater stimulation and can cope better with high levels of stress.\" This \"has important implications for the prescription of restorative procedures, with the introvert probably requiring early restoration and the extrovert requiring careful monitoring to prevent overtraining caused by a desire for greater stimulation.\" The book gives no source for the personality difference; it is presented as a general claim (\"appears\"), not as the authors' finding."," 3. **Task complexity.** \"Complex tasks require low levels of arousal to reach optimal performance over very limited periods (e.g. in golf, gymnastics and pole vaulting), whereas simple sports tasks may be executed over a longer period at higher levels of arousal.\" Applied to training: \"Excessive exposure of an athlete to complex training tasks can lead rapidly to 'overstress', while simple tasks may be insufficiently challenging to produce continued supercompensation. A balance between complexity, simplicity, intensity and volume of load is necessary to ensure growth and to minimise overtraining\" (PDF pp. 351-352 / printed pp. 340-341)."," 4. **Repeated stress changes the response.** \"regular exposure to a stressor may produce either habituation or oversensitisation, so that the same level of stress may not produce the same response at a later stage. In either event, timely use of restoration measures is of central importance.\""," 5. **Limits of the curve.** It \"implies that there is just one discrete peak\"; the \"'second wind' phenomenon (curve B) and the effect of task complexity on curve shape are two of several reasons why the original curve must be applied to restoration and stress management with circumspection.\" Also, \"it is well known that both sensory underload and overload may lead to phases of enhanced performance (curves A, C),\" and meditation and similar states \"may enhance performance in certain situations (curve A).\" Hence \"both optimisation and transcendental stress management strategies are necessary.\""]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e5c33db7-fac0-4ef9-8735-f384a0d1e0d7","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"ffa99f3c-aa37-45e5-bef4-e7e115d7f045","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnohzdAMqNL4JDi6yTb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UUvGeqQCe5eQ6ztqSHCFFx","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7201,"cache_read_input_tokens":99511,"output_tokens":862,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":142},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7201},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":862,"cache_read_input_tokens":99511,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7201,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7201,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01UUvGeqQCe5eQ6ztqSHCFFx":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":4,"requestId":"req_011CffnofMsfAJUqK24p14wa","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"63369708-5bc3-4aa5-a395-0870c3f47acf","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:38:47.978Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"63369708-5bc3-4aa5-a395-0870c3f47acf","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UUvGeqQCe5eQ6ztqSHCFFx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttype: library-entry\n3\tkind: framework\n4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n6\tchapter: \"6.7\"\n7\ttopic: classification of sports\n8\tcategory: [classification, energy-systems, programming, specificity]\n9\tconfidence: supported\n10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Classify Sports by Movement Organisation and Energy Supply: Acyclic, Cyclic and Complex (Farfel 1969)\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — Programming rules need \"the limits of generality and specificity in individual sports or groups of related sports,\" so sports must be classified \"scientifically.\" \"Classification of sports into individual, team, ball or water sports, for example, is not scientifically useful; it is more appropriate to classify sports on the basis of the particular organisation of the sporting movements and characteristics of the physical systems used to produce their working effect\" (PDF p. 356 / printed p. 345, checked against the page image). The authors adopt three groups \"as already outlined by Farfel (1969)\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346, checked against the page image):\n17\t1. **Acyclic sports**, \"in which perfecting of the neuromuscular system for precise regulation of movements and the ability to produce great power play the dominant roles.\" Earlier on the same page: \"complex organisation of motor actions and an intense concentration of working effort, displayed briefly,\" with energy \"primarily through ATP-CP energy processes.\" Subgroups: \"powerful, brief, explosive efforts\" and \"fine motor control and spatially precise movement.\"\n18\t2. **Cyclic sports** \"(usually of submaximal and of moderate power), associated primarily with the production of muscular work through aerobic processes\": \"prolonged repetition of stereotyped work cycles of relatively simple organisation which do not require maximal muscular tension.\" Subgroups: \"extensive oxygen utilisation\" and work where \"the muscles have an inadequate oxygen supply.\"\n19\t3. **Complex (combined) sports**, \"where characteristically there is a high variability of motor actions under conditions of advancing fatigue and varying intensity of work, typified by team sports and one-on-one events (e.g. boxing and fencing).\" Their energy production \"is of a mixed aerobic-anaerobic character, with the greater part being derived from glycolytic reactions.\"\n20\t\n21\tA second rule: \"In classifying sports, it is necessary to consider competitive, as well as training activities,\" because training exercises can differ from competition, \"often due to the necessity for repeated reproduction of the competitive movements in training\"; this is where \"special endurance (e.g. speed-, jumping-, and strength-endurance)\" belongs. The authors' example: the 100 m takes about 10 s and \"does not depend on oxidative efficiency. However, in order for a sprinter to prepare himself to effectively display such effort, he should execute training loads that develop his aerobic and anaerobic potential, in particular the latter\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346).\n22\t\n23\t**Why it works** — The authors' rationale is physiological: \"The more prolonged and the lower the intensity of muscular work, the greater the role of aerobic processes\"; the glycolytic role \"grows as the duration of work decreases and the power of muscular work increases\"; in brief acyclic work under heavy load, athletes overcome resistance in \"tenths and even hundredths of a second,\" which demands \"an effective and reliable biodynamic structure\" (PDF p. 356 / printed p. 345). The three-group scheme itself is Farfel's convention, adopted without data, hence `supported` rather than `proven`.\n24\t\n25\t**When to apply** — Inference: use it as the first question when writing a programme for a new sport or position: which group, and which subgroup? The authors use this scheme again in 6.8.2 to say which systems specialise (see Related). Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once\"; \"priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 115, Explicit). Inference: Farfel's grouping is a coarse first pass at that priority choice, and the sprinter example supports training activities that differ from the competition's own energy profile.\n26\t\n27\t**When NOT to apply** — Source against Eric's baseline on football: the authors put team sports in the complex group, with \"the greater part\" of energy \"derived from glycolytic reactions\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346). Eric's baseline: for football, glycolytic conditioning \"is treated as a 'worst case scenario' to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116, Explicit, claimed again in interview 2026-09-28, Q1), and \"our staff does program it in the off-season\" (line 117, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)). The authors give no football data, and \"team sports\" is a broad category. Inference: within the complex group, positions differ enough (a lineman's short bursts against a receiver's repeated sprints) that the group label decides little on its own; the authors' own call to \"further subdivide\" applies. This is a different scheme from Diachkov's four categories by motor regime used in 5.1 (see Related).\n28\t\n29\t**Example** — The authors' own: boxing and fencing as one-on-one complex sports; the 100 m sprinter whose event does not depend on oxidative efficiency but whose training should still develop aerobic and, especially, anaerobic potential (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346).\n30\t\n31\t**Related** — [[framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type]] (Diachkov's four sport categories, 5.1; a different classification), [[principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system]] (6.8.2 applies this scheme), [[heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue]], [[claim--energy-system-contribution-by-sport-table]], [[claim--all-round-athletes-main-regime-cyclic-and-phasic-tonic]]\n32\t"}]},"uuid":"a41ba828-71fe-4e1c-91a8-def128409c39","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:38:48.129Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.7\"\ntopic: classification of sports\ncategory: [classification, energy-systems, programming, specificity]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Classify Sports by Movement Organisation and Energy Supply: Acyclic, Cyclic and Complex (Farfel 1969)\n\n**What it is** — Programming rules need \"the limits of generality and specificity in individual sports or groups of related sports,\" so sports must be classified \"scientifically.\" \"Classification of sports into individual, team, ball or water sports, for example, is not scientifically useful; it is more appropriate to classify sports on the basis of the particular organisation of the sporting movements and characteristics of the physical systems used to produce their working effect\" (PDF p. 356 / printed p. 345, checked against the page image). The authors adopt three groups \"as already outlined by Farfel (1969)\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346, checked against the page image):\n1. **Acyclic sports**, \"in which perfecting of the neuromuscular system for precise regulation of movements and the ability to produce great power play the dominant roles.\" Earlier on the same page: \"complex organisation of motor actions and an intense concentration of working effort, displayed briefly,\" with energy \"primarily through ATP-CP energy processes.\" Subgroups: \"powerful, brief, explosive efforts\" and \"fine motor control and spatially precise movement.\"\n2. **Cyclic sports** \"(usually of submaximal and of moderate power), associated primarily with the production of muscular work through aerobic processes\": \"prolonged repetition of stereotyped work cycles of relatively simple organisation which do not require maximal muscular tension.\" Subgroups: \"extensive oxygen utilisation\" and work where \"the muscles have an inadequate oxygen supply.\"\n3. **Complex (combined) sports**, \"where characteristically there is a high variability of motor actions under conditions of advancing fatigue and varying intensity of work, typified by team sports and one-on-one events (e.g. boxing and fencing).\" Their energy production \"is of a mixed aerobic-anaerobic character, with the greater part being derived from glycolytic reactions.\"\n\nA second rule: \"In classifying sports, it is necessary to consider competitive, as well as training activities,\" because training exercises can differ from competition, \"often due to the necessity for repeated reproduction of the competitive movements in training\"; this is where \"special endurance (e.g. speed-, jumping-, and strength-endurance)\" belongs. The authors' example: the 100 m takes about 10 s and \"does not depend on oxidative efficiency. However, in order for a sprinter to prepare himself to effectively display such effort, he should execute training loads that develop his aerobic and anaerobic potential, in particular the latter\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' rationale is physiological: \"The more prolonged and the lower the intensity of muscular work, the greater the role of aerobic processes\"; the glycolytic role \"grows as the duration of work decreases and the power of muscular work increases\"; in brief acyclic work under heavy load, athletes overcome resistance in \"tenths and even hundredths of a second,\" which demands \"an effective and reliable biodynamic structure\" (PDF p. 356 / printed p. 345). The three-group scheme itself is Farfel's convention, adopted without data, hence `supported` rather than `proven`.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: use it as the first question when writing a programme for a new sport or position: which group, and which subgroup? The authors use this scheme again in 6.8.2 to say which systems specialise (see Related). Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once\"; \"priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 115, Explicit). Inference: Farfel's grouping is a coarse first pass at that priority choice, and the sprinter example supports training activities that differ from the competition's own energy profile.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Source against Eric's baseline on football: the authors put team sports in the complex group, with \"the greater part\" of energy \"derived from glycolytic reactions\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346). Eric's baseline: for football, glycolytic conditioning \"is treated as a 'worst case scenario' to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116, Explicit, claimed again in interview 2026-09-28, Q1), and \"our staff does program it in the off-season\" (line 117, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)). The authors give no football data, and \"team sports\" is a broad category. Inference: within the complex group, positions differ enough (a lineman's short bursts against a receiver's repeated sprints) that the group label decides little on its own; the authors' own call to \"further subdivide\" applies. This is a different scheme from Diachkov's four categories by motor regime used in 5.1 (see Related).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: boxing and fencing as one-on-one complex sports; the 100 m sprinter whose event does not depend on oxidative efficiency but whose training should still develop aerobic and, especially, anaerobic potential (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type]] (Diachkov's four sport categories, 5.1; a different classification), [[principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system]] (6.8.2 applies this scheme), [[heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue]], [[claim--energy-system-contribution-by-sport-table]], [[claim--all-round-athletes-main-regime-cyclic-and-phasic-tonic]]\n","numLines":32,"startLine":1,"totalLines":32}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"63369708-5bc3-4aa5-a395-0870c3f47acf","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
