{"parentUuid":"49b3b47f-7bc4-4595-84eb-aee0a8b63423","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnpraaoNaavtx4DRDvD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01R3ae9xbyTkhrQj9X5XBZCE","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming.md","old_string":"that the group label decides little on its own; the authors' own call to \"further subdivide\" applies.","new_string":"that the group label decides little on its own. The authors call for subdivision (\"One should further subdivide the sports in each of these broad groups\") only within the acyclic and cyclic groups, before they introduce the third group (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346); extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":950,"cache_read_input_tokens":110261,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":950,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":30,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01R3ae9xbyTkhrQj9X5XBZCE":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming.md","old_string":"that the group label decides little on its own; the authors' own call to \"further subdivide\" applies.","new_string":"that the group label decides little on its own. The authors call for subdivision (\"One should further subdivide the sports in each of these broad groups\") only within the acyclic and cyclic groups, before they introduce the third group (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346); extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference."}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffnpoLNcKwNJ9gs42DbF","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d9f76840-a474-47c0-9bd8-0815e7a424a1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:38:58.675Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"d9f76840-a474-47c0-9bd8-0815e7a424a1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R3ae9xbyTkhrQj9X5XBZCE","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"8ca707be-1db7-48ab-969c-dab282e5c84a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:38:58.813Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming.md","oldString":"that the group label decides little on its own; the authors' own call to \"further subdivide\" applies.","newString":"that the group label decides little on its own. The authors call for subdivision (\"One should further subdivide the sports in each of these broad groups\") only within the acyclic and cyclic groups, before they introduce the third group (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346); extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.7\"\ntopic: classification of sports\ncategory: [classification, energy-systems, programming, specificity]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Classify Sports by Movement Organisation and Energy Supply: Acyclic, Cyclic and Complex (Farfel 1969)\n\n**What it is** — Programming rules need \"the limits of generality and specificity in individual sports or groups of related sports,\" so sports must be classified \"scientifically.\" \"Classification of sports into individual, team, ball or water sports, for example, is not scientifically useful; it is more appropriate to classify sports on the basis of the particular organisation of the sporting movements and characteristics of the physical systems used to produce their working effect\" (PDF p. 356 / printed p. 345, checked against the page image). The authors adopt three groups \"as already outlined by Farfel (1969)\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346, checked against the page image):\n1. **Acyclic sports**, \"in which perfecting of the neuromuscular system for precise regulation of movements and the ability to produce great power play the dominant roles.\" Earlier on the same page: \"complex organisation of motor actions and an intense concentration of working effort, displayed briefly,\" with energy \"primarily through ATP-CP energy processes.\" Subgroups: \"powerful, brief, explosive efforts\" and \"fine motor control and spatially precise movement.\"\n2. **Cyclic sports** \"(usually of submaximal and of moderate power), associated primarily with the production of muscular work through aerobic processes\": \"prolonged repetition of stereotyped work cycles of relatively simple organisation which do not require maximal muscular tension.\" Subgroups: \"extensive oxygen utilisation\" and work where \"the muscles have an inadequate oxygen supply.\"\n3. **Complex (combined) sports**, \"where characteristically there is a high variability of motor actions under conditions of advancing fatigue and varying intensity of work, typified by team sports and one-on-one events (e.g. boxing and fencing).\" Their energy production \"is of a mixed aerobic-anaerobic character, with the greater part being derived from glycolytic reactions.\"\n\nA second rule: \"In classifying sports, it is necessary to consider competitive, as well as training activities,\" because training exercises can differ from competition, \"often due to the necessity for repeated reproduction of the competitive movements in training\"; this is where \"special endurance (e.g. speed-, jumping-, and strength-endurance)\" belongs. The authors' example: the 100 m takes about 10 s and \"does not depend on oxidative efficiency. However, in order for a sprinter to prepare himself to effectively display such effort, he should execute training loads that develop his aerobic and anaerobic potential, in particular the latter\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' rationale is physiological: \"The more prolonged and the lower the intensity of muscular work, the greater the role of aerobic processes\"; the glycolytic role \"grows as the duration of work decreases and the power of muscular work increases\"; in brief acyclic work under heavy load, athletes overcome resistance in \"tenths and even hundredths of a second,\" which demands \"an effective and reliable biodynamic structure\" (PDF p. 356 / printed p. 345). The three-group scheme itself is Farfel's convention, adopted without data, hence `supported` rather than `proven`.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: use it as the first question when writing a programme for a new sport or position: which group, and which subgroup? The authors use this scheme again in 6.8.2 to say which systems specialise (see Related). Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once\"; \"priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 115, Explicit). Inference: Farfel's grouping is a coarse first pass at that priority choice, and the sprinter example supports training activities that differ from the competition's own energy profile.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Source against Eric's baseline on football: the authors put team sports in the complex group, with \"the greater part\" of energy \"derived from glycolytic reactions\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346). Eric's baseline: for football, glycolytic conditioning \"is treated as a 'worst case scenario' to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116, Explicit, claimed again in interview 2026-09-28, Q1), and \"our staff does program it in the off-season\" (line 117, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)). The authors give no football data, and \"team sports\" is a broad category. Inference: within the complex group, positions differ enough (a lineman's short bursts against a receiver's repeated sprints) that the group label decides little on its own; the authors' own call to \"further subdivide\" applies. This is a different scheme from Diachkov's four categories by motor regime used in 5.1 (see Related).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: boxing and fencing as one-on-one complex sports; the 100 m sprinter whose event does not depend on oxidative efficiency but whose training should still develop aerobic and, especially, anaerobic potential (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--special-strength-methods-classified-by-sport-group-and-strength-type]] (Diachkov's four sport categories, 5.1; a different classification), [[principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system]] (6.8.2 applies this scheme), [[heuristic--cyclic-vs-acyclic-speed-strength-tempo-rest-and-fatigue]], [[claim--energy-system-contribution-by-sport-table]], [[claim--all-round-athletes-main-regime-cyclic-and-phasic-tonic]]\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":24,"oldLines":7,"newStart":24,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Inference: use it as the first question when writing a programme for a new sport or position: which group, and which subgroup? The authors use this scheme again in 6.8.2 to say which systems specialise (see Related). Eric's baseline: \"A sport cannot maximize all three energy systems at once\"; \"priority has to be picked based on what the competition actually demands\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 115, Explicit). Inference: Farfel's grouping is a coarse first pass at that priority choice, and the sprinter example supports training activities that differ from the competition's own energy profile."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — Source against Eric's baseline on football: the authors put team sports in the complex group, with \"the greater part\" of energy \"derived from glycolytic reactions\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346). Eric's baseline: for football, glycolytic conditioning \"is treated as a 'worst case scenario' to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116, Explicit, claimed again in interview 2026-09-28, Q1), and \"our staff does program it in the off-season\" (line 117, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)). The authors give no football data, and \"team sports\" is a broad category. Inference: within the complex group, positions differ enough (a lineman's short bursts against a receiver's repeated sprints) that the group label decides little on its own; the authors' own call to \"further subdivide\" applies. This is a different scheme from Diachkov's four categories by motor regime used in 5.1 (see Related).","+**When NOT to apply** — Source against Eric's baseline on football: the authors put team sports in the complex group, with \"the greater part\" of energy \"derived from glycolytic reactions\" (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346). Eric's baseline: for football, glycolytic conditioning \"is treated as a 'worst case scenario' to be absorbed incidentally through camp and practice rather than programmed as a primary target\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 116, Explicit, claimed again in interview 2026-09-28, Q1), and \"our staff does program it in the off-season\" (line 117, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q19)). The authors give no football data, and \"team sports\" is a broad category. Inference: within the complex group, positions differ enough (a lineman's short bursts against a receiver's repeated sprints) that the group label decides little on its own. The authors call for subdivision (\"One should further subdivide the sports in each of these broad groups\") only within the acyclic and cyclic groups, before they introduce the third group (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346); extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference. This is a different scheme from Diachkov's four categories by motor regime used in 5.1 (see Related)."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: boxing and fencing as one-on-one complex sports; the 100 m sprinter whose event does not depend on oxidative efficiency but whose training should still develop aerobic and, especially, anaerobic potential (PDF p. 357 / printed p. 346)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d9f76840-a474-47c0-9bd8-0815e7a424a1","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"8ca707be-1db7-48ab-969c-dab282e5c84a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnpraaoNaavtx4DRDvD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015DipR3BoxFPC3ZUN554USL","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":950,"cache_read_input_tokens":110261,"output_tokens":433,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":950},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":433,"cache_read_input_tokens":110261,"cache_creation_input_tokens":950,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":950,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_015DipR3BoxFPC3ZUN554USL":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffnpoLNcKwNJ9gs42DbF","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"044846b6-a4d0-4e55-bbab-635c5e2563c0","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:38:59.422Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"044846b6-a4d0-4e55-bbab-635c5e2563c0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015DipR3BoxFPC3ZUN554USL","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttype: library-entry\n3\tkind: principle\n4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n6\tchapter: \"6.8.1\"\n7\ttopic: mesocycle as the unit of training\n8\tcategory: [periodisation, programming, supercompensation, planning]\n9\tconfidence: untested\n10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Plan in Mesocycles, Not Microcycles, and Do Not Read Every Planned Dip in Work Capacity as a Programming Error\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — The authors list three practical rules that followed from supercompensation research (Weigert's law, \"first formally described by Folbrot (1941, 1952, 1958)\"; also Yakovlev 1955, 1975; Gorkin et al 1973; Volkov 1975; Monogarov & Platonov 1975; Sergeyev 1981) (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image):\n17\t- \"It is necessary for the athlete to maintain a state of adaptation during training, so that it is possible to execute the fundamental sporting actions effectively over an extended period\"\n18\t- \"Repetition of training loads when the body is in a state of insufficient restoration is generally undesirable, although this is permissible in certain instances\"\n19\t- \"Special work capacity should rise steadily during the course of training; any decrease at given stages of training would indicate that the training is incorrectly organised.\"\n20\t\n21\tTheir verdict: \"Undoubtedly, all of these premises were progressive in their time and to a certain extent are still valid today. However, modern sporting requirements reveal the necessity to alter the main approach to structuring training and obey common sense, which indicates that the fundamental unit of training should be the mesocycle and not the microcycle.\" This requires \"An extensive study of the characteristics of long-term adaptation,\" whose value \"will be appreciated only if one can observe the specific effect of given training loads on the dynamics of the athlete's state and determine the quantitative characteristics of the adaptation process\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\n22\t\n23\tThe same section then describes a loading form that deliberately lowers the functional indicators for weeks (concentrated loading, Fig 6.23; PDF pp. 360-361 / printed pp. 349-350). Inference: this is the authors' own revision of the third rule: a planned decrease inside a concentrated mesocycle is not, in their model, a sign of incorrect organisation.\n24\t\n25\t**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that the effects they care about (long-term adaptation, delayed gains after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so judging each microcycle against the previous one misreads them. They give no study for the mesocycle claim itself on this page; \"common sense\" is their stated basis.\n26\t\n27\t**When to apply** — Inference: when reviewing a plan, judge progress block by block, not week by week, especially during a deliberately heavy block. Eric's baseline: \"Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust programming\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 59, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the two fit if weekly data are read against the mesocycle's expected shape: a decline during a planned concentrated block is expected, while the same decline in a normal block is a signal. Eric's baseline: undulating periodization, \"waving volume week to week\" (line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: a wave inside a mesocycle is already a planned non-steady rise.\n28\t\n29\t**When NOT to apply** — The authors keep the old rules \"to a certain extent ... still valid,\" and the second one (\"generally undesirable ... permissible in certain instances\") is not withdrawn. Inference: a decline outside a planned loading block, or one that does not recover when load drops, is still the warning the third rule describes. Batch 6a (sections 6.1 to 6.3, Matveyev's periodisation and \"The Periodisation Controversy,\" PDF pp. 324-346) may carry an overlapping entry on the authors' critique of classical periodisation; this entry is limited to the 6.8.1 statement.\n30\t\n31\t**Example** — The authors' own contrast: the third rule says any decrease means the training is \"incorrectly organised\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347), while concentrated loading produces a \"decrease of the functional indicators\" followed by a rise that \"exceeds the level achieved with the first form of adaptation\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349).\n32\t\n33\t**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--supercompensation-cycle]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]] (Issurin also builds plans from mesocycle blocks)\n34\t"}]},"uuid":"a0ebe010-0a90-4260-bc6a-5459687a7a74","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:38:59.599Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.8.1\"\ntopic: mesocycle as the unit of training\ncategory: [periodisation, programming, supercompensation, planning]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Plan in Mesocycles, Not Microcycles, and Do Not Read Every Planned Dip in Work Capacity as a Programming Error\n\n**What it is** — The authors list three practical rules that followed from supercompensation research (Weigert's law, \"first formally described by Folbrot (1941, 1952, 1958)\"; also Yakovlev 1955, 1975; Gorkin et al 1973; Volkov 1975; Monogarov & Platonov 1975; Sergeyev 1981) (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image):\n- \"It is necessary for the athlete to maintain a state of adaptation during training, so that it is possible to execute the fundamental sporting actions effectively over an extended period\"\n- \"Repetition of training loads when the body is in a state of insufficient restoration is generally undesirable, although this is permissible in certain instances\"\n- \"Special work capacity should rise steadily during the course of training; any decrease at given stages of training would indicate that the training is incorrectly organised.\"\n\nTheir verdict: \"Undoubtedly, all of these premises were progressive in their time and to a certain extent are still valid today. However, modern sporting requirements reveal the necessity to alter the main approach to structuring training and obey common sense, which indicates that the fundamental unit of training should be the mesocycle and not the microcycle.\" This requires \"An extensive study of the characteristics of long-term adaptation,\" whose value \"will be appreciated only if one can observe the specific effect of given training loads on the dynamics of the athlete's state and determine the quantitative characteristics of the adaptation process\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\n\nThe same section then describes a loading form that deliberately lowers the functional indicators for weeks (concentrated loading, Fig 6.23; PDF pp. 360-361 / printed pp. 349-350). Inference: this is the authors' own revision of the third rule: a planned decrease inside a concentrated mesocycle is not, in their model, a sign of incorrect organisation.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that the effects they care about (long-term adaptation, delayed gains after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so judging each microcycle against the previous one misreads them. They give no study for the mesocycle claim itself on this page; \"common sense\" is their stated basis.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when reviewing a plan, judge progress block by block, not week by week, especially during a deliberately heavy block. Eric's baseline: \"Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust programming\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 59, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the two fit if weekly data are read against the mesocycle's expected shape: a decline during a planned concentrated block is expected, while the same decline in a normal block is a signal. Eric's baseline: undulating periodization, \"waving volume week to week\" (line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: a wave inside a mesocycle is already a planned non-steady rise.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors keep the old rules \"to a certain extent ... still valid,\" and the second one (\"generally undesirable ... permissible in certain instances\") is not withdrawn. Inference: a decline outside a planned loading block, or one that does not recover when load drops, is still the warning the third rule describes. Batch 6a (sections 6.1 to 6.3, Matveyev's periodisation and \"The Periodisation Controversy,\" PDF pp. 324-346) may carry an overlapping entry on the authors' critique of classical periodisation; this entry is limited to the 6.8.1 statement.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own contrast: the third rule says any decrease means the training is \"incorrectly organised\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347), while concentrated loading produces a \"decrease of the functional indicators\" followed by a rise that \"exceeds the level achieved with the first form of adaptation\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--supercompensation-cycle]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]] (Issurin also builds plans from mesocycle blocks)\n","numLines":34,"startLine":1,"totalLines":34}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"044846b6-a4d0-4e55-bbab-635c5e2563c0","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
