{"parentUuid":"7bf738c9-e3e9-4f9c-9c2f-154817e8122e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnqXQCiR1Lq5nP3URV6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XrGVokrfYBzjuEKk7jWtJ2","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md","old_string":"**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that the effects they care about (long-term adaptation, delayed gains after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so judging each microcycle against the previous one misreads them. They give no study for the mesocycle claim itself on this page; \"common sense\" is their stated basis.","new_string":"**Why it works** — The authors' stated basis is \"modern sporting requirements\" and \"common sense\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe (long-term adaptation, the delayed rise after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":454,"cache_read_input_tokens":113563,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":454,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01XrGVokrfYBzjuEKk7jWtJ2":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md","old_string":"**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that the effects they care about (long-term adaptation, delayed gains after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so judging each microcycle against the previous one misreads them. They give no study for the mesocycle claim itself on this page; \"common sense\" is their stated basis.","new_string":"**Why it works** — The authors' stated basis is \"modern sporting requirements\" and \"common sense\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe (long-term adaptation, the delayed rise after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them."}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffnqTEBBwDaXVDSCutA9","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"5cfe20c6-0296-46a6-a99d-126a97da5440","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:39:08.659Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"5cfe20c6-0296-46a6-a99d-126a97da5440","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XrGVokrfYBzjuEKk7jWtJ2","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"3d613945-77b0-4eeb-96ea-55ceae18dc94","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:39:08.789Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md","oldString":"**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that the effects they care about (long-term adaptation, delayed gains after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so judging each microcycle against the previous one misreads them. They give no study for the mesocycle claim itself on this page; \"common sense\" is their stated basis.","newString":"**Why it works** — The authors' stated basis is \"modern sporting requirements\" and \"common sense\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe (long-term adaptation, the delayed rise after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them.","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.8.1\"\ntopic: mesocycle as the unit of training\ncategory: [periodisation, programming, supercompensation, planning]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Plan in Mesocycles, Not Microcycles, and Do Not Read Every Planned Dip in Work Capacity as a Programming Error\n\n**What it is** — The authors list three practical rules that followed from supercompensation research (Weigert's law, \"first formally described by Folbrot (1941, 1952, 1958)\"; also Yakovlev 1955, 1975; Gorkin et al 1973; Volkov 1975; Monogarov & Platonov 1975; Sergeyev 1981) (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image):\n- \"It is necessary for the athlete to maintain a state of adaptation during training, so that it is possible to execute the fundamental sporting actions effectively over an extended period\"\n- \"Repetition of training loads when the body is in a state of insufficient restoration is generally undesirable, although this is permissible in certain instances\"\n- \"Special work capacity should rise steadily during the course of training; any decrease at given stages of training would indicate that the training is incorrectly organised.\"\n\nTheir verdict: \"Undoubtedly, all of these premises were progressive in their time and to a certain extent are still valid today. However, modern sporting requirements reveal the necessity to alter the main approach to structuring training and obey common sense, which indicates that the fundamental unit of training should be the mesocycle and not the microcycle.\" This requires \"An extensive study of the characteristics of long-term adaptation,\" whose value \"will be appreciated only if one can observe the specific effect of given training loads on the dynamics of the athlete's state and determine the quantitative characteristics of the adaptation process\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\n\nThe same section then describes a loading form that deliberately lowers the functional indicators for weeks (concentrated loading, Fig 6.23; PDF pp. 360-361 / printed pp. 349-350). Inference: this is the authors' own revision of the third rule: a planned decrease inside a concentrated mesocycle is not, in their model, a sign of incorrect organisation.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that the effects they care about (long-term adaptation, delayed gains after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so judging each microcycle against the previous one misreads them. They give no study for the mesocycle claim itself on this page; \"common sense\" is their stated basis.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when reviewing a plan, judge progress block by block, not week by week, especially during a deliberately heavy block. Eric's baseline: \"Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust programming\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 59, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the two fit if weekly data are read against the mesocycle's expected shape: a decline during a planned concentrated block is expected, while the same decline in a normal block is a signal. Eric's baseline: undulating periodization, \"waving volume week to week\" (line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: a wave inside a mesocycle is already a planned non-steady rise.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors keep the old rules \"to a certain extent ... still valid,\" and the second one (\"generally undesirable ... permissible in certain instances\") is not withdrawn. Inference: a decline outside a planned loading block, or one that does not recover when load drops, is still the warning the third rule describes. Batch 6a (sections 6.1 to 6.3, Matveyev's periodisation and \"The Periodisation Controversy,\" PDF pp. 324-346) may carry an overlapping entry on the authors' critique of classical periodisation; this entry is limited to the 6.8.1 statement.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own contrast: the third rule says any decrease means the training is \"incorrectly organised\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347), while concentrated loading produces a \"decrease of the functional indicators\" followed by a rise that \"exceeds the level achieved with the first form of adaptation\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--supercompensation-cycle]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]] (Issurin also builds plans from mesocycle blocks)\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":22,"oldLines":7,"newStart":22,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," The same section then describes a loading form that deliberately lowers the functional indicators for weeks (concentrated loading, Fig 6.23; PDF pp. 360-361 / printed pp. 349-350). Inference: this is the authors' own revision of the third rule: a planned decrease inside a concentrated mesocycle is not, in their model, a sign of incorrect organisation."," ","-**Why it works** — The authors' reason is that the effects they care about (long-term adaptation, delayed gains after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so judging each microcycle against the previous one misreads them. They give no study for the mesocycle claim itself on this page; \"common sense\" is their stated basis.","+**Why it works** — The authors' stated basis is \"modern sporting requirements\" and \"common sense\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe (long-term adaptation, the delayed rise after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them."," "," **When to apply** — Inference: when reviewing a plan, judge progress block by block, not week by week, especially during a deliberately heavy block. Eric's baseline: \"Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust programming\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 59, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the two fit if weekly data are read against the mesocycle's expected shape: a decline during a planned concentrated block is expected, while the same decline in a normal block is a signal. Eric's baseline: undulating periodization, \"waving volume week to week\" (line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: a wave inside a mesocycle is already a planned non-steady rise."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5cfe20c6-0296-46a6-a99d-126a97da5440","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"3d613945-77b0-4eeb-96ea-55ceae18dc94","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnqXQCiR1Lq5nP3URV6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019Bdk7ihS5SE9GM9p4yeKz1","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md","old_string":"is still the warning the third rule describes. Batch 6a (sections 6.1 to 6.3, Matveyev's periodisation and \"The Periodisation Controversy,\" PDF pp. 324-346) may carry an overlapping entry on the authors' critique of classical periodisation; this entry is limited to the 6.8.1 statement.","new_string":"is still the warning the third rule describes. The book calls the training session \"the fundamental unit\" of periodisation in 6.2 (PDF p. 327 / printed p. 316) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit of training in 6.8.1 (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording (see [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]])."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":454,"cache_read_input_tokens":113563,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":454,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_019Bdk7ihS5SE9GM9p4yeKz1":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md","old_string":"is still the warning the third rule describes. Batch 6a (sections 6.1 to 6.3, Matveyev's periodisation and \"The Periodisation Controversy,\" PDF pp. 324-346) may carry an overlapping entry on the authors' critique of classical periodisation; this entry is limited to the 6.8.1 statement.","new_string":"is still the warning the third rule describes. The book calls the training session \"the fundamental unit\" of periodisation in 6.2 (PDF p. 327 / printed p. 316) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit of training in 6.8.1 (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording (see [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]])."}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffnqTEBBwDaXVDSCutA9","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"2f79baf3-78f0-4bad-9539-6bcba0aead22","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:39:11.522Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"2f79baf3-78f0-4bad-9539-6bcba0aead22","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019Bdk7ihS5SE9GM9p4yeKz1","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"c4af900b-66c8-4335-8a61-978d68cfc7a5","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:39:11.641Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md","oldString":"is still the warning the third rule describes. Batch 6a (sections 6.1 to 6.3, Matveyev's periodisation and \"The Periodisation Controversy,\" PDF pp. 324-346) may carry an overlapping entry on the authors' critique of classical periodisation; this entry is limited to the 6.8.1 statement.","newString":"is still the warning the third rule describes. The book calls the training session \"the fundamental unit\" of periodisation in 6.2 (PDF p. 327 / printed p. 316) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit of training in 6.8.1 (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording (see [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]]).","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.8.1\"\ntopic: mesocycle as the unit of training\ncategory: [periodisation, programming, supercompensation, planning]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Plan in Mesocycles, Not Microcycles, and Do Not Read Every Planned Dip in Work Capacity as a Programming Error\n\n**What it is** — The authors list three practical rules that followed from supercompensation research (Weigert's law, \"first formally described by Folbrot (1941, 1952, 1958)\"; also Yakovlev 1955, 1975; Gorkin et al 1973; Volkov 1975; Monogarov & Platonov 1975; Sergeyev 1981) (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image):\n- \"It is necessary for the athlete to maintain a state of adaptation during training, so that it is possible to execute the fundamental sporting actions effectively over an extended period\"\n- \"Repetition of training loads when the body is in a state of insufficient restoration is generally undesirable, although this is permissible in certain instances\"\n- \"Special work capacity should rise steadily during the course of training; any decrease at given stages of training would indicate that the training is incorrectly organised.\"\n\nTheir verdict: \"Undoubtedly, all of these premises were progressive in their time and to a certain extent are still valid today. However, modern sporting requirements reveal the necessity to alter the main approach to structuring training and obey common sense, which indicates that the fundamental unit of training should be the mesocycle and not the microcycle.\" This requires \"An extensive study of the characteristics of long-term adaptation,\" whose value \"will be appreciated only if one can observe the specific effect of given training loads on the dynamics of the athlete's state and determine the quantitative characteristics of the adaptation process\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\n\nThe same section then describes a loading form that deliberately lowers the functional indicators for weeks (concentrated loading, Fig 6.23; PDF pp. 360-361 / printed pp. 349-350). Inference: this is the authors' own revision of the third rule: a planned decrease inside a concentrated mesocycle is not, in their model, a sign of incorrect organisation.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated basis is \"modern sporting requirements\" and \"common sense\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe (long-term adaptation, the delayed rise after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when reviewing a plan, judge progress block by block, not week by week, especially during a deliberately heavy block. Eric's baseline: \"Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust programming\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 59, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the two fit if weekly data are read against the mesocycle's expected shape: a decline during a planned concentrated block is expected, while the same decline in a normal block is a signal. Eric's baseline: undulating periodization, \"waving volume week to week\" (line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: a wave inside a mesocycle is already a planned non-steady rise.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors keep the old rules \"to a certain extent ... still valid,\" and the second one (\"generally undesirable ... permissible in certain instances\") is not withdrawn. Inference: a decline outside a planned loading block, or one that does not recover when load drops, is still the warning the third rule describes. Batch 6a (sections 6.1 to 6.3, Matveyev's periodisation and \"The Periodisation Controversy,\" PDF pp. 324-346) may carry an overlapping entry on the authors' critique of classical periodisation; this entry is limited to the 6.8.1 statement.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own contrast: the third rule says any decrease means the training is \"incorrectly organised\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347), while concentrated loading produces a \"decrease of the functional indicators\" followed by a rise that \"exceeds the level achieved with the first form of adaptation\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--supercompensation-cycle]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]] (Issurin also builds plans from mesocycle blocks)\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":26,"oldLines":7,"newStart":26,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Inference: when reviewing a plan, judge progress block by block, not week by week, especially during a deliberately heavy block. Eric's baseline: \"Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust programming\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 59, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the two fit if weekly data are read against the mesocycle's expected shape: a decline during a planned concentrated block is expected, while the same decline in a normal block is a signal. Eric's baseline: undulating periodization, \"waving volume week to week\" (line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: a wave inside a mesocycle is already a planned non-steady rise."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The authors keep the old rules \"to a certain extent ... still valid,\" and the second one (\"generally undesirable ... permissible in certain instances\") is not withdrawn. Inference: a decline outside a planned loading block, or one that does not recover when load drops, is still the warning the third rule describes. Batch 6a (sections 6.1 to 6.3, Matveyev's periodisation and \"The Periodisation Controversy,\" PDF pp. 324-346) may carry an overlapping entry on the authors' critique of classical periodisation; this entry is limited to the 6.8.1 statement.","+**When NOT to apply** — The authors keep the old rules \"to a certain extent ... still valid,\" and the second one (\"generally undesirable ... permissible in certain instances\") is not withdrawn. Inference: a decline outside a planned loading block, or one that does not recover when load drops, is still the warning the third rule describes. The book calls the training session \"the fundamental unit\" of periodisation in 6.2 (PDF p. 327 / printed p. 316) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit of training in 6.8.1 (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording (see [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]])."," "," **Example** — The authors' own contrast: the third rule says any decrease means the training is \"incorrectly organised\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347), while concentrated loading produces a \"decrease of the functional indicators\" followed by a rise that \"exceeds the level achieved with the first form of adaptation\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349)."," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2f79baf3-78f0-4bad-9539-6bcba0aead22","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"c4af900b-66c8-4335-8a61-978d68cfc7a5","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnqXQCiR1Lq5nP3URV6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01S7AUSNRP1MHHb4dex886HJ","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md","old_string":"(Issurin also builds plans from mesocycle blocks)","new_string":"(Issurin also builds plans from mesocycle blocks), [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]] (6.2, the session as \"the fundamental unit\")"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":454,"cache_read_input_tokens":113563,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":454,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01S7AUSNRP1MHHb4dex886HJ":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md","old_string":"(Issurin also builds plans from mesocycle blocks)","new_string":"(Issurin also builds plans from mesocycle blocks), [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]] (6.2, the session as \"the fundamental unit\")"}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011CffnqTEBBwDaXVDSCutA9","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4ee32281-57c3-4bfb-92eb-d7fc7909f7eb","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:39:13.208Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"4ee32281-57c3-4bfb-92eb-d7fc7909f7eb","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S7AUSNRP1MHHb4dex886HJ","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"2b9af329-7d85-4772-b54f-bdfe8bdf484e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:39:13.321Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md","oldString":"(Issurin also builds plans from mesocycle blocks)","newString":"(Issurin also builds plans from mesocycle blocks), [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]] (6.2, the session as \"the fundamental unit\")","originalFile":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.8.1\"\ntopic: mesocycle as the unit of training\ncategory: [periodisation, programming, supercompensation, planning]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Plan in Mesocycles, Not Microcycles, and Do Not Read Every Planned Dip in Work Capacity as a Programming Error\n\n**What it is** — The authors list three practical rules that followed from supercompensation research (Weigert's law, \"first formally described by Folbrot (1941, 1952, 1958)\"; also Yakovlev 1955, 1975; Gorkin et al 1973; Volkov 1975; Monogarov & Platonov 1975; Sergeyev 1981) (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image):\n- \"It is necessary for the athlete to maintain a state of adaptation during training, so that it is possible to execute the fundamental sporting actions effectively over an extended period\"\n- \"Repetition of training loads when the body is in a state of insufficient restoration is generally undesirable, although this is permissible in certain instances\"\n- \"Special work capacity should rise steadily during the course of training; any decrease at given stages of training would indicate that the training is incorrectly organised.\"\n\nTheir verdict: \"Undoubtedly, all of these premises were progressive in their time and to a certain extent are still valid today. However, modern sporting requirements reveal the necessity to alter the main approach to structuring training and obey common sense, which indicates that the fundamental unit of training should be the mesocycle and not the microcycle.\" This requires \"An extensive study of the characteristics of long-term adaptation,\" whose value \"will be appreciated only if one can observe the specific effect of given training loads on the dynamics of the athlete's state and determine the quantitative characteristics of the adaptation process\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\n\nThe same section then describes a loading form that deliberately lowers the functional indicators for weeks (concentrated loading, Fig 6.23; PDF pp. 360-361 / printed pp. 349-350). Inference: this is the authors' own revision of the third rule: a planned decrease inside a concentrated mesocycle is not, in their model, a sign of incorrect organisation.\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' stated basis is \"modern sporting requirements\" and \"common sense\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe (long-term adaptation, the delayed rise after concentrated loading) unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when reviewing a plan, judge progress block by block, not week by week, especially during a deliberately heavy block. Eric's baseline: \"Weekly tracking is preferred over infrequent (e.g., once-a-phase) testing, specifically to catch plateaus or declines early enough to adjust programming\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 59, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the two fit if weekly data are read against the mesocycle's expected shape: a decline during a planned concentrated block is expected, while the same decline in a normal block is a signal. Eric's baseline: undulating periodization, \"waving volume week to week\" (line 106, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: a wave inside a mesocycle is already a planned non-steady rise.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The authors keep the old rules \"to a certain extent ... still valid,\" and the second one (\"generally undesirable ... permissible in certain instances\") is not withdrawn. Inference: a decline outside a planned loading block, or one that does not recover when load drops, is still the warning the third rule describes. The book calls the training session \"the fundamental unit\" of periodisation in 6.2 (PDF p. 327 / printed p. 316) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit of training in 6.8.1 (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording (see [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]]).\n\n**Example** — The authors' own contrast: the third rule says any decrease means the training is \"incorrectly organised\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347), while concentrated loading produces a \"decrease of the functional indicators\" followed by a rise that \"exceeds the level achieved with the first form of adaptation\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--supercompensation-cycle]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]] (Issurin also builds plans from mesocycle blocks)\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":30,"oldLines":4,"newStart":30,"newLines":4,"lines":[" "," **Example** — The authors' own contrast: the third rule says any decrease means the training is \"incorrectly organised\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347), while concentrated loading produces a \"decrease of the functional indicators\" followed by a rise that \"exceeds the level achieved with the first form of adaptation\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349)."," ","-**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--supercompensation-cycle]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]] (Issurin also builds plans from mesocycle blocks)","+**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--supercompensation-cycle]], [[framework--fitness-fatigue-modeling-and-taper-timing]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-transmutation-realization-mesocycles]] (Issurin also builds plans from mesocycle blocks), [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]] (6.2, the session as \"the fundamental unit\")"]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4ee32281-57c3-4bfb-92eb-d7fc7909f7eb","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"2b9af329-7d85-4772-b54f-bdfe8bdf484e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnqXQCiR1Lq5nP3URV6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YQN9MuyeNPMwHZACKfbfD5","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":454,"cache_read_input_tokens":113563,"output_tokens":1262,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":42},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":454},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1262,"cache_read_input_tokens":113563,"cache_creation_input_tokens":454,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":454,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01YQN9MuyeNPMwHZACKfbfD5":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":4,"requestId":"req_011CffnqTEBBwDaXVDSCutA9","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"315f86de-eff1-4f45-9261-17ec37cd062d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:39:13.899Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"315f86de-eff1-4f45-9261-17ec37cd062d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YQN9MuyeNPMwHZACKfbfD5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttype: library-entry\n3\tkind: claim\n4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n6\tchapter: \"6.8.1\"\n7\ttopic: long-term adaptation dynamics\n8\tcategory: [long-term-development, speed-strength, periodisation, monitoring]\n9\tconfidence: supported\n10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Long-Term Speed-Strength Adaptation: Rises Every Year, Starts Each Year Below Last Year's Peak, Rises Less in the Highly Qualified, and Follows the Season's Peaks\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — From \"observing the dynamics of an athlete's special physical preparedness indicators over many years of training,\" the authors state four conclusions, mainly for \"sports which require explosive effort\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image):\n17\t1. \"The absolute and mean level of special strength preparedness increases annually.\"\n18\t2. \"The initial level of speed-strength in each annual cycle is lower than the level at the end of the preceding year's, but higher than at its beginning.\"\n19\t3. \"The rise in speed-strength from year to year is larger for athletes of medium qualification than for highly qualified athletes.\"\n20\t4. The annual trend \"is determined by the traditional periodisation of training for the given sport and competition calendar. One can clearly trace a twin peak in the dynamics of speed-strength with two competition stages, because the level of special strength preparedness is higher in the second competition stage than in the first. In types of sports with one competition season, speed-strength rises regularly and uniformly.\"\n21\t\n22\tFig 6.22, \"The dynamics of adaptation in sports training over several years (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image and a high-resolution crop), is a schematic over Years 1-3 with no numeric axis (\"Relative levels\"): a thick, nearly straight rising line A labelled \"Fitness state\"; two wavy lines B and C labelled \"Functional indicators\" that oscillate around A and dip near each year boundary; and a lower, slowly rising wavy line D labelled \"Skill.\" The text assigns B to bi-cyclic and C to mono-cyclic periodisation, and A to \"relatively stable, long-term adaptation over many years.\" D shows that \"adaptation in sport consists not only of increased motor potential, but also the improvement of the athlete's skills to more effectively utilise this potential\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, text layer only).\n23\t\n24\t**Why it works** — The authors read the annual waves (B, C) as \"temporary, unstable and to a certain extent, reversible\" compensatory changes riding on slower stable adaptation (A) (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, text layer only; see [[claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation]]). No athletes, sports, sample sizes or citations are given for the four conclusions; \"Extensive research allows us to reach the following conclusions\" is the only basis stated, hence `supported` at most.\n25\t\n26\t**When to apply** — Inference: set each year's expectations from these patterns: speed-strength at the start of a new year should sit below last year's best but above last year's start; a smaller annual gain in an advanced athlete is expected, not a failure. Eric's baseline: \"Preparedness (the athlete's underlying physical capability, built over a long period) is explicitly distinguished from readiness (day-to-day fluctuation)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 64, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: Fig 6.22's A and B/C lines draw that distinction, on a season scale rather than a daily one. Eric's baseline: in football \"you want to peak every Saturday, but definitely throughout the month of November\" (line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: football's one long season is closest to the authors' single-season case, where speed-strength \"rises regularly and uniformly.\"\n27\t\n28\t**When NOT to apply** — The conclusions come from explosive-strength sports; the authors say long-term adaptation in endurance sports has not been observed as clearly (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, text layer only). Figure against text: the text later describes graph A as \"a parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348), but in the image A is close to a straight line over the three years drawn, with no visible peak. Inference: for an athlete whose speed-strength has plateaued across years, conclusion 1 no longer describes them; that is the limit the parabola refers to.\n29\t\n30\t**Example** — The authors' own: the \"twin peak\" in speed-strength with two competition stages, the second higher than the first (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\n31\t\n32\t**Related** — [[claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation]], [[principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors]], [[framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions]], [[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]], [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]\n33\t"}]},"uuid":"aca7b75e-9b34-4dae-94c5-86d41dd2e32d","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:39:14.066Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.8.1\"\ntopic: long-term adaptation dynamics\ncategory: [long-term-development, speed-strength, periodisation, monitoring]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Long-Term Speed-Strength Adaptation: Rises Every Year, Starts Each Year Below Last Year's Peak, Rises Less in the Highly Qualified, and Follows the Season's Peaks\n\n**What it is** — From \"observing the dynamics of an athlete's special physical preparedness indicators over many years of training,\" the authors state four conclusions, mainly for \"sports which require explosive effort\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image):\n1. \"The absolute and mean level of special strength preparedness increases annually.\"\n2. \"The initial level of speed-strength in each annual cycle is lower than the level at the end of the preceding year's, but higher than at its beginning.\"\n3. \"The rise in speed-strength from year to year is larger for athletes of medium qualification than for highly qualified athletes.\"\n4. The annual trend \"is determined by the traditional periodisation of training for the given sport and competition calendar. One can clearly trace a twin peak in the dynamics of speed-strength with two competition stages, because the level of special strength preparedness is higher in the second competition stage than in the first. In types of sports with one competition season, speed-strength rises regularly and uniformly.\"\n\nFig 6.22, \"The dynamics of adaptation in sports training over several years (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image and a high-resolution crop), is a schematic over Years 1-3 with no numeric axis (\"Relative levels\"): a thick, nearly straight rising line A labelled \"Fitness state\"; two wavy lines B and C labelled \"Functional indicators\" that oscillate around A and dip near each year boundary; and a lower, slowly rising wavy line D labelled \"Skill.\" The text assigns B to bi-cyclic and C to mono-cyclic periodisation, and A to \"relatively stable, long-term adaptation over many years.\" D shows that \"adaptation in sport consists not only of increased motor potential, but also the improvement of the athlete's skills to more effectively utilise this potential\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, text layer only).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors read the annual waves (B, C) as \"temporary, unstable and to a certain extent, reversible\" compensatory changes riding on slower stable adaptation (A) (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, text layer only; see [[claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation]]). No athletes, sports, sample sizes or citations are given for the four conclusions; \"Extensive research allows us to reach the following conclusions\" is the only basis stated, hence `supported` at most.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: set each year's expectations from these patterns: speed-strength at the start of a new year should sit below last year's best but above last year's start; a smaller annual gain in an advanced athlete is expected, not a failure. Eric's baseline: \"Preparedness (the athlete's underlying physical capability, built over a long period) is explicitly distinguished from readiness (day-to-day fluctuation)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 64, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: Fig 6.22's A and B/C lines draw that distinction, on a season scale rather than a daily one. Eric's baseline: in football \"you want to peak every Saturday, but definitely throughout the month of November\" (line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: football's one long season is closest to the authors' single-season case, where speed-strength \"rises regularly and uniformly.\"\n\n**When NOT to apply** — The conclusions come from explosive-strength sports; the authors say long-term adaptation in endurance sports has not been observed as clearly (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, text layer only). Figure against text: the text later describes graph A as \"a parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348), but in the image A is close to a straight line over the three years drawn, with no visible peak. Inference: for an athlete whose speed-strength has plateaued across years, conclusion 1 no longer describes them; that is the limit the parabola refers to.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: the \"twin peak\" in speed-strength with two competition stages, the second higher than the first (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation]], [[principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors]], [[framework--logical-scheme-of-training-management-load-fitness-state-external-interactions]], [[claim--strength-and-speed-training-response-shifts-with-training-age]], [[claim--physical-quality-transfer-decreases-as-specific-fitness-increases]]\n","numLines":33,"startLine":1,"totalLines":33}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"315f86de-eff1-4f45-9261-17ec37cd062d","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
