{"parentUuid":"cc967539-880d-450b-bf4e-3bff4cd5ab97","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnrTFw9JS3jWHWsgEXQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XhNKEsnCAW1X2Aokg78WBS","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; f=claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md; ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 - \"$f\" <<'EOF'\nimport sys\np=sys.argv[1]; s=open(p).read()\nreps=[\n(\"(PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image and a high-resolution crop), is a schematic\",\"(PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image and a 400 dpi crop), is a schematic\"),\n(\"a thick, nearly straight rising line A labelled \\\"Fitness state\\\"\",\"a thick rising line A labelled \\\"Fitness state\\\" whose slope shrinks each year (steepest in Year 1, flattest in Year 3)\"),\n(\"text layer only\",\"checked against the page image\"),\n(\"the authors say long-term adaptation in endurance sports has not been observed as clearly (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image). Figure against text: the text later describes graph A as \\\"a parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\\\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348), but in the image A is close to a straight line over the three years drawn, with no visible peak.\",\n \"the authors say long-term adaptation in endurance sports has not been observed as clearly, and that the problem lies \\\"in the ability to observe it in the factors used for analysis\\\": \\\"The measures of aerobic and anaerobic output reflect the general level of special work capacity (i.e. endurance), but they do not express the internal changes associated with adaptation to intense cyclic or acyclic work\\\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image). Inference: an endurance or conditioning test can show that work capacity changed without showing which adaptation produced the change. Graph A flattens year by year, in line with the text's \\\"parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\\\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348); no peak is drawn within the three years.\"),\n]\nfor a,b in reps:\n    n=s.count(a); print(n, a[:50]); assert n>=1\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"text layer\\|parabola\\|slope\" $f","description":"Apply four-observations fixes"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3836,"cache_read_input_tokens":117573,"output_tokens":1062,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":157},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3836},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1062,"cache_read_input_tokens":117573,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3836,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3836,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01XhNKEsnCAW1X2Aokg78WBS":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; f=claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md; ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 - \"$f\" <<'EOF'\nimport sys\np=sys.argv[1]; s=open(p).read()\nreps=[\n(\"(PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image and a high-resolution crop), is a schematic\",\"(PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image and a 400 dpi crop), is a schematic\"),\n(\"a thick, nearly straight rising line A labelled \\\"Fitness state\\\"\",\"a thick rising line A labelled \\\"Fitness state\\\" whose slope shrinks each year (steepest in Year 1, flattest in Year 3)\"),\n(\"text layer only\",\"checked against the page image\"),\n(\"the authors say long-term adaptation in endurance sports has not been observed as clearly (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image). Figure against text: the text later describes graph A as \\\"a parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\\\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348), but in the image A is close to a straight line over the three years drawn, with no visible peak.\",\n \"the authors say long-term adaptation in endurance sports has not been observed as clearly, and that the problem lies \\\"in the ability to observe it in the factors used for analysis\\\": \\\"The measures of aerobic and anaerobic output reflect the general level of special work capacity (i.e. endurance), but they do not express the internal changes associated with adaptation to intense cyclic or acyclic work\\\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image). Inference: an endurance or conditioning test can show that work capacity changed without showing which adaptation produced the change. Graph A flattens year by year, in line with the text's \\\"parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\\\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348); no peak is drawn within the three years.\"),\n]\nfor a,b in reps:\n    n=s.count(a); print(n, a[:50]); assert n>=1\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"text layer\\|parabola\\|slope\" $f","description":"Apply four-observations fixes"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffnrP7Qa3e9FuZGA5PQC","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"9f4e696f-3458-4dcb-9974-107263e9d3b9","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:39:26.236Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"9f4e696f-3458-4dcb-9974-107263e9d3b9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XhNKEsnCAW1X2Aokg78WBS","type":"tool_result","content":"1 (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the \n1 a thick, nearly straight rising line A labelled \"F\n3 text layer only\n1 the authors say long-term adaptation in endurance \n22:Fig 6.22, \"The dynamics of adaptation in sports training over several years (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image and a 400 dpi crop), is a schematic over Years 1-3 with no numeric axis (\"Relative levels\"): a thick rising line A labelled \"Fitness state\" whose slope shrinks each year (steepest in Year 1, flattest in Year 3); two wavy lines B and C labelled \"Functional indicators\" that oscillate around A and dip near each year boundary; and a lower, slowly rising wavy line D labelled \"Skill.\" The text assigns B to bi-cyclic and C to mono-cyclic periodisation, and A to \"relatively stable, long-term adaptation over many years.\" D shows that \"adaptation in sport consists not only of increased motor potential, but also the improvement of the athlete's skills to more effectively utilise this potential\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image).\n28:**When NOT to apply** — The conclusions come from explosive-strength sports; the authors say long-term adaptation in endurance sports has not been observed as clearly, and that the problem lies \"in the ability to observe it in the factors used for analysis\": \"The measures of aerobic and anaerobic output reflect the general level of special work capacity (i.e. endurance), but they do not express the internal changes associated with adaptation to intense cyclic or acyclic work\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image). Inference: an endurance or conditioning test can show that work capacity changed without showing which adaptation produced the change. Graph A flattens year by year, in line with the text's \"parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348); no peak is drawn within the three years. Inference: for an athlete whose speed-strength has plateaued across years, conclusion 1 no longer describes them; that is the limit the parabola refers to.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"1d9da274-4e71-46bb-981c-9fe290bb0156","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:39:26.628Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"1 (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the \n1 a thick, nearly straight rising line A labelled \"F\n3 text layer only\n1 the authors say long-term adaptation in endurance \n22:Fig 6.22, \"The dynamics of adaptation in sports training over several years (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image and a 400 dpi crop), is a schematic over Years 1-3 with no numeric axis (\"Relative levels\"): a thick rising line A labelled \"Fitness state\" whose slope shrinks each year (steepest in Year 1, flattest in Year 3); two wavy lines B and C labelled \"Functional indicators\" that oscillate around A and dip near each year boundary; and a lower, slowly rising wavy line D labelled \"Skill.\" The text assigns B to bi-cyclic and C to mono-cyclic periodisation, and A to \"relatively stable, long-term adaptation over many years.\" D shows that \"adaptation in sport consists not only of increased motor potential, but also the improvement of the athlete's skills to more effectively utilise this potential\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image).\n28:**When NOT to apply** — The conclusions come from explosive-strength sports; the authors say long-term adaptation in endurance sports has not been observed as clearly, and that the problem lies \"in the ability to observe it in the factors used for analysis\": \"The measures of aerobic and anaerobic output reflect the general level of special work capacity (i.e. endurance), but they do not express the internal changes associated with adaptation to intense cyclic or acyclic work\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image). Inference: an endurance or conditioning test can show that work capacity changed without showing which adaptation produced the change. Graph A flattens year by year, in line with the text's \"parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348); no peak is drawn within the three years. Inference: for an athlete whose speed-strength has plateaued across years, conclusion 1 no longer describes them; that is the limit the parabola refers to.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":19,"oldLines":13,"newStart":19,"newLines":13,"lines":[" 3. \"The rise in speed-strength from year to year is larger for athletes of medium qualification than for highly qualified athletes.\""," 4. The annual trend \"is determined by the traditional periodisation of training for the given sport and competition calendar. One can clearly trace a twin peak in the dynamics of speed-strength with two competition stages, because the level of special strength preparedness is higher in the second competition stage than in the first. In types of sports with one competition season, speed-strength rises regularly and uniformly.\""," ","-Fig 6.22, \"The dynamics of adaptation in sports training over several years (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image and a high-resolution crop), is a schematic over Years 1-3 with no numeric axis (\"Relative levels\"): a thick, nearly straight rising line A labelled \"Fitness state\"; two wavy lines B and C labelled \"Functional indicators\" that oscillate around A and dip near each year boundary; and a lower, slowly rising wavy line D labelled \"Skill.\" The text assigns B to bi-cyclic and C to mono-cyclic periodisation, and A to \"relatively stable, long-term adaptation over many years.\" D shows that \"adaptation in sport consists not only of increased motor potential, but also the improvement of the athlete's skills to more effectively utilise this potential\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, text layer only).","+Fig 6.22, \"The dynamics of adaptation in sports training over several years (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347, checked against the page image and a 400 dpi crop), is a schematic over Years 1-3 with no numeric axis (\"Relative levels\"): a thick rising line A labelled \"Fitness state\" whose slope shrinks each year (steepest in Year 1, flattest in Year 3); two wavy lines B and C labelled \"Functional indicators\" that oscillate around A and dip near each year boundary; and a lower, slowly rising wavy line D labelled \"Skill.\" The text assigns B to bi-cyclic and C to mono-cyclic periodisation, and A to \"relatively stable, long-term adaptation over many years.\" D shows that \"adaptation in sport consists not only of increased motor potential, but also the improvement of the athlete's skills to more effectively utilise this potential\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image)."," ","-**Why it works** — The authors read the annual waves (B, C) as \"temporary, unstable and to a certain extent, reversible\" compensatory changes riding on slower stable adaptation (A) (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, text layer only; see [[claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation]]). No athletes, sports, sample sizes or citations are given for the four conclusions; \"Extensive research allows us to reach the following conclusions\" is the only basis stated, hence `supported` at most.","+**Why it works** — The authors read the annual waves (B, C) as \"temporary, unstable and to a certain extent, reversible\" compensatory changes riding on slower stable adaptation (A) (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image; see [[claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation]]). No athletes, sports, sample sizes or citations are given for the four conclusions; \"Extensive research allows us to reach the following conclusions\" is the only basis stated, hence `supported` at most."," "," **When to apply** — Inference: set each year's expectations from these patterns: speed-strength at the start of a new year should sit below last year's best but above last year's start; a smaller annual gain in an advanced athlete is expected, not a failure. Eric's baseline: \"Preparedness (the athlete's underlying physical capability, built over a long period) is explicitly distinguished from readiness (day-to-day fluctuation)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 64, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: Fig 6.22's A and B/C lines draw that distinction, on a season scale rather than a daily one. Eric's baseline: in football \"you want to peak every Saturday, but definitely throughout the month of November\" (line 185, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q14)). Inference: football's one long season is closest to the authors' single-season case, where speed-strength \"rises regularly and uniformly.\""," ","-**When NOT to apply** — The conclusions come from explosive-strength sports; the authors say long-term adaptation in endurance sports has not been observed as clearly (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, text layer only). Figure against text: the text later describes graph A as \"a parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348), but in the image A is close to a straight line over the three years drawn, with no visible peak. Inference: for an athlete whose speed-strength has plateaued across years, conclusion 1 no longer describes them; that is the limit the parabola refers to.","+**When NOT to apply** — The conclusions come from explosive-strength sports; the authors say long-term adaptation in endurance sports has not been observed as clearly, and that the problem lies \"in the ability to observe it in the factors used for analysis\": \"The measures of aerobic and anaerobic output reflect the general level of special work capacity (i.e. endurance), but they do not express the internal changes associated with adaptation to intense cyclic or acyclic work\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image). Inference: an endurance or conditioning test can show that work capacity changed without showing which adaptation produced the change. Graph A flattens year by year, in line with the text's \"parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348); no peak is drawn within the three years. Inference: for an athlete whose speed-strength has plateaued across years, conclusion 1 no longer describes them; that is the limit the parabola refers to."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: the \"twin peak\" in speed-strength with two competition stages, the second higher than the first (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347)."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9f4e696f-3458-4dcb-9974-107263e9d3b9","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
