{"parentUuid":"20567f1a-4b10-412c-8662-9570d2b03fda","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnsDLJZp8jwN6xSogSU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012RcJXwhK53i2yB1VwBsSRM","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1827,"cache_read_input_tokens":121409,"output_tokens":251,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":123},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1827},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":251,"cache_read_input_tokens":121409,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1827,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1827,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_012RcJXwhK53i2yB1VwBsSRM":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffns7ekSR9KJwY4ibV78","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"25709a6d-293a-421c-b3c7-68194b42818a","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:39:30.800Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"25709a6d-293a-421c-b3c7-68194b42818a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012RcJXwhK53i2yB1VwBsSRM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttype: library-entry\n3\tkind: claim\n4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n6\tchapter: \"6.8.1\"\n7\ttopic: compensatory and long-term adaptation\n8\tcategory: [adaptation, long-term-development, theory, overtraining]\n9\tconfidence: supported\n10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Compensatory Adaptation Is Temporary and Comes First; Stable Adaptation Builds on It, and Has a Limit\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — The authors split adaptation into two layers (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348; text layer only for this page, the figure it refers to was checked on PDF p. 358):\n17\t- **Compensatory adaptation.** The annual changes in functional indicators (Fig 6.22, graphs B and C) \"are temporary, unstable and to a certain extent, reversible.\" Their basis is \"compensatory mechanisms, i.e. reactions designed to maintain the necessary level of functioning for brief periods under demanding conditions which ultimately lead to stable adaptation.\" These \"gradually fade as adaptation takes place,\" and \"Insofar as these mechanisms precede adaptation, they should be designated pre-adaptation phenomena (Kuznyetsov, 1979).\"\n18\t- **Long-term, stable adaptation** (graph A). \"compensatory adaptation is a prerequisite condition for the development of long-term, stable adaptation,\" which \"can progressively increase if the functional reconstruction during the course of compensatory adaptation is sufficient to produce a noticeable effect on the external interactions of the body and increase its special work capacity.\"\n19\t\n20\tThree further statements on the same page:\n21\t1. \"adaptation cannot be considered an equilibrium process. Adaptation is the dynamic maintenance of a definite level of imbalance between the body and the environment\" (Bauer, 1935; Bernstein, 1966).\n22\t2. The systems involved improve at different rates. This heterochronicity \"is determined by: the action of particular physiological systems in establishing long-term adaptation of a specific emphasis; differences in their reactiveness (or adaptive inertia); changes in the role of these systems at different stages of long-term adaptation.\"\n23\t3. \"Long-term compensatory adaptation cannot continue indefinitely.\" Its effect \"diminishes with each repetition,\" described \"by a parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time (Fig 6.22, graph A). This indicates that an athlete's general adaptation reserves have a limit that is genetically predetermined.\" The limit shows as \"waves in the dynamics of special work capacity (graphs B and C),\" caused partly by \"traditional periodisation\" but \"a more important reason is the exhaustion of the so-called current adaptation reserves (CAR) of the body.\"\n24\t\n25\t**Why it works** — The authors' account is the one above: short-term compensations hold function under stress, and if they change performance enough, the body consolidates them. They state that the picture is clearest in explosive-strength sports and that endurance adaptation \"has to be taken much further,\" assuming \"the same, above-mentioned chief mechanisms, although at another level of the physiological systems\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348). Inference: the \"genetically predetermined\" limit is inferred from the shape of the curve, not from genetic data.\n26\t\n27\t**When to apply** — Inference: treat a season's gains in functional indicators as provisional until they hold through a lower-load period; a quality that survives the transition phase has become stable adaptation in the authors' terms. When a block stops producing gains, the authors' model points to exhausted current reserves before it points to a genetic ceiling (see [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]]).\n28\t\n29\t**When NOT to apply** — Page 359 was read from the text layer only. No data are given for the parabola or the genetic limit, and Fig 6.22's graph A looks nearly straight over the three years drawn, not parabolic. The \"imbalance\" definition is a theoretical position (Bauer, Bernstein), not a measured one. Inference: it is closer to the allostasis model in Evans's course material (regulation that changes set points to meet demand; see Related) than to fixed-set-point homeostasis.\n30\t\n31\t**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 6.22, where the functional indicators B and C wave above and below the steadily rising fitness state A within each year (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\n32\t\n33\t**Related** — [[claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation]], [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[claim--heterochronic-biochemical-adaptation-sequence]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[claim--speed-barrier-is-genetic-ceiling-not-neural-block]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--homeostasis-vs-allostasis-mcewen-sterling]]\n34\t"}]},"uuid":"6696f58b-cf2e-4daf-8f6d-f50f9b9ae814","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:39:30.978Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.8.1\"\ntopic: compensatory and long-term adaptation\ncategory: [adaptation, long-term-development, theory, overtraining]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Compensatory Adaptation Is Temporary and Comes First; Stable Adaptation Builds on It, and Has a Limit\n\n**What it is** — The authors split adaptation into two layers (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348; text layer only for this page, the figure it refers to was checked on PDF p. 358):\n- **Compensatory adaptation.** The annual changes in functional indicators (Fig 6.22, graphs B and C) \"are temporary, unstable and to a certain extent, reversible.\" Their basis is \"compensatory mechanisms, i.e. reactions designed to maintain the necessary level of functioning for brief periods under demanding conditions which ultimately lead to stable adaptation.\" These \"gradually fade as adaptation takes place,\" and \"Insofar as these mechanisms precede adaptation, they should be designated pre-adaptation phenomena (Kuznyetsov, 1979).\"\n- **Long-term, stable adaptation** (graph A). \"compensatory adaptation is a prerequisite condition for the development of long-term, stable adaptation,\" which \"can progressively increase if the functional reconstruction during the course of compensatory adaptation is sufficient to produce a noticeable effect on the external interactions of the body and increase its special work capacity.\"\n\nThree further statements on the same page:\n1. \"adaptation cannot be considered an equilibrium process. Adaptation is the dynamic maintenance of a definite level of imbalance between the body and the environment\" (Bauer, 1935; Bernstein, 1966).\n2. The systems involved improve at different rates. This heterochronicity \"is determined by: the action of particular physiological systems in establishing long-term adaptation of a specific emphasis; differences in their reactiveness (or adaptive inertia); changes in the role of these systems at different stages of long-term adaptation.\"\n3. \"Long-term compensatory adaptation cannot continue indefinitely.\" Its effect \"diminishes with each repetition,\" described \"by a parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time (Fig 6.22, graph A). This indicates that an athlete's general adaptation reserves have a limit that is genetically predetermined.\" The limit shows as \"waves in the dynamics of special work capacity (graphs B and C),\" caused partly by \"traditional periodisation\" but \"a more important reason is the exhaustion of the so-called current adaptation reserves (CAR) of the body.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' account is the one above: short-term compensations hold function under stress, and if they change performance enough, the body consolidates them. They state that the picture is clearest in explosive-strength sports and that endurance adaptation \"has to be taken much further,\" assuming \"the same, above-mentioned chief mechanisms, although at another level of the physiological systems\" (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348). Inference: the \"genetically predetermined\" limit is inferred from the shape of the curve, not from genetic data.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: treat a season's gains in functional indicators as provisional until they hold through a lower-load period; a quality that survives the transition phase has become stable adaptation in the authors' terms. When a block stops producing gains, the authors' model points to exhausted current reserves before it points to a genetic ceiling (see [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]]).\n\n**When NOT to apply** — Page 359 was read from the text layer only. No data are given for the parabola or the genetic limit, and Fig 6.22's graph A looks nearly straight over the three years drawn, not parabolic. The \"imbalance\" definition is a theoretical position (Bauer, Bernstein), not a measured one. Inference: it is closer to the allostasis model in Evans's course material (regulation that changes set points to meet demand; see Related) than to fixed-set-point homeostasis.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 6.22, where the functional indicators B and C wave above and below the steadily rising fitness state A within each year (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\n\n**Related** — [[claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation]], [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[claim--heterochronic-biochemical-adaptation-sequence]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[claim--speed-barrier-is-genetic-ceiling-not-neural-block]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--homeostasis-vs-allostasis-mcewen-sterling]]\n","numLines":34,"startLine":1,"totalLines":34}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"25709a6d-293a-421c-b3c7-68194b42818a","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
