{"parentUuid":"e405dfea-64aa-4c13-9b4f-c5abd476576e","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffntQrPXTJkRkr1dLaUv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01414BpxGzEXkJwNTDim1xUp","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"f=$(find /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library -name \"principle--progressive-overload-threshold.md\" | head -1); echo $f; grep -n -i \"plateau\\|standard\\|below\\|maladapt\\|threshold\" \"$f\" | head -12","description":"Check Bompa threshold entry wording"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1926,"cache_read_input_tokens":126148,"output_tokens":238,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":41},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1926},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":238,"cache_read_input_tokens":126148,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1926,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1926,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01414BpxGzEXkJwNTDim1xUp":{"command":"f=$(find /Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library -name \"principle--progressive-overload-threshold.md\" | head -1); echo $f; grep -n -i \"plateau\\|standard\\|below\\|maladapt\\|threshold\" \"$f\" | head -12","description":"Check Bompa threshold entry wording"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffntLD5t5xbEkPwN4ric","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4449afe8-7085-4990-aa83-51df4b2b6d61","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:39:46.717Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"4449afe8-7085-4990-aa83-51df4b2b6d61","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"c0dcb81c-1a93-418b-82a5-bbfe558c5d2b","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01414BpxGzEXkJwNTDim1xUp","type":"tool_result","content":"/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/periodization--bompa/principle--progressive-overload-threshold.md\n14:# Progressive Overload and the Threshold of Adaptation\n16:**What it is** — Adaptation follows a load-dependent sequence with three outcomes: increasing, varied stimulus ⇒ adaptation ⇒ performance improvement; a *standard* (unchanging) load ⇒ plateau ⇒ stagnation; an *excessive* load ⇒ maladaptation ⇒ decline (overreaching/overtraining). Improvement requires systematically crossing the current \"threshold of adaptation\" by raising intensity, volume, or frequency — then doing so again once the body has adapted.\n18:**Why it works** — Training is beneficial only insofar as it overloads the system enough to demand adaptation. A stimulus below threshold provokes no change; a sustained intolerable stimulus injures or overtrains. Bompa stresses that overload must be paired with **variation** to keep maximizing adaptation rather than habituating.\n20:**When to apply** — The default logic for advancing any program; diagnosing a plateau (\"is the load still progressing, or has it gone standard?\"); justifying planned increases over a block.\n22:**When NOT to apply** — Not a license for linear, monotonic load increase indefinitely — that path leads to the maladaptation branch. The *rate* and *form* of progression must respect recovery and training age. Overload without adequate recovery is how you cross into overreaching. Pairs with [[principle--supercompensation-cycle]] (recovery side) and is bounded by [[heuristic--fatigue-fitness-3to1-ratio]].\n24:**Example** — A strength block uses loads >80% 1RM and nudges intensity/volume upward each microcycle; when testing shows a plateau, the coach changes the stimulus (exercise, tempo, rep scheme) rather than simply repeating the same successful load.\n26:**Related** — [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]]: gives numeric thresholds (Hettinger, Korobkov) for the same threshold-stimulus idea","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"04948f3e-64e1-44f6-8428-46e176ff1348","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:39:46.966Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/strength-and-conditioning/periodization--bompa/principle--progressive-overload-threshold.md\n14:# Progressive Overload and the Threshold of Adaptation\n16:**What it is** — Adaptation follows a load-dependent sequence with three outcomes: increasing, varied stimulus ⇒ adaptation ⇒ performance improvement; a *standard* (unchanging) load ⇒ plateau ⇒ stagnation; an *excessive* load ⇒ maladaptation ⇒ decline (overreaching/overtraining). Improvement requires systematically crossing the current \"threshold of adaptation\" by raising intensity, volume, or frequency — then doing so again once the body has adapted.\n18:**Why it works** — Training is beneficial only insofar as it overloads the system enough to demand adaptation. A stimulus below threshold provokes no change; a sustained intolerable stimulus injures or overtrains. Bompa stresses that overload must be paired with **variation** to keep maximizing adaptation rather than habituating.\n20:**When to apply** — The default logic for advancing any program; diagnosing a plateau (\"is the load still progressing, or has it gone standard?\"); justifying planned increases over a block.\n22:**When NOT to apply** — Not a license for linear, monotonic load increase indefinitely — that path leads to the maladaptation branch. The *rate* and *form* of progression must respect recovery and training age. Overload without adequate recovery is how you cross into overreaching. Pairs with [[principle--supercompensation-cycle]] (recovery side) and is bounded by [[heuristic--fatigue-fitness-3to1-ratio]].\n24:**Example** — A strength block uses loads >80% 1RM and nudges intensity/volume upward each microcycle; when testing shows a plateau, the coach changes the stimulus (exercise, tempo, rep scheme) rather than simply repeating the same successful load.\n26:**Related** — [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--strength-training-threshold-stimulus]]: gives numeric thresholds (Hettinger, Korobkov) for the same threshold-stimulus idea","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4449afe8-7085-4990-aa83-51df4b2b6d61","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
