odel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"cfdacec2-9dcf-43b2-970a-b5742634975f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TczNFdoSdCXRiMpGrThpkt","type":"tool_result","content":"1 (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, text layer only)\n1 states the same three outcomes (below threshold, p\n1 [[periodization--bompa/principle--progressive-over\n# Volume Drives Long-Term Adaptation and Prepares Connective Tissue; Intensify Only After Extensive Low-Intensity Work\n\n**What it is** — The authors give volume two roles. First: \"The function of the volume of the load consists chiefly of the systematic and prolonged disturbance of the body's homeostasis, which stimulates the mobilisation of its energy resources and plastic reserves. This is the fundamental condition for switching from the acute (specific) reaction ... to the general (non-specific) adaptive reactio\n\nVolume alone is not enough: \"the influence of the volume can be determined correctly only if one also takes into consideration the magnitude of the loading, its duration and intensity\" (PDF p. 369). Magnitude is set \"first of all, on the basis of the athlete's qualification\": the higher the qualification, the larger the yearly and stage volume, and the share of \"specialised loading\" grows (Panov 1","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"510f5c12-f6e7-42f6-9e50-06835c755f67","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:39:55.215Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"1 (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, text layer only)\n1 states the same three outcomes (below threshold, p\n1 [[periodization--bompa/principle--progressive-over\n# Volume Drives Long-Term Adaptation and Prepares Connective Tissue; Intensify Only After Extensive Low-Intensity Work\n\n**What it is** — The authors give volume two roles. First: \"The function of the volume of the load consists chiefly of the systematic and prolonged disturbance of the body's homeostasis, which stimulates the mobilisation of its energy resources and plastic reserves. This is the fundamental condition for switching from the acute (specific) reaction ... to the general (non-specific) adaptive reactio\n\nVolume alone is not enough: \"the influence of the volume can be determined correctly only if one also takes into consideration the magnitude of the loading, its duration and intensity\" (PDF p. 369). Magnitude is set \"first of all, on the basis of the athlete's qualification\": the higher the qualification, the larger the yearly and stage volume, and the share of \"specialised loading\" grows (Panov 1","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":13,"oldLines":13,"newStart":13,"newLines":13,"lines":[" "," # Load Must Sit Inside the Current Adaptation Reserve Window: Too Little Wastes It, Too Much Exhausts It"," ","-**What it is** — The authors make the current adaptation reserves (CAR) a programming rule (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, text layer only): \"At any instant, the body has a definite reserve potential, i.e. a capability of responding to adapt to external influences and raise itself to a new functional level,\" limited by \"definite physiological mechanisms.\" Hence \"the intensity, volume and duration of the training influences determine the optimal development of the body's CAR. If the magnitude of these are below certain thresholds, the body will not fully utilise its CAR, whereas, if they exceed it, exhaustion of reserve potential will occur and the training effect will be low or negative.\"","+**What it is** — The authors make the current adaptation reserves (CAR) a programming rule (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348, checked against the page image): \"At any instant, the body has a definite reserve potential, i.e. a capability of responding to adapt to external influences and raise itself to a new functional level,\" limited by \"definite physiological mechanisms.\" Hence \"the intensity, volume and duration of the training influences determine the optimal development of the body's CAR. If the magnitude of these are below certain thresholds, the body will not fully utilise its CAR, whereas, if they exceed it, exhaustion of reserve potential will occur and the training effect will be low or negative.\""," "," The goal follows: \"one can consider training organisation to be effective when it produces the maximum growth of the body's CAR by means of the appropriate volume of loading.\" Useful programming therefore needs \"means of assessing the body's CAR in each case, together with criteria for determining the contents and volume of the training loads,\" and a quantitative approach \"is based, in particular, on assessments of a number of bio-chemical indicators of energy exchange processes (Beckman et al, 1961; Yakovlev, 1977)\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349, checked against the page image)."," "," The authors place this inside the older three-phase schemes of form: \"(a) the growth of trainability, (b) sporting form, (c) loss of trainability (Letunov, 1952)\"; \"(a) adaptation, (b) the highest sport work-capacity, (c) re-adaptation (Prokor, 1959),\" where re-adaptation is \"decrease in work capacity as the limits of adaptability are exceeded\" (PDF pp. 359-360 / printed pp. 348-349); and Matveyev's \"acquisition, preservation and brief loss of sporting form (Matveyev, 1964)\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). \"Prokor\" is the book's spelling."," ","-**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is a threshold model: a reserve with a fixed size at any moment is under-used by small loads and depleted by large ones. They give no measured threshold. Inference: Bompa's threshold-of-adaptation entry (Related) states the same three outcomes (below threshold, plateau; above it, maladaptation) at the level of single loads; the authors apply it to a whole training phase.","+**Why it works** — The authors' reasoning is a threshold model: a reserve with a fixed size at any moment is under-used by small loads and depleted by large ones. They give no measured threshold. Inference: Bompa's threshold-of-adaptation entry (Related) sets out three outcomes of loading (an increasing, varied load, adaptation; an unchanging load, plateau; an excessive one, maladaptation) at the level of single loads; the authors apply it to a whole training phase."," "," **When to apply** — Inference: when a block produces no gain, ask first whether the load was below threshold (too little) or beyond it (exhaustion); the remedy is opposite in the two cases. The authors' wave signal (see Related) is one way to tell: waves in special work capacity mark the reserve running out. Eric's baseline: the mRSI decision read uses individualized thresholds, \"Percent change from baseline or a rolling average. We've used standard deviations and SWC to diagnose signal versus noise\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 75, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q2, Q2a)). Inference: that is a practical, non-biochemical stand-in for the \"means of assessing the body's CAR\" the authors ask for, read at the weekly scale."," "]},{"oldStart":27,"oldLines":4,"newStart":27,"newLines":4,"lines":[" "," **Example** — The authors' own application follows on the same page: the durations of continuous and concentrated loading blocks that they say maximally enhance the CAR (see [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]])."," ","-**Related** — [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation]], [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--verkhoshansky-activation-levels-and-threshold-of-adaptability]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--progressive-overload-threshold]]","+**Related** — [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation]], [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--verkhoshansky-activation-levels-and-threshold-of-adaptability]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--progressive-overload-threshold]], [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]] (6.9.4, loading volume as the disturbance that uses the reserve)"]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cfdacec2-9dcf-43b2-970a-b5742634975f","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
