{"parentUuid":"3b82bd0e-635c-4330-a0a9-456a31177bb1","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffnuhr7iwbvtMaRyDTfH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now the distributed-vs-concentrated entry and pages 360-361."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":386,"cache_read_input_tokens":130233,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":386,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":42,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffnud6cAKD6Rk3Hq4kvy","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"f3aa3533-c572-4a87-b3b3-efa3a1ef28dd","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:40:02.845Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"f3aa3533-c572-4a87-b3b3-efa3a1ef28dd","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffnuhr7iwbvtMaRyDTfH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WQvnnU3zuQFZyB9hev8jHb","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":386,"cache_read_input_tokens":130233,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":386,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":42,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01WQvnnU3zuQFZyB9hev8jHb":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffnud6cAKD6Rk3Hq4kvy","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"435c5f90-8cab-4cfd-ab30-9a6dc8e99fd0","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:40:03.778Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"435c5f90-8cab-4cfd-ab30-9a6dc8e99fd0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WQvnnU3zuQFZyB9hev8jHb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttype: library-entry\n3\tkind: framework\n4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n6\tchapter: \"6.8.1\"\n7\ttopic: distributed vs concentrated loading\n8\tcategory: [periodisation, concentrated-loading, speed-strength, programming]\n9\tconfidence: contested\n10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Two Forms of Compensatory Adaptation: Distributed Loading Rises Gradually; Concentrated Loading Dips First, Then Rises Higher\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — \"Two forms of compensatory adaptation, associated with enhancing the CAR by systematic training, which differ in volume and organisation of the training loads, have been identified and studied in explosive sports\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349, checked against the page image):\n17\t- **(A) Distributed loading:** \"a gradual increase in the functional indicators,\" with \"a moderate volume of continuous loading.\" \"periodic brief disturbances of the body's homeostasis occur and the current expenditure of energy resources is compensated during training.\" It \"follows traditional concepts for developing trainability and is typical for athletes of medium classification.\"\n18\t- **(B) Concentrated loading:** \"a large, concentrated volume of special physical preparation means, concentrated in the first half of the training phase. These large volumes provoke a profound and prolonged disturbance of the body's homeostasis, which is reflected by decrease of the functional indicators. Then, after the volume of loading is decreased, their increase accelerates and exceeds the level achieved with the first form of adaptation. This way of organising loading in the preparatory mesocycles was devised for highly qualified athletes and has been shown by research to be effective for all types of sport (Verkhoshansky, 1977).\"\n19\t\n20\tFig 6.23, \"Two forms of compensatory adaptation during the large preparation phase (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350, checked against the page image and high-resolution crops of both panels), plots % change (100 = start) over 18 weeks, with dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12. Values read by eye, all \"about\":\n21\t- **Panel A (distributed):** every curve rises from week 0. At week 18, starting strength about 144%, explosive strength about 128%; the three lower curves end at about 114%, 108% and 105%, matching the right-hand labels maximum strength, absolute strength and speed by end position. By that reading, speed peaks at about 113% near week 9 and then declines.\n22\t- **Panel B (concentrated):** every curve falls first, to lows between about 83% and about 96% around weeks 4-6, crosses back above 100% around weeks 7-9, and ends at week 18 at about 157% (starting strength), 143% (explosive strength), 129% (maximum strength, after a peak of about 131% near week 16), 120% (speed) and 114% (absolute strength). The page does not let one tell which quality dips deepest.\n23\t\n24\tThe authors' further claim: \"the more extensive and longer the exhaustion of the body's energy resources by concentrated loading during specific mesocycles of training (obviously within reasonable limits), the higher their subsequent super-restoration and the longer the new functional level is maintained\" (Verkhoshansky, 1977). \"The key issue, of course, is determining the optimal duration of concentrated loading, so as to avoid exhaustion and diminished performance\" (PDF pp. 360-361 / printed pp. 349-350).\n25\t\n26\tOn critics: Komarova (1984) \"correctly recognises that premature or prolonged application of concentrated loading can lead to overtraining, stagnation or injury,\" but \"errs in extrapolating this analysis to completely dismiss the periodic use of concentrated loading by qualified athletes\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349).\n27\t\n28\t**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the compensatory sequence: a large, prolonged homeostatic disturbance depresses function, and when volume drops the compensatory reaction overshoots. They cite Verkhoshansky (1977) and give no sample or data beyond Fig 6.23, which carries no study label.\n29\t\n30\t**When to apply** — Inference: concentrated loading is the authors' choice for highly qualified athletes in preparatory mesocycles with time to absorb a dip of several weeks; distributed loading for medium-level athletes or when performance must hold. Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the authors' split by qualification sits alongside that baseline split by strength level.\n31\t\n32\t**When NOT to apply** — **Contested across the library:** the authors say concentrated loading \"has been shown by research to be effective for all types of sport\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). Issurin (2016) judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, noting fitness gains without reported effects on team practice or competition (p. 6), and placed the model as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality (p. 11) ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). Figure against text: the figure's depressed phase (below 100% until about weeks 7-9) runs longer than the 3-4 week concentrated block the authors give on the same page, and its x-axis ends at 18 weeks while the text's period is 18-22 weeks (see [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]]). Inference: the \"deeper exhaustion, higher rebound\" claim sits on the same pages as the Bondarchuk result the authors read as \"calculations of volume are not as critical as those of intensity\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350); the authors do not reconcile the two (see [[claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect]]). Not for novices or medium-level athletes, by the authors' own scope.\n33\t\n34\t**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 6.23, where after concentrated loading starting strength ends at about 157% against about 144% with distributed loading over the same 18 weeks (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350).\n35\t\n36\t**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]], [[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\n37\t"}]},"uuid":"83a09d83-aaeb-480a-92d6-09cddd81bc39","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:40:03.967Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: framework\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.8.1\"\ntopic: distributed vs concentrated loading\ncategory: [periodisation, concentrated-loading, speed-strength, programming]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Two Forms of Compensatory Adaptation: Distributed Loading Rises Gradually; Concentrated Loading Dips First, Then Rises Higher\n\n**What it is** — \"Two forms of compensatory adaptation, associated with enhancing the CAR by systematic training, which differ in volume and organisation of the training loads, have been identified and studied in explosive sports\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349, checked against the page image):\n- **(A) Distributed loading:** \"a gradual increase in the functional indicators,\" with \"a moderate volume of continuous loading.\" \"periodic brief disturbances of the body's homeostasis occur and the current expenditure of energy resources is compensated during training.\" It \"follows traditional concepts for developing trainability and is typical for athletes of medium classification.\"\n- **(B) Concentrated loading:** \"a large, concentrated volume of special physical preparation means, concentrated in the first half of the training phase. These large volumes provoke a profound and prolonged disturbance of the body's homeostasis, which is reflected by decrease of the functional indicators. Then, after the volume of loading is decreased, their increase accelerates and exceeds the level achieved with the first form of adaptation. This way of organising loading in the preparatory mesocycles was devised for highly qualified athletes and has been shown by research to be effective for all types of sport (Verkhoshansky, 1977).\"\n\nFig 6.23, \"Two forms of compensatory adaptation during the large preparation phase (Siff & Verkhoshansky, 1999)\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350, checked against the page image and high-resolution crops of both panels), plots % change (100 = start) over 18 weeks, with dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12. Values read by eye, all \"about\":\n- **Panel A (distributed):** every curve rises from week 0. At week 18, starting strength about 144%, explosive strength about 128%; the three lower curves end at about 114%, 108% and 105%, matching the right-hand labels maximum strength, absolute strength and speed by end position. By that reading, speed peaks at about 113% near week 9 and then declines.\n- **Panel B (concentrated):** every curve falls first, to lows between about 83% and about 96% around weeks 4-6, crosses back above 100% around weeks 7-9, and ends at week 18 at about 157% (starting strength), 143% (explosive strength), 129% (maximum strength, after a peak of about 131% near week 16), 120% (speed) and 114% (absolute strength). The page does not let one tell which quality dips deepest.\n\nThe authors' further claim: \"the more extensive and longer the exhaustion of the body's energy resources by concentrated loading during specific mesocycles of training (obviously within reasonable limits), the higher their subsequent super-restoration and the longer the new functional level is maintained\" (Verkhoshansky, 1977). \"The key issue, of course, is determining the optimal duration of concentrated loading, so as to avoid exhaustion and diminished performance\" (PDF pp. 360-361 / printed pp. 349-350).\n\nOn critics: Komarova (1984) \"correctly recognises that premature or prolonged application of concentrated loading can lead to overtraining, stagnation or injury,\" but \"errs in extrapolating this analysis to completely dismiss the periodic use of concentrated loading by qualified athletes\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' mechanism is the compensatory sequence: a large, prolonged homeostatic disturbance depresses function, and when volume drops the compensatory reaction overshoots. They cite Verkhoshansky (1977) and give no sample or data beyond Fig 6.23, which carries no study label.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: concentrated loading is the authors' choice for highly qualified athletes in preparatory mesocycles with time to absorb a dip of several weeks; distributed loading for medium-level athletes or when performance must hold. Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the authors' split by qualification sits alongside that baseline split by strength level.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested across the library:** the authors say concentrated loading \"has been shown by research to be effective for all types of sport\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). Issurin (2016) judged the concentrated-unidirectional evidence insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, noting fitness gains without reported effects on team practice or competition (p. 6), and placed the model as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality (p. 11) ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). Figure against text: the figure's depressed phase (below 100% until about weeks 7-9) runs longer than the 3-4 week concentrated block the authors give on the same page, and its x-axis ends at 18 weeks while the text's period is 18-22 weeks (see [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]]). Inference: the \"deeper exhaustion, higher rebound\" claim sits on the same pages as the Bondarchuk result the authors read as \"calculations of volume are not as critical as those of intensity\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350); the authors do not reconcile the two (see [[claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect]]). Not for novices or medium-level athletes, by the authors' own scope.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 6.23, where after concentrated loading starting strength ends at about 157% against about 144% with distributed loading over the same 18 weeks (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]], [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]], [[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors]], [[periodization--bompa/principle--concentrated-loading-overreaching]], [[block-periodization--issurin/principle--concentration-of-training-workloads]], [[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]\n","numLines":37,"startLine":1,"totalLines":37}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"435c5f90-8cab-4cfd-ab30-9a6dc8e99fd0","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
