{"parentUuid":"4e00402e-32e5-4ecf-898e-1b81d65850ea","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015RFmUitwNT77pzoRnVfEW5","type":"tool_result","content":"590:3. The rise in speed-strength from year to year is larger for athletes of medium qualification than for highly \n889:on the athlete's level of qualification. Research indicates that both methods arc successful in training athletes of \n890:medium qualification. \n874:######## PDF 371\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md:51:- Whose model is whose: the concentrated-unidirectional (CU) model is Verkhoshansky's; the multi-targeted model with 28-study support is Issurin's own A-T-R block model. [pp. 6-7, 11] So this review does not dispute Issurin's 2008 book; it repeats the book's critique of traditional planning. The real 2008-2016 difference is on lower-level athletes: the book (p. 33) says they do fine with medium concentration, while this review says \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated block workloads. [p. 11] (Clarified 2026-09-27 during the Flint review of Issurin's book, checked against the paper pages.)\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md:77:- [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]: Supertraining 5.3.3's system of successive concentrated blocks; it cites this review's limits for team, combat and swimming preparation as the case against using that system there","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"ce7a6a32-558b-444e-aefb-95e533548741","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:40:11.154Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"590:3. The rise in speed-strength from year to year is larger for athletes of medium qualification than for highly \n889:on the athlete's level of qualification. Research indicates that both methods arc successful in training athletes of \n890:medium qualification. \n874:######## PDF 371\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md:51:- Whose model is whose: the concentrated-unidirectional (CU) model is Verkhoshansky's; the multi-targeted model with 28-study support is Issurin's own A-T-R block model. [pp. 6-7, 11] So this review does not dispute Issurin's 2008 book; it repeats the book's critique of traditional planning. The real 2008-2016 difference is on lower-level athletes: the book (p. 33) says they do fine with medium concentration, while this review says \"no evidence that amateur athletes have any serious restrictions\" with highly concentrated block workloads. [p. 11] (Clarified 2026-09-27 during the Flint review of Issurin's book, checked against the paper pages.)\n/Users/kristinarash/SecondBrain/Knowledge/library/research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a.md:77:- [[supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--conjugate-sequence-system-of-training-means]]: Supertraining 5.3.3's system of successive concentrated blocks; it cites this review's limits for team, combat and swimming preparation as the case against using that system there","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4e00402e-32e5-4ecf-898e-1b81d65850ea","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
