{"parentUuid":"a827e260-2a50-431a-93ae-e031038764aa","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffnx52t1cEmXJcNXcqEL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SqT2M4NuHG2GBQsi3y3Wbs","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1445,"cache_read_input_tokens":142563,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1445,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01SqT2M4NuHG2GBQsi3y3Wbs":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffnwtHjC6zE2MvVAcnvN","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"17c9aef2-61ee-436c-946e-0b0925e8e18e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:40:36.479Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"17c9aef2-61ee-436c-946e-0b0925e8e18e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SqT2M4NuHG2GBQsi3y3Wbs","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttype: library-entry\n3\tkind: heuristic\n4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n6\tchapter: \"6.8.1\"\n7\ttopic: loading block duration and sequence\n8\tcategory: [periodisation, concentrated-loading, programming, overtraining]\n9\tconfidence: contested\n10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Block Durations for Enhancing the Adaptation Reserve: 5-6 Weeks Continuous or 3-4 Weeks Concentrated, Three Blocks With 7-10 Day Pauses, Then a Longer Recovery\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — The authors give the durations \"vital for maximal enhancement of the body's CAR\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349, checked against the page image):\n17\t1. \"the appropriate duration of continuous training is 5-6 weeks, and with concentrated loading, the duration is 3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary for activating the compensatory processes.\"\n18\t2. \"the trained athlete is able to tolerate three such sequential blocks of loading, separated by short recuperation pauses (7-10 days). After this, a longer recuperation period is necessary to facilitate stabilisation at the new level of adaptation.\"\n19\t3. \"Thus, the time interval necessary for exhausting the body's CAR is between 18-22 weeks (see Fig 6.23). This period coincides with observations of highly qualified athletes training in different sports, including cyclic types (Zhikharevich, 1976; Sirenko, 1980).\"\n20\t4. \"the body cannot indefinitely continue to adapt successively to training loads,\" and \"it is inappropriate to subject the body to extreme exhaustion of its energy reserves. This requires a longer recuperation period and has a negative effect on the subsequent stages of adaptation.\"\n21\t\n22\t**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the CAR model: each block draws on a limited reserve, the recuperation pause lets the compensatory reaction occur, and after about three blocks the reserve is spent (see [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]]). No data for the durations are shown beyond the two citations for the 18-22 week period.\n23\t\n24\t**When to apply** — Inference: sketching an off-season or preparatory phase for a trained athlete: up to three loading blocks, each followed by a 7-10 day lighter pause, then a longer recovery before the next phase. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the 18-22 week ceiling is a reason to place a longer recovery period on that roadmap rather than run loading blocks back to back.\n25\t\n26\t**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (concentrated block duration).** Here a concentrated block lasts \"3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). In 5.3.3 the book says \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective\" and refers to \"4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; wording confirmed in the text layer, and verified against the image in [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]). In 6.12.2 the method concentrates \"a high volume of unidirectional loading for a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, text layer only). Inference: one reading reconciles them (three 3-4 week blocks give about 9-12 weeks of unidirectional emphasis), but the book does not say so. **Unclear arithmetic:** three 5-6 week continuous blocks plus two 7-10 day pauses come to about 17-21 weeks; three 3-4 week concentrated blocks plus two pauses come to about 11-15 weeks. The text does not say which form the 18-22 weeks refers to, and Fig 6.23, which it cites, spans only 18 weeks. Inference: the 18-22 week figure fits the continuous form better. The authors do not say how the pause length or the \"longer recuperation period\" should be set beyond these figures.\n27\t\n28\t**Example** — The authors give the rule, not a worked plan. Inference: a January-to-May preparatory phase of three 4-week concentrated blocks, each followed by a 7-10 day lighter week, then a longer recovery period before summer training (the authors give no length for it).\n29\t\n30\t**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-mesocycle-duration-and-monitoring]] (Issurin: accumulation blocks up to about six weeks, with gains falling sharply after that), [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--bondarchuk-hammer-throw-block-mesocycles]] (Bondarchuk's four-week blocks with two-week restorative blocks)\n31\t"}]},"uuid":"36ba5db6-1a4c-4dc3-a4d4-3531a0da74a5","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:40:36.651Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: heuristic\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.8.1\"\ntopic: loading block duration and sequence\ncategory: [periodisation, concentrated-loading, programming, overtraining]\nconfidence: contested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Block Durations for Enhancing the Adaptation Reserve: 5-6 Weeks Continuous or 3-4 Weeks Concentrated, Three Blocks With 7-10 Day Pauses, Then a Longer Recovery\n\n**What it is** — The authors give the durations \"vital for maximal enhancement of the body's CAR\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349, checked against the page image):\n1. \"the appropriate duration of continuous training is 5-6 weeks, and with concentrated loading, the duration is 3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary for activating the compensatory processes.\"\n2. \"the trained athlete is able to tolerate three such sequential blocks of loading, separated by short recuperation pauses (7-10 days). After this, a longer recuperation period is necessary to facilitate stabilisation at the new level of adaptation.\"\n3. \"Thus, the time interval necessary for exhausting the body's CAR is between 18-22 weeks (see Fig 6.23). This period coincides with observations of highly qualified athletes training in different sports, including cyclic types (Zhikharevich, 1976; Sirenko, 1980).\"\n4. \"the body cannot indefinitely continue to adapt successively to training loads,\" and \"it is inappropriate to subject the body to extreme exhaustion of its energy reserves. This requires a longer recuperation period and has a negative effect on the subsequent stages of adaptation.\"\n\n**Why it works** — The authors' reason is the CAR model: each block draws on a limited reserve, the recuperation pause lets the compensatory reaction occur, and after about three blocks the reserve is spent (see [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]]). No data for the durations are shown beyond the two citations for the 18-22 week period.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: sketching an off-season or preparatory phase for a trained athlete: up to three loading blocks, each followed by a 7-10 day lighter pause, then a longer recovery before the next phase. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the 18-22 week ceiling is a reason to place a longer recovery period on that roadmap rather than run loading blocks back to back.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (concentrated block duration).** Here a concentrated block lasts \"3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). In 5.3.3 the book says \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective\" and refers to \"4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; wording confirmed in the text layer, and verified against the image in [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]). In 6.12.2 the method concentrates \"a high volume of unidirectional loading for a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, text layer only). Inference: one reading reconciles them (three 3-4 week blocks give about 9-12 weeks of unidirectional emphasis), but the book does not say so. **Unclear arithmetic:** three 5-6 week continuous blocks plus two 7-10 day pauses come to about 17-21 weeks; three 3-4 week concentrated blocks plus two pauses come to about 11-15 weeks. The text does not say which form the 18-22 weeks refers to, and Fig 6.23, which it cites, spans only 18 weeks. Inference: the 18-22 week figure fits the continuous form better. The authors do not say how the pause length or the \"longer recuperation period\" should be set beyond these figures.\n\n**Example** — The authors give the rule, not a worked plan. Inference: a January-to-May preparatory phase of three 4-week concentrated blocks, each followed by a 7-10 day lighter week, then a longer recovery period before summer training (the authors give no length for it).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-mesocycle-duration-and-monitoring]] (Issurin: accumulation blocks up to about six weeks, with gains falling sharply after that), [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--bondarchuk-hammer-throw-block-mesocycles]] (Bondarchuk's four-week blocks with two-week restorative blocks)\n","numLines":31,"startLine":1,"totalLines":31}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"17c9aef2-61ee-436c-946e-0b0925e8e18e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
