{"parentUuid":"326c0b6f-34f8-4ed0-83bd-432d82019052","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnzLphrWiwj6gFgnAnC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All three pages confirmed. Rewriting the concentrated-blocks conflict paragraph."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4847,"cache_read_input_tokens":153006,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4847,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CffnzBRA6ERM8JqZfiMJd","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"7b1aec08-8795-4501-b521-f94cd0e7caa9","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:07.756Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"7b1aec08-8795-4501-b521-f94cd0e7caa9","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CffnzLphrWiwj6gFgnAnC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01T62dHAPZG3hTaG66pkbTSX","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; f=heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md; ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 - \"$f\" <<'EOF'\nimport sys\np=sys.argv[1]; s=open(p).read()\nstart=s.index(\"**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (concentrated block duration).**\")\nend=s.index(\"**Unclear arithmetic:**\")\nnew=(\"**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (concentrated block length).** Here the concentrated block lasts \\\"3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary\\\", given as the duration that was \\\"determined\\\" for maximal enhancement of the CAR (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). In 5.3.3 the book gives, \\\"for instance, ... 4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training\\\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image). In 6.12.2 the method concentrates \\\"a high volume of unidirectional loading for a limited period (of up to 2 months)\\\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, checked against the page image). The 4-8 weeks and \\\"up to 2 months\\\" agree with each other; the 3-4 weeks overlaps them only at 4 weeks, so the conflict is between the 3-4 week figure and the longer blocks. \"\n\"**Different quantities, not part of the conflict:** 4-12 weeks is the length of a unidirectional training *stage*: \\\"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective\\\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294) and \\\"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\\\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370). 4-12 weeks is also how long the delayed training effect lasts: \\\"the LDTE was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation\\\", and in principle its duration \\\"is approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage\\\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image; see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]). 1-3 weeks is the latency before the gains of a concentrated block appear (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). 18-22 weeks is the stage needed to exhaust the CAR (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). The upper limits for one primary emphasis by motor quality are in [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]. \"\n\"Inference: one reading links block length to stage length (three 3-4 week blocks give about 9-12 weeks of unidirectional emphasis, inside the 4-12 week stage), but the book does not say so. Inference: 5.3.3 and 6.12.2 both define loading as concentrated by one month's volume (\\\"23-25% of the general volume for the year\\\", PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; \\\"23-25% of the general annual volume\\\", PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370), which makes the month the working unit; \\\"up to 2 months\\\" reads as a ceiling. \")\ns=s[:start]+new+s[end:]\na=\"[[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[principle--load-must-sit\"\nassert s.count(a)==1\ns=s.replace(a,\"[[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]] (6.9.4), [[principle--load-must-sit\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"When NOT\" $f | cut -c1-200; grep -c \"text layer\" $f","description":"Rewrite concentrated-blocks conflict paragraph"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4847,"cache_read_input_tokens":153006,"output_tokens":1673,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":211},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4847},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1673,"cache_read_input_tokens":153006,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4847,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4847,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01T62dHAPZG3hTaG66pkbTSX":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; f=heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md; ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 - \"$f\" <<'EOF'\nimport sys\np=sys.argv[1]; s=open(p).read()\nstart=s.index(\"**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (concentrated block duration).**\")\nend=s.index(\"**Unclear arithmetic:**\")\nnew=(\"**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (concentrated block length).** Here the concentrated block lasts \\\"3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary\\\", given as the duration that was \\\"determined\\\" for maximal enhancement of the CAR (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). In 5.3.3 the book gives, \\\"for instance, ... 4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training\\\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image). In 6.12.2 the method concentrates \\\"a high volume of unidirectional loading for a limited period (of up to 2 months)\\\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, checked against the page image). The 4-8 weeks and \\\"up to 2 months\\\" agree with each other; the 3-4 weeks overlaps them only at 4 weeks, so the conflict is between the 3-4 week figure and the longer blocks. \"\n\"**Different quantities, not part of the conflict:** 4-12 weeks is the length of a unidirectional training *stage*: \\\"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective\\\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294) and \\\"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\\\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370). 4-12 weeks is also how long the delayed training effect lasts: \\\"the LDTE was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation\\\", and in principle its duration \\\"is approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage\\\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image; see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]). 1-3 weeks is the latency before the gains of a concentrated block appear (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). 18-22 weeks is the stage needed to exhaust the CAR (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). The upper limits for one primary emphasis by motor quality are in [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]. \"\n\"Inference: one reading links block length to stage length (three 3-4 week blocks give about 9-12 weeks of unidirectional emphasis, inside the 4-12 week stage), but the book does not say so. Inference: 5.3.3 and 6.12.2 both define loading as concentrated by one month's volume (\\\"23-25% of the general volume for the year\\\", PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; \\\"23-25% of the general annual volume\\\", PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370), which makes the month the working unit; \\\"up to 2 months\\\" reads as a ceiling. \")\ns=s[:start]+new+s[end:]\na=\"[[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[principle--load-must-sit\"\nassert s.count(a)==1\ns=s.replace(a,\"[[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]] (6.9.4), [[principle--load-must-sit\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"When NOT\" $f | cut -c1-200; grep -c \"text layer\" $f","description":"Rewrite concentrated-blocks conflict paragraph"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CffnzBRA6ERM8JqZfiMJd","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"756b95fd-2a8e-453c-a48b-c0dcd4c36c9b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:17.780Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"756b95fd-2a8e-453c-a48b-c0dcd4c36c9b","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T62dHAPZG3hTaG66pkbTSX","type":"tool_result","content":"26:**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (concentrated block length).** Here the concentrated block lasts \"3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary\", given as the durati\n0","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"2f28c7d6-f851-4d7f-8a27-3bb6d03e72d4","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:18.074Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"26:**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (concentrated block length).** Here the concentrated block lasts \"3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary\", given as the durati\n0","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"returnCodeInterpretation":"No matches found","noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":23,"oldLines":8,"newStart":23,"newLines":8,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Inference: sketching an off-season or preparatory phase for a trained athlete: up to three loading blocks, each followed by a 7-10 day lighter pause, then a longer recovery before the next phase. Eric's baseline: \"An annual plan is treated as a flexible roadmap, not a fixed contract\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 183, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: the 18-22 week ceiling is a reason to place a longer recovery period on that roadmap rather than run loading blocks back to back."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (concentrated block duration).** Here a concentrated block lasts \"3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). In 5.3.3 the book says \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective\" and refers to \"4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; wording confirmed in the text layer, and verified against the image in [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]]). In 6.12.2 the method concentrates \"a high volume of unidirectional loading for a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, text layer only). Inference: one reading reconciles them (three 3-4 week blocks give about 9-12 weeks of unidirectional emphasis), but the book does not say so. **Unclear arithmetic:** three 5-6 week continuous blocks plus two 7-10 day pauses come to about 17-21 weeks; three 3-4 week concentrated blocks plus two pauses come to about 11-15 weeks. The text does not say which form the 18-22 weeks refers to, and Fig 6.23, which it cites, spans only 18 weeks. Inference: the 18-22 week figure fits the continuous form better. The authors do not say how the pause length or the \"longer recuperation period\" should be set beyond these figures.","+**When NOT to apply** — **Contested inside the book (concentrated block length).** Here the concentrated block lasts \"3-4 weeks, after which a recuperation period is necessary\", given as the duration that was \"determined\" for maximal enhancement of the CAR (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). In 5.3.3 the book gives, \"for instance, ... 4-8 week concentrated blocks of strength, speed-strength or speed training\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294, checked against the page image). In 6.12.2 the method concentrates \"a high volume of unidirectional loading for a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370, checked against the page image). The 4-8 weeks and \"up to 2 months\" agree with each other; the 3-4 weeks overlaps them only at 4 weeks, so the conflict is between the 3-4 week figure and the longer blocks. **Different quantities, not part of the conflict:** 4-12 weeks is the length of a unidirectional training *stage*: \"unidirectional training over periods as long as 4-12 weeks can be very effective\" (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294) and \"prolonged stages (4-12 weeks)\" (PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370). 4-12 weeks is also how long the delayed training effect lasts: \"the LDTE was observed to last from 4-12 weeks during the long-term stages of strength preparation\", and in principle its duration \"is approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage\" (PDF p. 375 / printed p. 364, checked against the page image; see [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]). 1-3 weeks is the latency before the gains of a concentrated block appear (PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294). 18-22 weeks is the stage needed to exhaust the CAR (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). The upper limits for one primary emphasis by motor quality are in [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]. Inference: one reading links block length to stage length (three 3-4 week blocks give about 9-12 weeks of unidirectional emphasis, inside the 4-12 week stage), but the book does not say so. Inference: 5.3.3 and 6.12.2 both define loading as concentrated by one month's volume (\"23-25% of the general volume for the year\", PDF p. 305 / printed p. 294; \"23-25% of the general annual volume\", PDF p. 381 / printed p. 370), which makes the month the working unit; \"up to 2 months\" reads as a ceiling. **Unclear arithmetic:** three 5-6 week continuous blocks plus two 7-10 day pauses come to about 17-21 weeks; three 3-4 week concentrated blocks plus two pauses come to about 11-15 weeks. The text does not say which form the 18-22 weeks refers to, and Fig 6.23, which it cites, spans only 18 weeks. Inference: the 18-22 week figure fits the continuous form better. The authors do not say how the pause length or the \"longer recuperation period\" should be set beyond these figures."," "," **Example** — The authors give the rule, not a worked plan. Inference: a January-to-May preparatory phase of three 4-week concentrated blocks, each followed by a 7-10 day lighter week, then a longer recovery period before summer training (the authors give no length for it)."," ","-**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-mesocycle-duration-and-monitoring]] (Issurin: accumulation blocks up to about six weeks, with gains falling sharply after that), [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--bondarchuk-hammer-throw-block-mesocycles]] (Bondarchuk's four-week blocks with two-week restorative blocks)","+**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[framework--conditions-for-concentrated-unidirectional-loading]], [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]], [[heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance]], [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]] (6.9.4), [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]], [[block-periodization--issurin/framework--accumulation-mesocycle-duration-and-monitoring]] (Issurin: accumulation blocks up to about six weeks, with gains falling sharply after that), [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--bondarchuk-hammer-throw-block-mesocycles]] (Bondarchuk's four-week blocks with two-week restorative blocks)"]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"756b95fd-2a8e-453c-a48b-c0dcd4c36c9b","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
