{"parentUuid":"3a316980-5e6f-4cc8-bd8f-85ec558ba087","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffo1Zwmr2eLkJWayMrYf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0175aPoKkwMAdkZ2SyGueB6b","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1771,"cache_read_input_tokens":157853,"output_tokens":305,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":177},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1771},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":305,"cache_read_input_tokens":157853,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1771,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1771,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0175aPoKkwMAdkZ2SyGueB6b":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffo1L9oGX18UzeQtZCRs","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"435cb581-ee30-45ce-8772-e91d6a7aa59e","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:24.292Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"435cb581-ee30-45ce-8772-e91d6a7aa59e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0175aPoKkwMAdkZ2SyGueB6b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttype: library-entry\n3\tkind: claim\n4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n6\tchapter: \"6.8.1\"\n7\ttopic: volume versus intensity\n8\tcategory: [training-load, intensity, volume, throwing]\n9\tconfidence: untested\n10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Bondarchuk (1982): 50% More Volume of the Same Programme Did Not Lengthen the Training Effect in Hammer Throwers\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — The authors report: \"a study done by Bondarchuk (1982) which analysed the effect of different volumes of an identical training programme on a group of hammer-throwers. He found that a 50% increase in volume of loading had no significant effect on the duration of maintenance of the training effect and sporting performance. Although Bondarchuk stresses that this finding applies strictly to a particular group of athletes, it tends to confirm that major changes in intensity have a more profound effect on athletic state than comparable changes in volume. It also implies that calculations of volume are not as critical as those of intensity, thus corroborating the importance of paying careful attention to zones of loading intensity, as emphasized earlier in 6.2.2 (see also Table 6.1)\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350, checked against the page image; the last phrase is italic in the original).\n17\t\n18\tTwo layers: Bondarchuk's finding (50% more volume, no significant change in how long the training effect and performance were maintained) and the authors' extrapolation (intensity matters more than volume).\n19\t\n20\t**Why it works** — Bondarchuk's study as reported does not test intensity at all; the comparison with intensity is the authors' reading. No sample size, volumes, durations or measures are given on the page.\n21\t\n22\t**When to apply** — Inference: as one reason not to answer a stalled thrower or strength athlete with more of the same work. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is 'maintain intensity, reduce volume'\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the Bondarchuk result is consistent with that rule's premise that volume can drop without losing the effect, though it was not an in-season study.\n23\t\n24\t**When NOT to apply** — One group of hammer throwers, by Bondarchuk's own caveat \"strictly\" limited to them; hence `untested`. The study varied volume only, so \"intensity has a more profound effect\" is not shown by it. Inference: on the page before, the authors argue the opposite direction for volume: \"the more extensive and longer the exhaustion of the body's energy resources by concentrated loading ... the higher their subsequent super-restoration and the longer the new functional level is maintained\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349), which makes concentrated volume the lever; the authors set the two side by side without reconciling them. The Table 6.1 and 6.2.2 material it points to is in batch 6a's pages (PDF pp. 334-336), not checked here.\n25\t\n26\t**Example** — The authors' own: Bondarchuk's hammer throwers, whose 50% volume increase did not change how long the training effect was maintained (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350).\n27\t\n28\t**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--throws-strength-exercise-ceiling-effect]], [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--bondarchuk-hammer-throw-block-mesocycles]]\n29\t"}]},"uuid":"359af1d2-a831-4393-8534-fcd80e11c3ed","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:24.465Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.8.1\"\ntopic: volume versus intensity\ncategory: [training-load, intensity, volume, throwing]\nconfidence: untested\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Bondarchuk (1982): 50% More Volume of the Same Programme Did Not Lengthen the Training Effect in Hammer Throwers\n\n**What it is** — The authors report: \"a study done by Bondarchuk (1982) which analysed the effect of different volumes of an identical training programme on a group of hammer-throwers. He found that a 50% increase in volume of loading had no significant effect on the duration of maintenance of the training effect and sporting performance. Although Bondarchuk stresses that this finding applies strictly to a particular group of athletes, it tends to confirm that major changes in intensity have a more profound effect on athletic state than comparable changes in volume. It also implies that calculations of volume are not as critical as those of intensity, thus corroborating the importance of paying careful attention to zones of loading intensity, as emphasized earlier in 6.2.2 (see also Table 6.1)\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350, checked against the page image; the last phrase is italic in the original).\n\nTwo layers: Bondarchuk's finding (50% more volume, no significant change in how long the training effect and performance were maintained) and the authors' extrapolation (intensity matters more than volume).\n\n**Why it works** — Bondarchuk's study as reported does not test intensity at all; the comparison with intensity is the authors' reading. No sample size, volumes, durations or measures are given on the page.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: as one reason not to answer a stalled thrower or strength athlete with more of the same work. Eric's baseline: \"In-season, the stated rule is 'maintain intensity, reduce volume'\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the Bondarchuk result is consistent with that rule's premise that volume can drop without losing the effect, though it was not an in-season study.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — One group of hammer throwers, by Bondarchuk's own caveat \"strictly\" limited to them; hence `untested`. The study varied volume only, so \"intensity has a more profound effect\" is not shown by it. Inference: on the page before, the authors argue the opposite direction for volume: \"the more extensive and longer the exhaustion of the body's energy resources by concentrated loading ... the higher their subsequent super-restoration and the longer the new functional level is maintained\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349), which makes concentrated volume the lever; the authors set the two side by side without reconciling them. The Table 6.1 and 6.2.2 material it points to is in batch 6a's pages (PDF pp. 334-336), not checked here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: Bondarchuk's hammer throwers, whose 50% volume increase did not change how long the training effect was maintained (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation]], [[claim--excess-heavy-strength-volume-impairs-speed-strength-and-technique]], [[heuristic--structured-load-progression-beats-volume-alone-or-random-combination]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--throws-strength-exercise-ceiling-effect]], [[block-periodization--issurin/case-study--bondarchuk-hammer-throw-block-mesocycles]]\n","numLines":29,"startLine":1,"totalLines":29}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"435cb581-ee30-45ce-8772-e91d6a7aa59e","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
