{"parentUuid":"4b3c1301-622f-428a-8451-fef7a0cd2bd0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016vh3rVNKXHjGNJSGyX55gP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttype: library-entry\n3\tkind: principle\n4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n6\tchapter: \"6.8.2\"\n7\ttopic: specialisation by physiological system\n8\tcategory: [specificity, energy-systems, concurrent-training, long-term-development]\n9\tconfidence: supported\n10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Specialisation Sets a Primary Emphasis, Not an Exclusive System: Explosive Athletes Need Aerobic Efficiency, Endurance Athletes Need Strength\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — \"Structural-functional specialisation refers to those stable, adaptive changes that are produced by the specific form of conditioning imposed by a given sport,\" and \"those systems which are of major importance for success in sport undergo an accelerated rate of functional growth\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350, checked against the page image). The specifics of adaptation are set by \"the work regime in sports, and the gradual, but steady increase in complexity of the body's external interactions associated with the growth of sporting proficiency\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351, checked against the page image).\n17\t\n18\tUsing the sport groups, \"specialisation by physiological system means that acyclic sports rely predominantly on anaerobic processes and functional hypertrophy of the motor apparatus, whereas cyclic sports place major reliance on aerobic processes. In complex (combined) sports, there is an intense functional development of both the motor apparatus and the energy production systems\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). The page says \"the classification scheme of 6.5\"; the scheme is in 6.7.\n19\t\n20\tThe new point is the qualifier: \"specialisation in the first two cases does not imply that development is either strictly aerobic or anaerobic, but rather that there is a particular primary emphasis of functional specialisation. One can successfully develop a high level of explosive strength in acyclic locomotion only when one possesses sufficiently high aerobic efficiency. At the same time, improving the efficiency of the energy producing systems in cyclic locomotion should be based on highly developed muscular strength and local muscular endurance\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). For cyclic sports: \"strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role\" (Zhurbinoi, 1978).\n21\t\n22\t**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for why explosive strength needs aerobic efficiency on this page. For the cyclic side they point to Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related). On cost they add: \"energy expenditure being almost proportional to the fourth power of running speed (Sargent, 1926),\" with the rising cost met by \"a greater reliance being placed on anaerobic processes\" (Henry, 1951; Margaria, 1963) (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). The fourth-power figure carries only that 1926 citation, and no later check is given.\n23\t\n24\t**When to apply** — Inference: when a speed-power programme drops all aerobic work, or an endurance programme drops strength work, this principle argues for keeping a supporting dose of the other. Eric's baseline: \"For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 123, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: that is one plausible route for the authors' \"sufficiently high aerobic efficiency,\" though the authors do not give it. Eric's baseline: football's priority stack names \"alactic and aerobic capacity/power\" as Goals 1 and 2 (line 116, Explicit, claimed again in interview 2026-09-28, Q1). Inference: the baseline already pairs the explosive and aerobic systems, as the authors' qualifier does.\n25\t\n26\t**When NOT to apply** — \"Sufficiently high\" is not quantified, and no study is cited for the acyclic half of the claim. Inference: it does not license large aerobic volumes for power athletes; the claim is a minimum, not a target. The heterochronicity point on the same page (\"the body adapts to external influences only when they become necessary for the further growth of sporting proficiency\") is already covered by the Chapter 2 heterochronicity entry in Related and is not re-atomized here.\n27\t\n28\t**Example** — The authors' own: improving the triple jump \"requires developing the ability of the motor apparatus to produce great power,\" and a longer jump, gained mainly through running speed, brings \"a significant increase in complexity of the interaction of the feet with the support at each take-off, a situation that requires significant improvement in reactive ability\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\n29\t\n30\t**Related** — [[framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]] (5.4.3, specialisation by system and by ability), [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]], [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]], [[principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov]]\n31\t"}]},"uuid":"891ac5c8-26aa-44ae-8899-4b4dc21e41c3","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:43.516Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: principle\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.8.2\"\ntopic: specialisation by physiological system\ncategory: [specificity, energy-systems, concurrent-training, long-term-development]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Specialisation Sets a Primary Emphasis, Not an Exclusive System: Explosive Athletes Need Aerobic Efficiency, Endurance Athletes Need Strength\n\n**What it is** — \"Structural-functional specialisation refers to those stable, adaptive changes that are produced by the specific form of conditioning imposed by a given sport,\" and \"those systems which are of major importance for success in sport undergo an accelerated rate of functional growth\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350, checked against the page image). The specifics of adaptation are set by \"the work regime in sports, and the gradual, but steady increase in complexity of the body's external interactions associated with the growth of sporting proficiency\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351, checked against the page image).\n\nUsing the sport groups, \"specialisation by physiological system means that acyclic sports rely predominantly on anaerobic processes and functional hypertrophy of the motor apparatus, whereas cyclic sports place major reliance on aerobic processes. In complex (combined) sports, there is an intense functional development of both the motor apparatus and the energy production systems\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). The page says \"the classification scheme of 6.5\"; the scheme is in 6.7.\n\nThe new point is the qualifier: \"specialisation in the first two cases does not imply that development is either strictly aerobic or anaerobic, but rather that there is a particular primary emphasis of functional specialisation. One can successfully develop a high level of explosive strength in acyclic locomotion only when one possesses sufficiently high aerobic efficiency. At the same time, improving the efficiency of the energy producing systems in cyclic locomotion should be based on highly developed muscular strength and local muscular endurance\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). For cyclic sports: \"strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role\" (Zhurbinoi, 1978).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for why explosive strength needs aerobic efficiency on this page. For the cyclic side they point to Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related). On cost they add: \"energy expenditure being almost proportional to the fourth power of running speed (Sargent, 1926),\" with the rising cost met by \"a greater reliance being placed on anaerobic processes\" (Henry, 1951; Margaria, 1963) (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). The fourth-power figure carries only that 1926 citation, and no later check is given.\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: when a speed-power programme drops all aerobic work, or an endurance programme drops strength work, this principle argues for keeping a supporting dose of the other. Eric's baseline: \"For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 123, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: that is one plausible route for the authors' \"sufficiently high aerobic efficiency,\" though the authors do not give it. Eric's baseline: football's priority stack names \"alactic and aerobic capacity/power\" as Goals 1 and 2 (line 116, Explicit, claimed again in interview 2026-09-28, Q1). Inference: the baseline already pairs the explosive and aerobic systems, as the authors' qualifier does.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — \"Sufficiently high\" is not quantified, and no study is cited for the acyclic half of the claim. Inference: it does not license large aerobic volumes for power athletes; the claim is a minimum, not a target. The heterochronicity point on the same page (\"the body adapts to external influences only when they become necessary for the further growth of sporting proficiency\") is already covered by the Chapter 2 heterochronicity entry in Related and is not re-atomized here.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: improving the triple jump \"requires developing the ability of the motor apparatus to produce great power,\" and a longer jump, gained mainly through running speed, brings \"a significant increase in complexity of the interaction of the feet with the support at each take-off, a situation that requires significant improvement in reactive ability\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\n\n**Related** — [[framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming]], [[framework--specific-correspondence-of-the-training-effect-four-tasks]] (5.4.3, specialisation by system and by ability), [[framework--four-level-functional-specialisation-and-special-physical-preparedness]], [[claim--endurance-rests-on-muscle-oxidative-and-strength-qualities-not-vo2max]], [[principle--motor-system-leads-cardiorespiratory-adaptation-do-not-separate-them]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov]]\n","numLines":31,"startLine":1,"totalLines":31}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4b3c1301-622f-428a-8451-fef7a0cd2bd0","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
