{"parentUuid":"8383b557-182a-4632-8ef0-8a35433ca1c0","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffo3PhRz3tGGv6CpKBja","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Confirmed both points. Applying."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6490,"cache_read_input_tokens":161744,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6490,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":33,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cffo3FtPBnPsC3fgUfyL8","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"4413bf77-bd4a-4fd6-8f68-b0113c2d494b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:47.115Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"4413bf77-bd4a-4fd6-8f68-b0113c2d494b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffo3PhRz3tGGv6CpKBja","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XvFEs6LF8DxoV3nisnvoYH","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; f=principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md; ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 - \"$f\" <<'EOF'\nimport sys\np=sys.argv[1]; s=open(p).read()\nreps=[\n(\"For cyclic sports: \\\"strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role\\\" (Zhurbinoi, 1978).\",\n \"For cyclic sports: \\\"In cyclic sports, strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role. Research shows that, with a relatively moderate increase in muscular strength, together with a rise in the athlete's qualification, the qualities of dynamic strength-endurance and power accelerate the increase in functional strength (Zhurbinoi, 1978)\\\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\"),\n(\"For the cyclic side they point to Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related).\",\n \"For the cyclic side the authors cite Zhurbinoi (1978) on strength-endurance (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351); the page's cross-references are to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3 only. Inference: Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related) supports the same point.\"),\n]\nfor a,b in reps:\n    n=s.count(a); print(n, a[:50]); assert n==1\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF","description":"Apply functional-specialisation fixes"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6490,"cache_read_input_tokens":161744,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6490,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":33,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01XvFEs6LF8DxoV3nisnvoYH":{"command":"cd /Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky; f=principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md; ~/.local/bin/uv run python3 - \"$f\" <<'EOF'\nimport sys\np=sys.argv[1]; s=open(p).read()\nreps=[\n(\"For cyclic sports: \\\"strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role\\\" (Zhurbinoi, 1978).\",\n \"For cyclic sports: \\\"In cyclic sports, strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role. Research shows that, with a relatively moderate increase in muscular strength, together with a rise in the athlete's qualification, the qualities of dynamic strength-endurance and power accelerate the increase in functional strength (Zhurbinoi, 1978)\\\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\"),\n(\"For the cyclic side they point to Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related).\",\n \"For the cyclic side the authors cite Zhurbinoi (1978) on strength-endurance (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351); the page's cross-references are to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3 only. Inference: Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related) supports the same point.\"),\n]\nfor a,b in reps:\n    n=s.count(a); print(n, a[:50]); assert n==1\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF","description":"Apply functional-specialisation fixes"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cffo3FtPBnPsC3fgUfyL8","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"1b9a001d-5c4b-4626-9e91-ad618cdb64bb","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:51.385Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"1b9a001d-5c4b-4626-9e91-ad618cdb64bb","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XvFEs6LF8DxoV3nisnvoYH","type":"tool_result","content":"1 For cyclic sports: \"strength preparedness, mainly \n1 For the cyclic side they point to Chapter 4's argu","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"211234d0-ea71-4170-a1ec-cf8bd4d92816","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:51.648Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"1 For cyclic sports: \"strength preparedness, mainly \n1 For the cyclic side they point to Chapter 4's argu","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":17,"oldLines":9,"newStart":17,"newLines":9,"lines":[" "," Using the sport groups, \"specialisation by physiological system means that acyclic sports rely predominantly on anaerobic processes and functional hypertrophy of the motor apparatus, whereas cyclic sports place major reliance on aerobic processes. In complex (combined) sports, there is an intense functional development of both the motor apparatus and the energy production systems\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). The page says \"the classification scheme of 6.5\"; the scheme is in 6.7."," ","-The new point is the qualifier: \"specialisation in the first two cases does not imply that development is either strictly aerobic or anaerobic, but rather that there is a particular primary emphasis of functional specialisation. One can successfully develop a high level of explosive strength in acyclic locomotion only when one possesses sufficiently high aerobic efficiency. At the same time, improving the efficiency of the energy producing systems in cyclic locomotion should be based on highly developed muscular strength and local muscular endurance\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). For cyclic sports: \"strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role\" (Zhurbinoi, 1978).","+The new point is the qualifier: \"specialisation in the first two cases does not imply that development is either strictly aerobic or anaerobic, but rather that there is a particular primary emphasis of functional specialisation. One can successfully develop a high level of explosive strength in acyclic locomotion only when one possesses sufficiently high aerobic efficiency. At the same time, improving the efficiency of the energy producing systems in cyclic locomotion should be based on highly developed muscular strength and local muscular endurance\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). For cyclic sports: \"In cyclic sports, strength preparedness, mainly in the form of strength-endurance, plays an important role. Research shows that, with a relatively moderate increase in muscular strength, together with a rise in the athlete's qualification, the qualities of dynamic strength-endurance and power accelerate the increase in functional strength (Zhurbinoi, 1978)\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351)."," ","-**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for why explosive strength needs aerobic efficiency on this page. For the cyclic side they point to Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related). On cost they add: \"energy expenditure being almost proportional to the fourth power of running speed (Sargent, 1926),\" with the rising cost met by \"a greater reliance being placed on anaerobic processes\" (Henry, 1951; Margaria, 1963) (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). The fourth-power figure carries only that 1926 citation, and no later check is given.","+**Why it works** — The authors give no mechanism for why explosive strength needs aerobic efficiency on this page. For the cyclic side the authors cite Zhurbinoi (1978) on strength-endurance (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351); the page's cross-references are to 2.2.3 and 5.4.3 only. Inference: Chapter 4's argument that endurance rests on muscular as well as respiratory qualities (see Related) supports the same point. On cost they add: \"energy expenditure being almost proportional to the fourth power of running speed (Sargent, 1926),\" with the rising cost met by \"a greater reliance being placed on anaerobic processes\" (Henry, 1951; Margaria, 1963) (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351). The fourth-power figure carries only that 1926 citation, and no later check is given."," "," **When to apply** — Inference: when a speed-power programme drops all aerobic work, or an endurance programme drops strength work, this principle argues for keeping a supporting dose of the other. Eric's baseline: \"For speed-power athletes, the aerobic system's main value is inter-effort recovery (regenerating ATP between bouts)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 123, Inferred (transcript)). Inference: that is one plausible route for the authors' \"sufficiently high aerobic efficiency,\" though the authors do not give it. Eric's baseline: football's priority stack names \"alactic and aerobic capacity/power\" as Goals 1 and 2 (line 116, Explicit, claimed again in interview 2026-09-28, Q1). Inference: the baseline already pairs the explosive and aerobic systems, as the authors' qualifier does."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1b9a001d-5c4b-4626-9e91-ad618cdb64bb","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"211234d0-ea71-4170-a1ec-cf8bd4d92816","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cffo3PhRz3tGGv6CpKBja","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WNb75YxUmkZpKqhnhtbHME","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6490,"cache_read_input_tokens":161744,"output_tokens":800,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6490},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":800,"cache_read_input_tokens":161744,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6490,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6490,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01WNb75YxUmkZpKqhnhtbHME":{"file_path":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov.md"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cffo3FtPBnPsC3fgUfyL8","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"b0c18731-4d4f-4089-b513-49eb956863b1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:52.209Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
{"parentUuid":"b0c18731-4d4f-4089-b513-49eb956863b1","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WNb75YxUmkZpKqhnhtbHME","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\ttype: library-entry\n3\tkind: claim\n4\tdomain: strength-and-conditioning\n5\tbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\n6\tchapter: \"6.8.2\"\n7\ttopic: training emphasis by qualification\n8\tcategory: [long-term-development, sprinting, endurance, energy-systems]\n9\tconfidence: supported\n10\tingest-tier: full-text\n11\tcreated: 2026-10-03\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t# Training Emphasis Shifts With Qualification: Sprinters Move From Aerobic Endurance and Speed to Speed Endurance; Distance Runners Add Anaerobic Work at the Top (Vankov 1978)\n15\t\n16\t**What it is** — The authors report Vankov (1978) on multi-year emphasis (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351, checked against the page image): \"In the multi-year training of sprinters, up to Class I-CMS (Class I - Candidate for Master of Sport), the chief emphasis is on developing general (aerobic) endurance and speed, whereas at MS-MSIC (Master of Sport - Master of Sport, International Class) level the emphasis is on developing speed (anaerobic) endurance. In the training of distance athletes of all qualification levels, the chief emphasis is on developing aerobic productivity, while at the MS-MSIC level, the emphasis is on enhancing anaerobic productivity (Vankov, 1978).\"\n17\t\n18\tThe surrounding point: kinesiological patterns across sports \"are approximately equivalent, although there are some qualitative and quantitative differences\"; \"sprint swimmers have higher rates and developmental levels of anaerobic productivity than distance swimmers, whereas aerobic productivity is superior for distance swimmers. This is determined by the emphasis of the training\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\n19\t\n20\t**Why it works** — The authors attribute the swimmer differences to training emphasis; no mechanism is given for the qualification shift. Inference: it fits the convergence principle, in which training effects move toward the sport's specific regime as mastery rises (see Related).\n21\t\n22\t**When to apply** — Inference: in long-term plans for sprinters, a broad aerobic and speed base below national-class level, with speed endurance becoming the main emphasis only at the top. Eric's baseline: \"The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 101, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: Vankov's sprinter sequence also starts from general capacity and narrows toward the event's specific demand.\n23\t\n24\t**When NOT to apply** — **Unclear as printed.** The distance-runner sentence says the chief emphasis is aerobic \"of all qualification levels\" and then that \"at the MS-MSIC level, the emphasis is on enhancing anaerobic productivity,\" which contradicts \"all.\" Inference: the likely meaning is that aerobic work stays the chief emphasis at every level and anaerobic work is added at the top, but the page does not say so. Soviet classification levels (Class I, CMS, MS, MSIC) do not map directly onto college levels, and no data, sample or event distances are given; hence `supported` at most.\n25\t\n26\t**Example** — The authors' own: sprint swimmers with higher anaerobic and distance swimmers with higher aerobic productivity, \"determined by the emphasis of the training\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\n27\t\n28\t**Related** — [[principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system]], [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[claim--russian-sport-school-age-guidelines-table]], [[claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--endurance-event-transfer-range-widens-with-distance]]\n29\t"}]},"uuid":"5680b744-5ec6-4914-89bc-d4afb59c9ee5","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:52.364Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov.md","content":"---\ntype: library-entry\nkind: claim\ndomain: strength-and-conditioning\nbook: supertraining--verkhoshansky\nchapter: \"6.8.2\"\ntopic: training emphasis by qualification\ncategory: [long-term-development, sprinting, endurance, energy-systems]\nconfidence: supported\ningest-tier: full-text\ncreated: 2026-10-03\n---\n\n# Training Emphasis Shifts With Qualification: Sprinters Move From Aerobic Endurance and Speed to Speed Endurance; Distance Runners Add Anaerobic Work at the Top (Vankov 1978)\n\n**What it is** — The authors report Vankov (1978) on multi-year emphasis (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351, checked against the page image): \"In the multi-year training of sprinters, up to Class I-CMS (Class I - Candidate for Master of Sport), the chief emphasis is on developing general (aerobic) endurance and speed, whereas at MS-MSIC (Master of Sport - Master of Sport, International Class) level the emphasis is on developing speed (anaerobic) endurance. In the training of distance athletes of all qualification levels, the chief emphasis is on developing aerobic productivity, while at the MS-MSIC level, the emphasis is on enhancing anaerobic productivity (Vankov, 1978).\"\n\nThe surrounding point: kinesiological patterns across sports \"are approximately equivalent, although there are some qualitative and quantitative differences\"; \"sprint swimmers have higher rates and developmental levels of anaerobic productivity than distance swimmers, whereas aerobic productivity is superior for distance swimmers. This is determined by the emphasis of the training\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\n\n**Why it works** — The authors attribute the swimmer differences to training emphasis; no mechanism is given for the qualification shift. Inference: it fits the convergence principle, in which training effects move toward the sport's specific regime as mastery rises (see Related).\n\n**When to apply** — Inference: in long-term plans for sprinters, a broad aerobic and speed base below national-class level, with speed endurance becoming the main emphasis only at the top. Eric's baseline: \"The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 101, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: Vankov's sprinter sequence also starts from general capacity and narrows toward the event's specific demand.\n\n**When NOT to apply** — **Unclear as printed.** The distance-runner sentence says the chief emphasis is aerobic \"of all qualification levels\" and then that \"at the MS-MSIC level, the emphasis is on enhancing anaerobic productivity,\" which contradicts \"all.\" Inference: the likely meaning is that aerobic work stays the chief emphasis at every level and anaerobic work is added at the top, but the page does not say so. Soviet classification levels (Class I, CMS, MS, MSIC) do not map directly onto college levels, and no data, sample or event distances are given; hence `supported` at most.\n\n**Example** — The authors' own: sprint swimmers with higher anaerobic and distance swimmers with higher aerobic productivity, \"determined by the emphasis of the training\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351).\n\n**Related** — [[principle--functional-specialisation-is-a-primary-emphasis-not-an-exclusive-energy-system]], [[principle--converging-partial-effects-of-strength-training-means]], [[claim--russian-sport-school-age-guidelines-table]], [[claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation]], [[transfer-of-training-in-sports--bondarchuk/claim--endurance-event-transfer-range-widens-with-distance]]\n","numLines":29,"startLine":1,"totalLines":29}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b0c18731-4d4f-4089-b513-49eb956863b1","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
