{"parentUuid":"1899ff6b-05de-43a6-abe5-b0a4dc8eeeca","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"a9f69a56-41de-4fd2-88ce-bc0e210b67cb","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014jB9juqAQutxQFXmUdjD5Q","type":"tool_result","content":"1 Distance Runners Add Anaerobic Work at the Top (Va\n1 The distance-runner sentence says\n# Training Emphasis Shifts With Qualification: Sprinters Move From Aerobic Endurance and Speed to Speed Endurance; for Distance Athletes the Top-Level Emphasis Is Printed as Anaerobic (Vankov 1978)","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b613cfe1-3717-4f57-b53b-1aba480e795f","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:41:59.495Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"1 Distance Runners Add Anaerobic Work at the Top (Va\n1 The distance-runner sentence says\n# Training Emphasis Shifts With Qualification: Sprinters Move From Aerobic Endurance and Speed to Speed Endurance; for Distance Athletes the Top-Level Emphasis Is Printed as Anaerobic (Vankov 1978)","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":11,"oldLines":7,"newStart":11,"newLines":7,"lines":[" created: 2026-10-03"," ---"," ","-# Training Emphasis Shifts With Qualification: Sprinters Move From Aerobic Endurance and Speed to Speed Endurance; Distance Runners Add Anaerobic Work at the Top (Vankov 1978)","+# Training Emphasis Shifts With Qualification: Sprinters Move From Aerobic Endurance and Speed to Speed Endurance; for Distance Athletes the Top-Level Emphasis Is Printed as Anaerobic (Vankov 1978)"," "," **What it is** — The authors report Vankov (1978) on multi-year emphasis (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351, checked against the page image): \"In the multi-year training of sprinters, up to Class I-CMS (Class I - Candidate for Master of Sport), the chief emphasis is on developing general (aerobic) endurance and speed, whereas at MS-MSIC (Master of Sport - Master of Sport, International Class) level the emphasis is on developing speed (anaerobic) endurance. In the training of distance athletes of all qualification levels, the chief emphasis is on developing aerobic productivity, while at the MS-MSIC level, the emphasis is on enhancing anaerobic productivity (Vankov, 1978).\""," "]},{"oldStart":21,"oldLines":7,"newStart":21,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Inference: in long-term plans for sprinters, a broad aerobic and speed base below national-class level, with speed endurance becoming the main emphasis only at the top. Eric's baseline: \"The Vermeil hierarchy of athletic development (work capacity, then strength, then power, then speed, then sport skill, each level a required foundation for the next)\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 101, Explicit (interview 2026-09-28, Q1)). Inference: Vankov's sprinter sequence also starts from general capacity and narrows toward the event's specific demand."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — **Unclear as printed.** The distance-runner sentence says the chief emphasis is aerobic \"of all qualification levels\" and then that \"at the MS-MSIC level, the emphasis is on enhancing anaerobic productivity,\" which contradicts \"all.\" Inference: the likely meaning is that aerobic work stays the chief emphasis at every level and anaerobic work is added at the top, but the page does not say so. Soviet classification levels (Class I, CMS, MS, MSIC) do not map directly onto college levels, and no data, sample or event distances are given; hence `supported` at most.","+**When NOT to apply** — **Unclear as printed.** The distance-athlete sentence (\"distance athletes\", not runners) says the chief emphasis is aerobic \"of all qualification levels\" and then that \"at the MS-MSIC level, the emphasis is on enhancing anaerobic productivity,\" which contradicts \"all.\" Inference: the likely meaning is that aerobic work stays the chief emphasis at every level and anaerobic work is added at the top, but the page does not say so. Soviet classification levels (Class I, CMS, MS, MSIC) do not map directly onto college levels, and no data, sample or event distances are given; hence `supported` at most."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: sprint swimmers with higher anaerobic and distance swimmers with higher aerobic productivity, \"determined by the emphasis of the training\" (PDF p. 362 / printed p. 351)."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--sprint-and-distance-training-emphasis-shifts-with-qualification-vankov.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1899ff6b-05de-43a6-abe5-b0a4dc8eeeca","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
