ach specific goals, whether social, economic, military, sporting or otherwise. What is also common \n51:SUPERTRAINING\n59:characteristic curve for any individual; instead it depends on the effort of a manager, leader or coach to become \n60:adequately informed of the personal attributes, performance and characteristics of each worker or athlete. What is \n61:relevant is that the manager or coach appreciates that all motivational techniques are not universally applicable to \n87:high levels of stress. This difference in the effect of high levels of training and competitive stress has important \n89:and the extrovert requiring careful monitoring to prevent overtraining caused by a desire for greater stimulation.\n91:optimal performance over very limited periods (e.g. in golf, gymnastics and pole vaulting), whereas simple sports \n93:Excessive exposure of an athlete to\n98:SUPERTRAINING\n118:complex training tasks can lead rapidly to ‘overstrcss*, while simple tasks may be insufficiently challenging to \n120:necessary to ensure growth and to minimise overtraining.\n130:However, this state is not simply the preserve of the ascetic or mystic, because many athletes have reported \n135:SUPERTRAINING\n156:leader can motivate athletes strongly if he appreciates that motivation depends on perceptions of goal attractiveness \n157:and attainability. He can improve performance either by modifying the task accordingly or by altering the athlete’s \n165:6.17). All too often this model continues to be applied by managers, leaders and coaches steeped in the traditional\n181:SUPERTRAINING\n189:To use this system, the coach in playing a role as leader rates the maturity of the follower as low, moderate or high \n201:SUPERTRAINING\n236:SUPERTRAINING\n250:6.7 Classification of Sports\n251:Establishing the principles and rules of programming training necessitates determining clearly the limits of \n252:generality and specificity in individual sports or groups of related sports. To do this it is necessary to classify \n253:sports scientifically to establish programming criteria.\n254:Sport is associated first of all, with the spatio-temporal organisation of the athlete's movements, which is \n259:sports into individual, team, ball or water sports, for example, is not scientifically useful; it is more appropriate to \n260:classify sports on the basis of the particular organisation of the sporting movements and characteristics of the \n269:acyclic work. Under conditions of heavy dynamic overload, athletes must overcome significant external \n273:regulation and increases the power of anaerobic energy production. This preamble shows that sports may be \n274:divided into two broad groups, namely acyclic and cyclic sports:","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"be60723b-bb0f-44e3-8200-c6153b568994","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:42:08.411Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"2:SUPERTRAINING\n8:athletes in cyclic sports. However, this should not have been unexpected if they had concurrently examined the \n9:contractile and metabolic characteristics of the muscles of athletes who specialise in general endurance sports.\n10:Sport training theory also abounds with misdirected efforts. Here there is a similar division in the interests of \n11:specialists who give preference primarily to speed-strength or cyclic sports and tend to minimise the role of \n12:general endurance training. This professional specialisation cannot be condemned if they limit their theoretical \n13:and practical generalisations to 'their' types of sport. It is when they attempt to apply the same principles and \n14:methods to other types of sport that this single-minded approach becomes misleading.\n15:A definite weakness in modem sports training theory is the result of underestimating the importance of \n16:information obtained from physiological and biochemical research in sport, as well as from the limited amount of \n17:definitive scientific information which forms the basis for establishing training prin