g the task accordingly or by altering the athlete’s \n165:6.17). All too often this model continues to be applied by managers, leaders and coaches steeped in the traditional\n181:SUPERTRAINING\n189:To use this system, the coach in playing a role as leader rates the maturity of the follower as low, moderate or high \n201:SUPERTRAINING\n236:SUPERTRAINING\n250:6.7 Classification of Sports\n251:Establishing the principles and rules of programming training necessitates determining clearly the limits of \n252:generality and specificity in individual sports or groups of related sports. To do this it is necessary to classify \n253:sports scientifically to establish programming criteria.\n254:Sport is associated first of all, with the spatio-temporal organisation of the athlete's movements, which is \n259:sports into individual, team, ball or water sports, for example, is not scientifically useful; it is more appropriate to \n260:classify sports on the basis of the particular organisation of the sporting movements and characteristics of the \n269:acyclic work. Under conditions of heavy dynamic overload, athletes must overcome significant external \n273:regulation and increases the power of anaerobic energy production. This preamble shows that sports may be \n274:divided into two broad groups, namely acyclic and cyclic sports:","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"16af7bd4-53be-4059-b32a-d980c23871db","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
