{"parentUuid":"5c69d3b6-d494-4c1b-9fe3-b0dd62903a99","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"eca50d28-8512-43f5-a4de-783e8ab372de","agentId":"a3a7c5b2195561b4b","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TjvCaNwV2XHGhtr6mLdu8K","type":"tool_result","content":"1\n1\n1\n1\n0\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 132, 133]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\nbatch files: none above","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"ac42796e-4b51-4993-800a-4e08bf5d321b","timestamp":"2026-10-03T18:55:22.133Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"1\n1\n1\n1\n0\nsupertraining--verkhoshansky/_book.md: body em dash lines [12, 132, 133]\nTOTAL ISSUES: 1\nbatch files: none above","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6b_applied.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":15,"oldLines":9,"newStart":15,"newLines":9,"lines":[" | framework--three-sport-groups-acyclic-cyclic-complex-for-programming.md | **Fix 6.** When NOT now says the authors call for subdivision (\"One should further subdivide the sports in each of these broad groups\") only within the acyclic and cyclic groups, before they introduce the third group (PDF 357 / p.346), and that \"extending it to the complex group is this entry's inference\". Verified in the text layer: the call sits between the cyclic bullet and \"Finally, one should identify a third group\". |"," | principle--mesocycle-not-microcycle-as-the-unit-and-planned-dips-are-not-errors.md | **Fix 5 + 4.** Why it works now reads: \"The authors' stated basis is 'modern sporting requirements' and 'common sense' (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347); they give no study. Inference: the effects they go on to describe ... unfold over weeks, so microcycle-to-microcycle comparison would misread them.\" The batch 6a sentence is deleted. In its place: the book calls the training session \"the fundamental unit\" in 6.2 (PDF 327 / p.316, confirmed in the text layer) and the mesocycle the fundamental unit in 6.8.1 (PDF 358); \"the first is a structural unit, the second a planning unit. Inference: the book does not reconcile the wording\". Link to [[framework--training-cycle-hierarchy-durations-and-russian-term-swap]] added there and in Related (review's reciprocal-link suggestion). |"," | claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation.md | **Fix 1 + 7, missing idea 5.** \"a thick, nearly straight rising line A\" is now \"a thick rising line A ... whose slope shrinks each year (steepest in Year 1, flattest in Year 3)\". \"a high-resolution crop\" is now \"a 400 dpi crop\". The \"Figure against text\" sentence is replaced with \"Graph A flattens year by year, in line with the text's 'parabola whose peak is eventually reached with time' (PDF p. 359 / printed p. 348); no peak is drawn within the three years.\" All three \"text layer only\" locators for PDF 359 now read \"checked against the page image\" (I opened the page). Missing idea 5 is folded into the endurance caveat: the problem lies \"in the ability to observe it in the factors used for analysis\", with the full \"measures of aerobic and anaerobic output ... do not express the internal changes associated with adaptation to intense cyclic or acyclic work\" quote, plus a labelled Inference on what that means for conditioning tests. |","-| claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md | **Fix 1 + 6 + 7.** The PDF 359 locator now reads \"checked against the page image\". Why it works now says research on adaptation (Folbrot, Selye) \"has to be taken much further in its application to the development of sporting mastery, especially in endurance sports\"; until then they assume \"the same, above-mentioned chief mechanisms ...\". When NOT: the \"Page 359 was read from the text layer only\" sentence and the \"graph A looks nearly straight\" clause are deleted, replaced with \"Graph A's slope shrinks each year, consistent with the parabola, but no peak is drawn within the three years (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\" |","+| claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md | **Fix 1 + 6 + 7.** The PDF 359 locator now reads \"checked against the page image\". Why it works now says research on adaptation (Folbrot, Selye) \"has to be taken much further in its application to the development of sporting mastery, especially in endurance sports\"; until then they assume \"the same, above-mentioned chief mechanisms ...\". When NOT: the \"Page 359 was read from the text layer only\" sentence and the \"graph A looks nearly straight\" clause are deleted, replaced with \"Graph A's slope shrinks each year, consistent with the parabola, but no peak is drawn within the three years (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).\" The Example's \"steadily rising fitness state A\" is now \"rising fitness state A\", to match. |"," | principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window.md | **GO row, minor items applied.** PDF 359 locator now \"checked against the page image\". The Bompa comparison now reads \"sets out three outcomes of loading (an increasing, varied load, adaptation; an unchanging load, plateau; an excessive one, maladaptation)\", checked against the vault `periodization--bompa/principle--progressive-overload-threshold` (\"a *standard* (unchanging) load ⇒ plateau\"). Related adds the review's reciprocal link [[principle--loading-volume-disturbs-homeostasis-and-prepares-connective-tissue]]. |","-| framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md | **Fix 2.** (1) The dip-versus-block sentence is replaced: Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18, which fits \"concentrated in the first half of the training phase\" (PDF 360); the figure marks 6-week intervals (dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12) and shows no 3-4 week blocks or 7-10 day pauses, so it does not illustrate the block durations; its x-axis ends at 18 weeks against the text's 18-22 (all checked on the PDF 360 and 361 images). (2) \"Issurin (2016) judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient ...\" (the vault Issurin brief, line 51, says the CU model is Verkhoshansky's). (3) \"Not for novices or medium-level athletes, by the authors' own scope\" is replaced with: devised for highly qualified athletes (PDF 360); in 6.9.5 \"both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification\" (PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360, text layer), \"so it is not required there\". I also changed the When to apply Inference, which said \"distributed loading for medium-level athletes\", to \"distributed loading when performance must hold; at medium qualification the authors say both forms work\". Reciprocal link [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]] added in When NOT and Related. `contested` stays, for the Issurin reason. |","+| framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md | **Fix 2.** (1) The dip-versus-block sentence is replaced: Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18, which fits \"concentrated in the first half of the training phase\" (PDF 360); the figure marks 6-week intervals (dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12) and shows no 3-4 week blocks or 7-10 day pauses, so it does not illustrate the block durations; its x-axis ends at 18 weeks against the text's 18-22 (all checked on the PDF 360 and 361 images). (2) \"Issurin (2016) judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient ...\" (the vault Issurin brief, line 51, says the CU model is Verkhoshansky's). (3) \"Not for novices or medium-level athletes, by the authors' own scope\" is replaced with: devised for highly qualified athletes (PDF 360); in 6.9.5 \"both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification\" (PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360, checked against the page image; the text layer misreads \"are\" as \"arc\"), \"so it is not required there\". I also changed the When to apply Inference, which said \"distributed loading for medium-level athletes\", to \"distributed loading when performance must hold; at medium qualification the authors say both forms work\". Reciprocal link [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]] added in When NOT and Related. `contested` stays, for the Issurin reason. |"," | heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery.md | **Fix 3.** The When NOT conflict paragraph is rewritten by quantity. **Concentrated block length (the conflict):** 3-4 weeks (PDF 360, given as the \"determined\" optimum), \"for instance, ... 4-8 week concentrated blocks\" (PDF 305, image checked) and \"a limited period (of up to 2 months)\" (PDF 381, image checked). **Different quantities:** 4-12 week unidirectional *stage* (PDF 305, 381); 4-12 week LDTE duration, \"approximately equal to the duration of the strength training stage\" (PDF 375 / p.364, image checked); 1-3 week latency (PDF 305); 18-22 week stage to exhaust the CAR (PDF 360); per-quality upper limits in [[claim--time-limits-of-single-emphasis-loading-by-motor-quality]]. The 9-12 week reconciliation is kept as Inference, now framed as linking block length to stage length. New Inference: both 5.3.3 and 6.12.2 define concentration by one month's volume (23-25%, PDF 305 and 381, quoted), so the month is the working unit and \"up to 2 months\" reads as a ceiling. The PDF 381 \"text layer only\" locator is gone. The \"Unclear arithmetic\" note is unchanged. Related adds the time-limits link. `contested` kept (see Disagreements 1). |"," | heuristic--restorative-stage-length-scales-with-homeostatic-disturbance.md | **GO row, minor item applied.** \"as quoted in the 5.3.3 entry above\" is now \"as quoted in [[heuristic--allow-restoration-after-a-concentrated-block-before-the-next]]\". |"," | claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect.md | **Fix 4 + 5.** The batch 6a sentence is replaced with \"The 6.2.2 intensity-zone material and Table 6.1 it points to are at PDF pp. 334-335 / printed pp. 323-324 (6.2.2 opens on PDF p. 334; Table 6.1 is on PDF p. 335)\" (checked in the text layer; see Disagreements 3). Why it works now opens \"Inference: Bondarchuk's study as reported does not test intensity at all ...\". |"]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":28,"oldLines":6,"newStart":28,"newLines":6,"lines":[" "," **When NOT to apply** — No data are given for the parabola or the genetic limit. Graph A's slope shrinks each year, consistent with the parabola, but no peak is drawn within the three years (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347). The \"imbalance\" definition is a theoretical position (Bauer, Bernstein), not a measured one. Inference: it is closer to the allostasis model in Evans's course material (regulation that changes set points to meet demand; see Related) than to fixed-set-point homeostasis."," ","-**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 6.22, where the functional indicators B and C wave above and below the steadily rising fitness state A within each year (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347).","+**Example** — The authors' own: Fig 6.22, where the functional indicators B and C wave above and below the rising fitness state A within each year (PDF p. 358 / printed p. 347)."," "," **Related** — [[claim--four-observations-on-long-term-speed-strength-adaptation]], [[principle--load-must-sit-within-the-current-adaptation-reserve-window]], [[claim--adaptive-reconstruction-vs-supercompensation]], [[claim--heterochronic-biochemical-adaptation-sequence]], [[claim--heterochronicity-of-strength-quality-and-muscle-group-development-order]], [[claim--speed-barrier-is-genetic-ceiling-not-neural-block]], [[elite-sports-performance--evans/framework--homeostasis-vs-allostasis-mcewen-sterling]]"]}]},{"filePath":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":29,"oldLines":7,"newStart":29,"newLines":7,"lines":[" "," **When to apply** — Inference: concentrated loading is the authors' choice for highly qualified athletes in preparatory mesocycles with time to absorb a dip of several weeks; distributed loading when performance must hold; at medium qualification the authors say both forms work (PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360). Eric's baseline: \"Off-season strength work prioritizes relative strength and technique first; already-strong athletes shift more time toward strength-speed/speed-strength work\" (`docs/method-baseline.md` line 105, Inferred (transcript + shared slides); the baseline adds \"Not claimed in the interview\"). Inference: the authors' split by qualification sits alongside that baseline split by strength level."," ","-**When NOT to apply** — **Contested across the library:** the authors say concentrated loading \"has been shown by research to be effective for all types of sport\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). Issurin (2016) judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, noting fitness gains without reported effects on team practice or competition (p. 6), and placed the model as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality (p. 11) ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18, which fits the text's \"concentrated in the first half of the training phase\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). The figure marks 6-week intervals (dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12) and shows no 3-4 week blocks or 7-10 day pauses, so it does not illustrate the block durations given on the same page; its x-axis ends at 18 weeks, while the text's period is 18-22 weeks (see [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]]). Inference: the \"deeper exhaustion, higher rebound\" claim sits on the same pages as the Bondarchuk result the authors read as \"calculations of volume are not as critical as those of intensity\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350); the authors do not reconcile the two (see [[claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect]]). The authors devised it for highly qualified athletes (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349); in 6.9.5 they say \"both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification\" (PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360), so it is not required there (see [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]]).","+**When NOT to apply** — **Contested across the library:** the authors say concentrated loading \"has been shown by research to be effective for all types of sport\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). Issurin (2016) judged the evidence for Verkhoshansky's concentrated-unidirectional model insufficient for broad use in high-performance combat, team-sport and swimming preparation, noting fitness gains without reported effects on team practice or competition (p. 6), and placed the model as better suited to sports dominated by a narrow target quality (p. 11) ([[research-papers/issurin-2016-benefits-and-limitations-of-block-periodized-training-approaches-to-athletes-preparation-a]]). Panel B's depression lasts until about weeks 7-9 of 18, which fits the text's \"concentrated in the first half of the training phase\" (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349). The figure marks 6-week intervals (dashed lines at weeks 6 and 12) and shows no 3-4 week blocks or 7-10 day pauses, so it does not illustrate the block durations given on the same page; its x-axis ends at 18 weeks, while the text's period is 18-22 weeks (see [[heuristic--concentrated-blocks-of-3-4-weeks-three-in-a-row-then-longer-recovery]]). Inference: the \"deeper exhaustion, higher rebound\" claim sits on the same pages as the Bondarchuk result the authors read as \"calculations of volume are not as critical as those of intensity\" (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350); the authors do not reconcile the two (see [[claim--bondarchuk-50-percent-more-volume-did-not-prolong-training-effect]]). The authors devised it for highly qualified athletes (PDF p. 360 / printed p. 349); in 6.9.5 they say \"both methods are successful in training athletes of medium qualification\" (PDF p. 371 / printed p. 360, checked against the page image), so it is not required there (see [[claim--concentrated-beats-distributed-loading-for-highly-qualified-athletes]])."," "," **Example** — The authors' own: Fig 6.23, where after concentrated loading starting strength ends at about 157% against about 144% with distributed loading over the same 18 weeks (PDF p. 361 / printed p. 350)."," "]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/fixes_supertraining_ch6b_applied.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/claim--compensatory-adaptation-is-temporary-and-precedes-stable-adaptation.md","/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint/staging/library/strength-and-conditioning/supertraining--verkhoshansky/framework--distributed-vs-concentrated-loading-two-forms-of-compensatory-adaptation.md"]}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5c69d3b6-d494-4c1b-9fe3-b0dd62903a99","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Flint","sessionId":"9d08ef48-9f28-44dc-845b-1ca05ec6873c","version":"2.1.288","gitBranch":"supertraining-ch4-3-4"}
